window.EPPP_TESTS["practice-exam-7"] = {"name": "Practice Exam 7", "slug": "practice-exam-7", "category": "Full Tests", "questionCount": 225, "questions": [{"question": "Attention-De\u0000cit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) involves a pattern of inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that has lasted for at least six months and had an onset before ___ years of age.", "options": {"A": "10", "B": "12", "C": "14", "D": "16"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "A diagnosis of ADHD requires an onset of symptoms before 12 years of age. Additionally, symptoms must be present for at least six months\nand must be apparent in at least two settings, with impairment in social, academic, or occupational functioning.\nAnswers A, C, and D: These responses are incorrect as the DSM-5 requires an onset of ADHD symptoms before age 12."}, {"question": "In contrast to most organic forms of amnesia, Dissociative Amnesia usually:", "options": {"A": "affects only memory for events that occurred before the trauma that precipitated the amnesia", "B": "affects memory for events that occurred both before and after the trauma that precipitated the amnesia", "C": "affects the ability to acquire new information from the time of the trauma that precipitated the amnesia to the present", "D": "affects memory for events that occurred at the time of the trauma that precipitated the amnesia and for a circumscribed period following the trauma"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Dissociative Amnesia is a type of functional (psychogenic) amnesia that is often associated with a traumatic event. This disorder involves an\ninability to recall important personal information, and gaps in memory are related to the traumatic event. Memory loss in Dissociative Amnesia\nis most often anterograde (i.e., it typically involves the trauma and, in some cases, other events that occurred during a circumscribed period\nfollowing the trauma).\nAnswer A: Retrograde amnesia (loss of memory for events that occurred before the trauma) is rare in Dissociative Amnesia; and, when it\noccurs, the loss of memories is often reversed through hypnosis.\nAnswer B: Dissociative amnesia most often involves anterograde amnesia, and retrograde amnesia is rare.\nAnswer C: In most cases of Dissociative Amnesia, the individual does not have trouble acquiring new information once the trauma that\nprecipitated the memory loss is over. In other words, the anterograde amnesia is usually related to the traumatic event and for events that\noccurred during a circumscribed period following the trauma."}, {"question": "PET scans of people who are diagnosed with _______________ show that these individuals often have increased activity levels in the orbitofrontal cortex, cingulate cortex, and caudate nucleus.", "options": {"A": "Posttraumatic Stress Disorder", "B": "Narcolepsy", "C": "Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder", "D": "Schizophrenia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "To answer this question, it is helpful to know that the caudate nucleus is part of the basal ganglia, that the basal ganglia are involved in\nvoluntary movement, and that the orbitofrontal cortex and cingulate cortex mediate emotional reactions. Activity in these areas of the brain is\nhigher in individuals who are diagnosed with OCD, especially when symptoms are active. In contrast, drug and behavioral treatments for OCD\nreduce activity in these areas.\nAnswers A, B, and D: Individuals who have been diagnosed with Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder have displayed increased activity levels in the\norbitofrontal cortex, cingulate cortex, and caudate nucleus."}, {"question": "Tom, a 35-year-old television repairman, becomes extremely upset when his boss and co-workers say something unfavorable about his work, and he is very uncomfortable whenever he feels others are watching him at work. He reports having only one close friend who he says he is very afraid of losing. Tom rarely leaves the house except to go to work, and he tells you that one of the reasons is because he is not a very good driver and he fears he will get into a car accident. Based on this information, the best diagnosis for Tom is:", "options": {"A": "Schizoid Personality Disorder", "B": "Schizotypal Personality Disorder", "C": "Avoidant Personality Disorder", "D": "Dependent Personality Disorder"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Tom's behavior is characterized by social discomfort and extreme sensitivity to negative evaluations. Based on the information given, the best\ndiagnosis is Avoidant Personality Disorder, which entails sensitivity to criticism, avoidance of social activities, embarrassment, and distress at\nthe inability to form close personal relationships.\nAnswer A: Although some of Tom's symptoms are also suggestive of Schizoid Personality Disorder, this is not the best answer since Tom\nseems to desire close relationships (i.e., he says he is worried about losing his best friend). Also, people with Schizoid Personality Disorder\nappear indifferent to criticism.\nAnswer B: Some of Tom's symptoms (i.e., interpersonal de\u0000cits and lack of close friends) are consistent with symptoms of Schizotypal\nPersonality Disorder. However, his sensitivity to criticism and avoidance behaviors are more suggestive of Avoidant Personality Disorder.\nAnswer D: Dependent Personality Disorder is characterized by a pattern of dependent and submissive behaviors."}, {"question": "In terms of long-term effects, the most successful treatment for a DSM-5 diagnosis of Enuresis is:", "options": {"A": "\u0000uid restriction and night lifting", "B": "the night alarm", "C": "a synthetic antidiuretic drug", "D": "an antidepressant drug"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "All the interventions listed in the responses are useful treatments for Enuresis, but only one has good long-term effects. The night alarm, which\nis the current version of the bell-and-pad, is successful in about 80% of cases, and of the treatments listed, it has the best long-term effects.\nAnswer A: While these interventions reduce symptoms initially, the bene\u0000ts are usually short-term.\nAnswer C: Although synthetic antidiuretics have good short-term effects, relapse rates are high once the individual discontinues the drug.\nAnswer D: Antidepressants are rarely used alone to treat Enuresis due to the high relapse rate associated with their use."}, {"question": "The most common precursor of Bulimia Nervosa during adolescence is:", "options": {"A": "academic failure", "B": "dieting", "C": "sexual abuse", "D": "peer rejection"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The onset of Bulimia typically occurs during adolescence or young adulthood and often follows an episode of strict dieting or exposure to\nmultiple stressful life events. Additional high-risk factors include depression, social anxiety, low self-concept, and poor identity formation.\nAnswer A: Although academic (and interpersonal) problems are associated with this disorder, they are less consistent predictors than dieting.\nAnswers C and D: Sexual abuse and peer rejection are not consistent predictive precursors of the diagnosis of Bulimia Nervosa."}, {"question": "In contrast to Korsakoff's Syndrome, Alzheimer's disease is associated with:", "options": {"A": "more severe anterograde amnesia", "B": "greater confabulation", "C": "less severe working memory impairment", "D": "a wider range of cognitive de\u0000cits"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Korsakoff's syndrome and Alzheimer's disease share several symptoms. Korsakoff's syndrome entails disproportionate memory de\u0000cits\nrelative to other types of cognitive impairment. In contrast, Alzheimer's disease involves memory impairment plus severe impairments in other\naspects of cognitive functioning.\nAnswer A: This response is imprecise and not the best choice. Moreover, an essential feature of Korsakoff's syndrome is severe anterograde\namnesia.\nAnswer B: Confabulation is an essential feature of Korsakoff's syndrome.\nAnswer C: In contrast, Alzheimer's disease is associated with more severe working memory de\u0000cits than Korsakoff's syndrome."}, {"question": "The onset of Bulimia often follows a period of moderate dieting, which has been linked to low blood levels of tryptophan. Low levels of tryptophan, in turn, are associated with:", "options": {"A": "lower-than-normal levels of brain serotonin", "B": "higher-than-normal levels of brain serotonin", "C": "lower-than-normal levels of brain dopamine", "D": "higher-than-normal levels of brain dopamine"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Even if you are unfamiliar with the link between tryptophan and serotonin, knowing that SSRIs have established e\u0000cacy for treating Bulimia\nmay have helped you choose the correct response. Tryptophan is a precursor of serotonin, and a rapid drop in plasma levels of tryptophan is\nknown to precede a decrease in brain levels of tryptophan and serotonin.\nAnswers B, C, and D: These are incorrect responses as low levels of tryptophan are associated with lower-than-normal levels of brain serotonin."}, {"question": "Which of the following is not an empirically supported treatment for primary insomnia?", "options": {"A": "Stimulus control therapy", "B": "Sleep restriction", "C": "Cognitive therapy", "D": "Habit reversal training"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Researchers have identi\u0000ed several non-pharmacological treatments as empirically validated interventions for primary insomnia. Habit reversal\ntraining is used to eliminate habits and other undesirable repetitive behaviors, including tics, trichotillomania, and stuttering. It is not an\nevidence-based treatment for insomnia.\nAnswer A: Stimulus control therapy is an empirically supported intervention for insomnia. It incorporates several strategies so that the\nindividual's bed and bedroom serve as cues for sleep.\nAnswer B: Sleep restriction is also an effective treatment for insomnia. When using this technique, the amount of time allotted for sleep is\ninitially restricted to create a \"sleep debt\" and the amount of time spent sleeping is then gradually lengthened.\nAnswer C: Cognitive therapy for insomnia involves identifying and replacing dysfunctional thoughts and attitudes that underlie the person's\nsleep problems."}, {"question": "Cataplexy is:", "options": {"A": "a movement disorder in which involuntary muscle contractions produce repetitive twisting movements", "B": "a sudden loss of muscle tone that can produce muscle weakness, loss of voluntary muscle control, or postural collapse", "C": "a condition involving postural rigidity and a lack of response to external stimuli", "D": "a loss of the ability to perform skilled, coordinated movements in the absence of motor or sensory de\u0000cits"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Cataplexy is associated with narcolepsy. Cataplexy is often precipitated by strong emotion and causes \u0000accid paralysis (loss of muscle tone).\nAnswer A: This describes dystonia.\nAnswer C: This describes catalepsy, which is a symptom of Schizophrenia and epilepsy and can be produced by hypnosis.\nAnswer D: This describes apraxia."}, {"question": "Kegel exercises are used to:", "options": {"A": "decrease performance anxiety", "B": "treat insomnia", "C": "establish a state of relaxation", "D": "increase muscle tone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Kegel exercises are used to restore muscle tone in the perineal area. Arnold Kegel developed the Kegel exercise to help women regain control\nover urination after childbirth. The exercise also helps increase sensitivity during intercourse.\nAnswers A, B, and C: These responses are incorrect as Kegel exercises are used to develop muscle tone."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a negative symptom of Schizophrenia?", "options": {"A": "Catatonic behavior", "B": "Derealization", "C": "Disorganized speech", "D": "Avolition"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Negative symptoms include restrictions in range and intensity of emotional expression (affective \u0000attening), restrictions in \u0000uency and\nproductivity of thought and speech (alogia), and restrictions in the initiation of goal-directed behavior (avolition).\nAnswer A: Catatonic behavior is not a negative symptom of Schizophrenia.\nAnswer B: Derealization is not a symptom of Schizophrenia.\nAnswer C: Disorganized speech is not classi\u0000ed in the DSM-5 as a negative symptom of Schizophrenia."}, {"question": "Marlatt and Gordon's (1985) relapse prevention model focuses on:", "options": {"A": "situations antecedent to relapse", "B": "the perceived consequences of relapse", "C": "controlled drinking", "D": "contingency management"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Marlatt and Gordon's approach to the de\u0000nition and treatment of addictions is classi\u0000ed as a social learning approach that combines\nbehavioral and cognitive principles. According to Marlatt and Gordon, relapse is a failure to maintain a behavior change after treatment and is\nbest avoided by identifying and dealing with its antecedents.\nAnswers B, C, and D: These responses are incorrect as Marlatt and Gordon's relapse prevention model concentrates on precursors to relapse."}, {"question": "People diagnosed with Narcolepsy often experience:", "options": {"A": "auditory hallucinations", "B": "hypnagogic hallucinations but not hypnopompic hallucinations", "C": "hypnopompic hallucinations but not hypnagogic hallucinations", "D": "hypnagogic or hypnopompic hallucinations"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Narcolepsy involves recurrent periods of an irresistible need to sleep. People diagnosed with this disorder often experience hypnagogic or\nhypnopompic hallucinations. Hypnagogic hallucinations occur while falling asleep, and hypnopompic hallucinations occur while waking up.\nAnswer A: Auditory hallucinations are uncommon in Narcolepsy.\nAnswers B and C: Both hypnagogic and hypnopompic hallucinations are common in individuals who are diagnosed with Narcolepsy."}, {"question": "Among older adults, major surgery, congestive heart failure, and pneumonia are all associated with an increased risk for which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Dyskinesia", "B": "Dementia", "C": "Delirium", "D": "Dystonia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The risk for delirium is affected by age, with children and older adults being at the highest risk. The conditions listed in the question are\nassociated with an increased risk for delirium for older adults. Note that one of the most common causes of delirium for members of this\npopulation is intoxication with prescription drugs.\nAnswers A, B, and C: Older adults with the abovementioned health conditions are not at an increased risk for these disorders."}, {"question": "The DSM-5 identi\u0000es all of the following subtypes of Speci\u0000c Learning Disorder except:", "options": {"A": "with impairment in reading", "B": "with impairment in written expression", "C": "with impairment in spelling", "D": "with impairment in mathematics"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The diagnosis of Speci\u0000c Learning Disorder involves di\u0000culties related to academic skills. While an individual with this diagnosis may have\ndi\u0000culty with spelling, this is not one of the DSM-5 subtypes for Speci\u0000c Learning Disorder. The three subtypes include: with impairment in\nreading, with impairment in written expression, and with impairment in mathematics.\nAnswers A, B, and D: These responses are incorrect as they are each DSM-5 subtypes of Speci\u0000c Learning Disorder."}, {"question": "The mean age of onset of motor tics in Tourette's Disorder is:", "options": {"A": "1 to 3 years", "B": "5 to 7 years", "C": "9 to 11 years", "D": "12 to 14 years"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "For individuals with Tourette's Disorder, the onset of motor tics ordinarily precedes the onset of vocal tics. The reported mean age of onset of\nmotor tics varies, but a commonly reported range is between 5 and 7 years. The onset of vocal tics is usually later, with a mean age of 11 years.\nAnswers A, C, and D: These responses are incorrect as the mean age of onset of motor tics in Tourette's Disorder is 5 to 7 years."}, {"question": "Approximately 80% of children diagnosed with ADHD continue to have symptoms of the disorder as adolescents. However, in adolescence, symptoms of ____________ typically decrease.", "options": {"A": "hyperactivity", "B": "inattention", "C": "hyperactivity and inattention", "D": "inattention and impulsivity"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Studies have demonstrated that most children diagnosed with ADHD continue to experience symptoms in adolescence but that the symptoms\nchange somewhat. Hyperactivity typically decreases in adolescence while impulsivity (especially verbal and emotional impulsivity) and\ninattention continue.\nAnswers B, C, and D: Symptoms of hyperactivity typically decrease in adolescence while inattention and impulsivity continue."}, {"question": "John is a 35-year-old graphic designer who is seeking help for his fear of public speaking. Due to a recent promotion, he is now required to make regular \"pitches\" to prospective clients. Previously, his work was mostly \"behind the scenes,\" and he is afraid that he will \"blow it\" when making presentations and look incompetent and silly to his co-workers and the clients. John is considering asking to be demoted so that he won't have to make presentations. He states that public speaking has been a problem for him since high school and that he feels like he is having a panic attack whenever he has to speak in front of a group. The most likely DSM-5 diagnosis for John is:", "options": {"A": "Social Anxiety Disorder", "B": "Panic Disorder", "C": "Speci\u0000c Phobia", "D": "Generalized Anxiety Disorder"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "John's symptoms meet the diagnostic criteria for Social Anxiety Disorder, which is diagnosed when the individual experiences marked fear of\nscrutiny by others in one or more social situations that is out of proportion to the actual threat posed by the situation. Since John's symptoms\noccur only when he speaks in public, the speci\u0000er \"performance only\" would be added to his diagnosis.\nAnswer B: Panic Disorder is characterized by sudden, unexpected panic attacks. John's panicky feelings arise in response to a speci\u0000c stressor\n(public speaking) and, therefore, are not unexpected.\nAnswer C: Speci\u0000c Phobia is characterized by marked fear or anxiety about a speci\u0000c object or situation, whereas the fear or anxiety\nassociated with Social Anxiety Disorder is related to a fear of scrutiny by others.\nAnswer D: Generalized Anxiety Disorder involves excessive anxiety and worry about multiple events or activities and the anxiety and worry are\nnot necessarily due to a fear of scrutiny by others. John's anxiety and worry are related to a single activity (public speaking) and to a fear of\nscrutiny by others, so Social Anxiety Disorder is the appropriate diagnosis."}, {"question": "Jacob, age 14, is being seen by a school clinician for repeated angry outbursts at school. He is persistently irritable, constantly argues with adults, has a history of behavioral referrals, and recently changed schools after being expelled for threatening a teacher. Jacob's parents report his symptom began prior to age 10, but have consistently gotten worse over the years. He is failing in school, and his parents are thinking of sending him to a wilderness camp where he can \"get some sense knocked into him.\" The most likely DSM-5 diagnosis for Jacob is which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Intermittent Explosive Disorder", "B": "Oppositional De\u0000ant Disorder", "C": "Bipolar II Disorder", "D": "Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder involves severe, recurrent temper outbursts with a persistently irritable or angry mood between\noutbursts on most days. Jacob's symptoms of persistent irritability and frequent outbursts are consistent with this diagnosis.\nAnswer A: A persistent irritable mood between temper outbursts is not characteristic of Intermittent Explosive Disorder.\nAnswer B: Oppositional De\u0000ant Disorder differs from Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder primarily in terms of the severity, frequency, and\nchronicity of the temper outbursts, with Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder having more severe, frequent, and chronic symptoms. When\nthe criteria for both disorders are met, a diagnosis of Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder is assigned.\nAnswer C: The essential feature of Bipolar II Disorder is hypomania (a distinct period of abnormally elevated, expansive, or irritable mood\naccompanied by increased activity or energy). Although people with Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder are irritable, they do not exhibit the\nincreased energy that is characteristic of Bipolar II Disorder. Also, the mood disturbance associated with Bipolar Disorder is episodic, whereas\nthe mood disturbance associated with Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder is persistent."}, {"question": "A 48-year-old woman is admitted to the emergency room of a hospital, complaining of chest pain and shortness of breath. While being interviewed, she is very restless and often gets up to pace back and forth. She says she has had similar \"attacks\" in the past but that this one is the worst. She also states that the attacks sometimes occur when she's concerned about getting bad news, but most of the time, they don't seem to be related to anything, and they make her feel like she's \"going to die.\" Assuming that there is no medical explanation for the woman's symptoms, the most likely DSM-5 diagnosis is which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Illness Anxiety Disorder", "B": "Speci\u0000c Phobia", "C": "Panic Disorder", "D": "Panic Disorder without Agoraphobia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "A DSM-5 diagnosis of Panic Disorder requires recurrent unexpected panic attacks, followed by one month of persistent concern about\nadditional attacks or their consequences.\nAnswer A: Illness Anxiety Disorder involves a preoccupation with having a serious illness, an absence of somatic symptoms or the presence of\nmild somatic symptoms, a high level of anxiety about one's health, and performance of excessive health-related behaviors or a maladaptive\navoidance of doctors, hospitals, etc.\nAnswer B: Speci\u0000c Phobia is characterized by an intense fear of or anxiety about a speci\u0000c object or situation, with the individual either\navoiding the object or situation or enduring it with marked distress.\nAnswer D: Panic Disorder without Agoraphobia is not a DSM-5 diagnosis."}, {"question": "A new client joins a clinician's grief group and tells everyone that his wife died and that he \"just can't get over it.\" He says they were married 25 years and were planning on traveling around the world when she became ill. The man complains of not sleeping, eating very little, and not wanting to spend time with friends. The clinician is more likely to assign a V code of Uncomplicated Bereavement to the man than a diagnosis of Major Depressive Disorder if:", "options": {"A": "the man's feeling of loss is accompanied by bouts of anxiety, a sense of emptiness, and intrusive thoughts of his wife and their relationship", "B": "the man's symptoms have dissipated in intensity over time and primarily occur when he thinks about his wife and their relationship", "C": "the man has had persistent symptoms for less than two months", "D": "the man's wife died less than six months ago and he considers his reaction to her death to be normal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Uncomplicated Bereavement is not a mental disorder, but it is included in the DSM-5 with Other Conditions That May Be a Focus of Clinical\nAttention. It involves a feeling of emptiness or loss that tends to decrease in intensity over days to weeks and is associated with thoughts or\nreminders of the deceased.\nAnswer A: This description is more consistent with a traumatic response and does not help distinguish between Uncomplicated Bereavement\nand Major Depressive Disorder.\nAnswer C: The DSM-5 does not specify a particular duration of symptoms as a requirement for a diagnosis of Uncomplicated Bereavement.\nAnswer D: Although the DSM-5 identi\u0000es \"viewing one's reaction to the death as normal\" as an indicator of Uncomplicated Bereavement, it does\nnot specify a particular interval of time since the death occurred as a requirement for its diagnosis."}, {"question": "During her \u0000rst session, Jane, age 43, tells the clinician that she needs to work on anxiety and relationship issues. She says she divorced her second husband 11 months ago and has been dating a man she met at church for a month, but she thinks he's going to \"dump\" her because of her physical problems. Jane has Type 2 diabetes, which is well-managed with medication. However, she complains of frequent stomach problems, severe headaches, and back pain, and she says she spends a lot of time researching diseases on the Internet to try to \u0000gure out what's wrong with her. Jane has been evaluated by a physician who told her that, other than diabetes, she does not have any medical problems. Which DSM-5 diagnosis should the clinician consider \u0000rst?", "options": {"A": "Somatic Symptom Disorder", "B": "Factitious Disorder", "C": "Illness Anxiety Disorder", "D": "Psychological Factors Affecting Other Medical Conditions"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Jane's symptoms are consistent with Somatic Symptom Disorder. The essential feature of this disorder is the presence of multiple somatic\nsymptoms that cause distress or disruption in one's daily life.\nAnswer B: Factitious Disorder involves falsi\u0000cation of physical or psychological symptoms or induction of an illness or injury that is associated\nwith an identi\u0000ed deception. None of the information in the question suggests that Jane is faking or causing her symptoms.\nAnswer C: Illness Anxiety Disorder is characterized by excessive worry about being or becoming ill and usually does not include somatic\nsymptoms or includes only symptoms of mild intensity.\nAnswer D: The diagnosis of Psychological Factors Affecting Other Medical Conditions is assigned when a person's psychological problems\naffect the course of a medical condition or interfere with its treatment. Jane's symptoms do not seem to be interfering with the management of\nher diabetes."}, {"question": "Which of the following is most likely to be an effective treatment for PTSD?", "options": {"A": "Cognitive processing therapy", "B": "Multisystemic therapy", "C": "Critical incident stress debrie\u0000ng", "D": "Mentalization-based therapy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Studies indicate that cognitive processing therapy (CPT) is an effective treatment for PTSD. This intervention integrates psychoeducation,\nexposure, and cognitive restructuring. Exposure involves writing and then reading a detailed narrative about the event, while cognitive\nrestructuring facilitates the identi\u0000cation and modi\u0000cation of maladaptive beliefs.\nAnswer B: Multisystemic Therapy (MST) is a comprehensive intervention that targets individual, family, school, peer group, and community\nfactors that are maintaining conduct problems. It is considered especially useful for adolescents who have exhibited the more serious\nsymptoms of Conduct Disorder and are at-risk for out-of-home placement.\nAnswer C: Critical incident stress debrie\u0000ng (CISD) is an intervention that involves providing treatment to victims of trauma within 72 hours of\nthe event regardless of whether they have exhibited symptoms, and it is usually administered during a single lengthy session. Research on its\neffects suggests that it may worsen symptoms of PTSD by interfering with the normal recovery process.\nAnswer D: Mentalization-based therapy (MBT) is a treatment for Borderline Personality Disorder."}, {"question": "The World Health Organization's International Pilot Study of Schizophrenia (IPSS) and Determinants of Outcome Study have compared patients with Schizophrenia from non-Western developing countries to those from Western industrialized countries. This research has found that patients from developing and industrialized countries tend to differ in terms of:", "options": {"A": "age and gender ratios", "B": "course and outcomes", "C": "symptom pro\u0000les", "D": "gender and prognosis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The IPSS found course and outcome differences in patients from developing and industrialized countries. Patients from developing countries\nwere much more likely to exhibit an acute onset of symptoms, a shorter clinical course, and a complete remission of symptoms.\nAnswers A, C, and D: Differences between patients from developing and industrialized countries in the IPSS were related to course and\noutcomes. Age, gender, and symptom pro\u0000le were not found to differ in consistent ways."}, {"question": "Strauss and Carpenter (1991) conducted longitudinal research, which demonstrated that for patients who are diagnosed with Schizophrenia, the best predictor of future employment is:", "options": {"A": "frequency of social contacts", "B": "symptom severity", "C": "employment history", "D": "duration of hospitalization"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "W. Strauss and J. Carpenter investigated this issue in their longitudinal study of individuals with Schizophrenia (The prediction of outcome in\nSchizophrenia IV: Eleven-year follow-up of the Washington IPSS cohort, The Journal of Nervous and Mental Disease, 179(9), 517-525, 1991). A\nkey \u0000nding of their study was that the various domains of functioning are relatively independent. Speci\u0000cally, previous employment is the best\npredictor of current employment, and past symptom severity is the best predictor of current symptom severity. These \u0000ndings are consistent\nwith the general theme that the best predictor of the future is the past.\nAnswers A, B, and D: This study indicated that the various domains of functioning for individuals diagnosed with Schizophrenia are\nindependent. Thus, the other listed domains did predict future employment as well as employment history."}, {"question": "Ella E. age 10, often says she doesn't want to go to school because she's afraid some of the children in her class will be \"mean\" to her. She seems overly concerned about her ability to perform well at school and in social situations. Moreover, she has trouble \u0000nishing her homework and other tasks because she keeps re-doing them since she wants them to be \"perfect.\" Ella frequently says she has a headache and has trouble falling asleep at night. Her mother tries to keep Ella from watching the news because she is unwilling to leave the house whenever she hears a story about a local murder or accident. Based on these symptoms, the best diagnosis for Ella is:", "options": {"A": "Separation Anxiety Disorder", "B": "Agoraphobia", "C": "Generalized Anxiety Disorder", "D": "Speci\u0000c Phobia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Ella is expressing excessive worry about multiple situations. Generalized Anxiety Disorder involves excessive worry or anxiety about several\nevents or activities that the person \u0000nds di\u0000cult to control. Children with this disorder are often perfectionistic, \u0000lled with self-doubt, and\ntypically exhibit physical signs of anxiety (e.g., headaches, stomachaches).\nAnswer A: A diagnosis of Separation Anxiety Disorder requires evidence of excessive anxiety concerning separation from those to whom the\nchild is attached. This evidence is not provided in the question description.\nAnswer B: The description does not indicate the essential features of Agoraphobia: that the child has a fear of being in places where escape\nmight be di\u0000cult or embarrassing, or where help might not be available if panic-like symptoms develop.\nAnswer D: Speci\u0000c Phobia is characterized by a marked and persistent fear of a speci\u0000c object or situation. Ella's fears are broader than this\nand, therefore, Generalized Anxiety Disorder is a better diagnosis."}, {"question": "Although no single structural abnormality of the brain explains most cases of schizophrenia, the most frequently observed abnormality in patients with this diagnosis is:", "options": {"A": "enlargement of the frontal lobes", "B": "enlargement of the ventricles", "C": "atrophy of the cerebellum", "D": "atrophy of the right hemisphere"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Researchers have linked several structural brain abnormalities to schizophrenia, although they have not found a single abnormality to underlie\nmost cases. Approximately 15 to 30% of patients diagnosed with schizophrenia exhibit enlargement of the lateral and third ventricles. Other\nabnormalities found in an even smaller percentage of patients include smaller-than-normal hippocampus and amygdala.\nAnswers A, C, and D: Enlargement of the ventricles is the most frequently found structural abnormality in people diagnosed with schizophrenia."}, {"question": "For a person with Bipolar Disorder, the risk for suicide is greatest:", "options": {"A": "when depressive symptoms are most severe", "B": "when depressive symptoms have become less severe", "C": "shortly after the onset of a depressive episode", "D": "shortly after the onset of a manic episode"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "There is some evidence that the risk for suicide is related to the severity of symptoms. According to some experts, the risk for suicide is\ngreatest when depressive symptoms begin to lift. This is because, at this time, suicidal ideation has not subsided, and the individual has the\nincreased energy and planning ability required to carry out a suicide plan.\nAnswer A: When depressive symptoms are most severe, the individual often lacks the energy and planning ability to carry out a suicide plan.\nAnswer C: The greatest risk for suicide is related to the severity of depressive symptoms, not the onset.\nAnswer D: Risk for suicide is related to the severity of depressive symptoms, not mania."}, {"question": "Recent research investigating treatments for Generalized Anxiety Disorder suggests that the most effective intervention is:", "options": {"A": "EMDR", "B": "systematic desensitization", "C": "stress inoculation training", "D": "cognitive-behavioral therapy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Several well-designed studies on interventions for symptoms of Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) con\u0000rm that the most effective treatment\nis cognitive-behavioral therapy (Kaczkurkin, A. N., & Foa, E. B. (2015). Cognitive-behavioral therapy for anxiety disorders: an update on the\nempirical evidence. Dialogues in clinical neuroscience, 17(3), 337346).\nAnswer A: EMDR is typically utilized to treat PTSD.\nAnswer B: Systematic desensitization is generally used for speci\u0000c phobias, and while it can be used to treat anxiety, there is more evidence to\nsupport the e\u0000cacy of cognitive-behavioral therapy for GAD.\nAnswer C: Stress inoculation training is a speci\u0000c type of cognitive-behavioral therapy that is most often used to treat PTSD."}, {"question": "Social change is a primary goal of which of the following interventions?", "options": {"A": "Advocacy consultation", "B": "Program-centered administrative consultation", "C": "Organizational consultation", "D": "Social justice consultation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Advocacy consultation, unlike other consultation modes, focuses on social systems rather than individuals or small groups. A distinctive\ncharacteristic of advocacy consultation is that it is based on an explicit value orientation that targets social change in the direction of power\nequalization.\nAnswers B & C: While these forms of consultation could potentially contribute to social change; advocacy consultation is the only consultation\nmodel that explicitly focuses on fostering social change.\nAnswer D: This is a \"made-up\" term."}, {"question": "When working with older adults, it is important to remember that:", "options": {"A": "psychotherapy, especially insight-oriented therapy, is generally ineffective", "B": "there is greater variability among older people than younger people on a range of characteristics", "C": "a therapeutic alliance is particularly di\u0000cult to establish, especially when the therapist is young", "D": "CBT and brief psychodynamic therapies work best with this population"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "When working with older adults, it is important to remember that there is greater variability among older people than younger people on a range\nof characteristics (physical health, cognitive skills, income, etc.).\nAnswer A: It has generally been con\u0000rmed that older adults respond well to a variety of forms of psychotherapy.\nAnswer C: While clients may have many preferences when choosing a therapist, research does not support the notion that it is more di\u0000cult to\nestablish rapport with older clients.\nAnswer D: Cognitive, behavioral, and brief psychodynamic therapies have been shown to be \"probably e\u0000cacious\" as treatments for depression\namong older adults."}, {"question": "A couple comes to therapy complaining that their conversations always end up as arguments. In talking with the couple, you realize that a common pattern is for one partner to make an offensive comment to the other and for the other partner to respond with an even more offensive remark. This pattern of communication is referred to as:", "options": {"A": "symmetrical", "B": "complementary", "C": "mysti\u0000cation", "D": "pseudohostility"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "In this situation, the husband and wife are mirroring each other's behavior in what is sometimes referred to as a \"one-upmanship\" game.\nSymmetrical communication is characterized by equality between the partners. It can lead to con\u0000ict and competition (symmetrical escalation)\nwhen each partner tries to \"one-up\" the other.\nAnswer B: Complementary interactions are based on inequality with one partner assuming a dominant role and the other a subordinate\nposition.\nAnswer C: Mysti\u0000cation refers to masking what is really going on through denial in order to maintain the status quo.\nAnswer D: Pseudohostility occurs when real con\u0000icts are denied and obscured by super\u0000cial bickering."}, {"question": "A practitioner of nonsexist therapy:", "options": {"A": "interprets a client's behavior in terms of social, political, and cultural forces", "B": "stresses the egalitarian nature of the therapeutic relationship", "C": "views gender as a key determinant of behavior", "D": "uses unbiased techniques designed to promote personal growth"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Nonsexist therapy and feminist therapy share several characteristics. For the exam, you want to be familiar with their major similarities and\ndifferences. Of the answers given, this is the best one. As its name implies, nonsexist therapy involves using nonsexist (unbiased) techniques.\nIn addition, its focus is on personal growth.\nAnswer A: This is characteristic of feminist therapy but not nonsexist therapy.\nAnswer B: A nonsexist therapist may or may not emphasize an egalitarian relationship depending on his/her theoretical orientation. Therefore,\nthis is not the best answer.\nAnswer C: For nonsexist therapists, gender is only one of many personal characteristics that affect behavior and are relevant to counseling."}, {"question": "According to Margaret Mahler's object relations theory, the development of a sense of self is the result of a separation- individuation process that begins at about _____ months of age.", "options": {"A": "2 to 3", "B": "4 to 5", "C": "8 to 10", "D": "12 to 14"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Mahler distinguishes between two phases that contribute to the development of a unique sense of self: symbiosis and separation-\nindividuation. Separation-individuation is triggered by the child's ability to separate from his/her primary caregiver. The process begins at about\n4 to 5 months of age when a child who is being held by his/her caregiver is able to lean away to scan the environment.\nAnswer A: An infant is in the symbiosis phase at this age.\nAnswer C: This age is very important for the separation-individuation process because the child is now able to crawl away from his/her\ncaregiver. However, the process begins prior to 8 to 10 months of age.\nAnswer D: Separation-individuation begins at 4 to 5 months."}, {"question": "According to Prochaska and DiClemente's (1992) stages of change (transtheoretical) model, a person in the __________ stage is planning to take action within the next 30 days that will alter his/her problematic behavior.", "options": {"A": "action", "B": "contemplation", "C": "preparation", "D": "goal attainment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Prochaska and DiClemente's stages of change model distinguishes between six stages of change: precontemplation, contemplation,\npreparation, action, maintenance, and termination. A person in the preparation stage is planning to take action in the near future (usually\nde\u0000ned as in the next month).\nAnswer A: A person in the action stage is currently taking action to alter his/her behavior.\nAnswer B: A person in the contemplation stage intends to take action within the next six months.\nAnswer D: Goal attainment is not one of the stages identi\u0000ed by Prochaska and DiClemente."}, {"question": "According to LaFromboise et al., an effective intervention with American Indian clients might involve coordination with traditional healers (if appropriate), incorporation of members of the extended family, and:", "options": {"A": "an insight-oriented approach", "B": "reality therapy or a solution-focused approach", "C": "the use of network therapy", "D": "a combination of Adlerian and rational-emotive approaches"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "LaFromboise et al. (1990) recommend the use of network therapy, which incorporates family and community members into\nthe treatment process and situates an individual’s psychological problems within the context of his/her family, workplace, community,\nand other social systems.\nAnswers A, B, & D: LaFromboise et al. did not speci\u0000cally recommend the use of these three interventions with American Indian clients."}, {"question": "The most common informal caregivers for older adults with chronic health conditions are family members. More speci\u0000cally, the most common primary caregiver is:", "options": {"A": "the spouse or adult daughter", "B": "the adult daughter or son", "C": "a same-gender sibling", "D": "the eldest child"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The most common informal caregiver depends on the availability of family members and the gender of the individual requiring caregiving.\nHowever, some generalizations can be drawn from research. Past research has found that the spouse is the most common primary caregiver,\nwith the children adopting a secondary role. However, when there is no spouse, a daughter is most likely to assume the role of primary\ncaregiver. See, e.g., S. H. Zarit and D. J. Eggebeen, Parent-child relationships in adulthood and later years, in M. H. Bornstein (Ed.), Handbook of\nparenting: Children and parenting (Vol. 1, pp. 135-164), Hillsdale, NJ, Lawrence Erlbaum, 2002.\nAnswer B: If married, the spouse is most likely to be the primary caregiver with the adult daughter being the next most likely individual to ful\u0000ll\nthis role.\nAnswer C: Research does not support this answer.\nAnswer D: If unmarried, a female child rather than the eldest child is most likely to take a primary caregiving role."}, {"question": "Freud and the object relations theorists share in common an emphasis on the role of _________________ in personality development.", "options": {"A": "the superego", "B": "innate sexual and aggressive drives", "C": "early childhood experiences", "D": "separation and individuation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Object relations theory shares a number of assumptions in common with Freudian theory but differs in its emphasis on the need for\ninterpersonal relationships (versus needs related to sex and aggression). Object relations theorists, like Freud, believe that early childhood\nexperiences are critical to personality development. They also posit a stage theory of development, but they view interpersonal relations as the\nprimary contributor to personality.\nAnswer A: This is true about Freudian theory but not about object relations theory.\nAnswer B: This characterizes Freudian theory.\nAnswer D: This applies only to object relations theory."}, {"question": "As de\u0000ned by Carl Jung, individuation refers to:", "options": {"A": "being able to separate one's intellectual and emotional functioning", "B": "differentiating between self and object", "C": "experiencing a state of anonymity", "D": "integrating parts of the self to create a unique identity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to be familiar with all of the terms that are referred to in the four answers to this question. As de\u0000ned by Jung,\nindividuation is the goal of psychological development and involves integrating the conscious and the unconscious elements of the psyche.\nAnswer A: This answer describes Bowen's concept of differentiation. According to Bowen, the greater a person's differentiation, the better able\nhe/she is to resist becoming emotionally fused with other family members.\nAnswer B: This answer refers to Mahler's separation-individuation phase of early development.\nAnswer C: This describes deindividuation which is used to explain why a person may be more willing to act aggressively or in other\nuncharacteristic ways when acting as a member of a group than when acting alone."}, {"question": "As described by Murray Bowen, \"emotional triangles\" are most closely associated with:", "options": {"A": "stress and con\u0000ict", "B": "repetitive communication patterns", "C": "irresponsibility", "D": "self-deception"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Emotional triangles are a central part of Bowen's theory of family pathology. According to Bowen, emotional triangles help reduce con\u0000ict and\nstress. For example, if there is a great deal of con\u0000ict between a mom and dad, they can triangulate a child into the relationship and reduce the\ncon\u0000ict between them by focusing their attention on the child.\nAnswers B, C, and D: While these dynamics may occur in an emotionally triangulated family, emotional triangles are most closely associated\nwith stress and con\u0000ict as this is often the initiate."}, {"question": "For practitioners of Minuchin's structural family therapy, a symptom is:", "options": {"A": "both the result and cause of dysfunctional communication patterns", "B": "the result of a family projection process", "C": "a re\u0000ection of an \"impasse\"", "D": "a maladaptive reaction to stress"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "A primary goal of structural family therapy is to restructure the family so that it is better able to adapt to maturational and situational stressors.\nFor structural family therapists, developmental and environmental changes produce stress which a family can respond to in either a healthy or\nunhealthy way.\nAnswer A: Haley's strategic family therapy focuses on dysfunctional communication patterns.\nAnswer B: The family projection process is a focus of Bowen's extended family systems therapy.\nAnswer C: An impasse is not a focus of structural family therapy."}, {"question": "The primary difference between feminist and nonsexist therapy is that, in contrast to nonsexist therapy, feminist therapy:", "options": {"A": "promotes an egalitarian therapist-client relationship", "B": "emphasizes political values and social change", "C": "rejects sex-role stereotypes", "D": "recognizes the impact of sexism on mental health"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Feminist therapy and non-sexist therapy share a number of characteristics but differ in terms of their emphasis on socio-political contributors\nto mental health problems. Feminist therapy is the more political of the two types of therapy and is based on the assumption that the \"personal\nis political\".\nAnswer A: An egalitarian relationship is characteristic of both types of therapy.\nAnswer C: Both types of therapy reject traditional sex-role stereotypes.\nAnswer D: Recognition of the impact of sexism on mental health is also characteristic of both types of therapy."}, {"question": "A family therapist would use the technique known as \"prescribing the symptom\" in order to:", "options": {"A": "alter the family's resistance to change", "B": "reduce tension between family members", "C": "change the meaning of a situation by changing the way it is perceived", "D": "educate family members about the bene\u0000ts of change"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Prescribing the symptom is a paradoxical technique that involves instructing family members to engage in the problematic behavior, often in an\nexaggerated form. For the exam, you want to be familiar with all of the paradoxical techniques. The purpose of prescribing the symptom is to\nalter the family's resistance to changing a behavior by requesting that family members engage in the behavior. Ideally, this will cause them to\nrebel and engage, instead, in a more desirable behavior.\nAnswer B: This is not an accurate description of the purpose of prescribing the symptom.\nAnswer C: This is a better description of the technique known as relabeling.\nAnswer D: This is not an accurate description of the purpose of prescribing the symptom."}, {"question": "A psychology intern responds with suspicion and hostility to his supervisor, which is the same way that a new therapy client is responding to him (the intern) in therapy. This illustrates which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Triangulation", "B": "The therapeutic double-bind", "C": "Diagnostic overshadowing", "D": "Parallel process"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "In this situation, the intern is recreating the intern-client relationship in the intern-supervisor relationship. The situation described in this\nquestion illustrates parallel process, which involves a combination of transference, countertransference, and projection in the client-\nsupervisee/supervisee-supervisor relationship.\nAnswer A: Triangulation has several de\u0000nitions, depending on the context, but is not relevant to the situation described in this question. As\nde\u0000ned by Minuchin, triangulation occurs when each parent demands that the child side with him/her against the other parent.\nAnswer B: The therapeutic double bind refers to paradoxical techniques that are used by therapists to elicit a client's resistance and thereby\nfoster the desired change.\nAnswer C: Diagnostic overshadowing is the tendency to attribute all of a client's behavioral, social, and emotional problems to one salient\ncharacteristic (e.g., race or diagnosis) rather than considering alternative explanations."}, {"question": "Client-centered therapists are most likely to interpret a client's heart palpitations, hyperventilation, tension headaches, and nausea as:", "options": {"A": "the result of unresolved intrapsychic con\u0000icts", "B": "an emergence of denied threats to the self-concept", "C": "a manifestation of the client's avoidance of existential anxiety", "D": "the consequence of being unable to ful\u0000ll one's own needs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "For practitioners of client-centered therapy, personality and behavioral problems arise when an individual's natural tendency toward growth and\nactualization is disrupted by incongruence between the self and experience. Incongruence results when the evaluations made by others are\ninconsistent with one's self-concept. Rogers posited that this incongruence often produces unpleasant visceral sensations that are subjectively\nexperienced as anxiety and serve as a signal that the uni\u0000ed self is being threatened.\nAnswer A: This is more congruent with psychoanalytic theory.\nAnswer C: This is congruent with existential theory.\nAnswer D: This is more congruent with reality therapy."}, {"question": "Freud and Skinner share a(n) _______ view of behavior.", "options": {"A": "intuitive", "B": "holistic", "C": "deterministic", "D": "phenomenological"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Freud looked to unconscious con\u0000icts and relied on internal structures and activities (such as the id, ego, and defense mechanisms) to explain\nbehavior. In contrast, Skinner viewed the mind as an irrelevant construct and proposed that behavior is environmentally controlled. Although\nthey had different explanations for the causes of behavior, Freud and Skinner both believed in determinism. Skinner believed that all behavior is\nenvironmentally controlled, while Freud believed in psychic determinism (i.e., that unconscious mental content is the primary cause of\nbehavior).\nAnswer A: Intuitive refers to knowing or understanding without the use of rational processes and is not a characteristic shared by Freud and\nSkinner.\nAnswer B: A holistic point of view emphasizes the importance of the whole and the interdependence of the parts of the whole and is not\napplicable to the theories of Freud and Skinner.\nAnswer D: A phenomenological view is characteristic of humanistic theories and therapeutic approaches."}, {"question": "Freud introduced the concept of \"defense\" in his description of hysteria and attributed hysteria to which of the following defense mechanisms?", "options": {"A": "Projection", "B": "Reaction formation", "C": "Sublimation", "D": "Repression"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Freud's initial explanation for hysteria was that it represents an attempt to eliminate memories related to early psychic trauma from\nconsciousness. Repression is considered the most basic defense mechanism because it underlies all others. It was the initial defense\nmechanism identi\u0000ed by Freud, and he used it to explain the etiology of hysteria.\nAnswer A: Freud did not attribute hysteria to projection.\nAnswer B: Freud did not attribute reaction formation to hysteria.\nAnswer D: Freud did not attribute sublimation to hysteria."}, {"question": "Polly Pathos is seeing Dr. Genial for her depressive symptoms. As a practitioner of Interpersonal Therapy (IPT), Dr. Genial will most likely:", "options": {"A": "help Polly gain insight into how interpersonal experiences during childhood are contributing to Polly's problems in the present", "B": "focus on clarifying how Polly's current relationships and her expectations about them are contributing to her symptoms", "C": "have Polly participate in a family therapy group that targets the dysfunctional interaction patterns that contribute to symptoms", "D": "help Polly identify and understand how interpersonal \"schemas\" are inhibiting her ability to actualize her full potential"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Interpersonal Therapy is a brief form of therapy that was originally developed as a treatment for depression but has since been applied to other\ndisorders. The focus of interpersonal therapy is on the link between current interpersonal problems and depressive symptoms. Interpersonal\ntherapy targets one or more of the following areas: grief, interpersonal role disputes, role transitions, interpersonal de\u0000cits.\nAnswer A: This sounds more psychodynamic.\nAnswer C: This intervention would likely be utilized by a practitioner of communication/ interaction family therapy.\nAnswer D: This is not an intervention likely to be used by a practitioner of IPT."}, {"question": "In family therapy, the husband and wife complain that they seem to hardly have anything in common anymore. The therapist responds by saying, \"I agree. It truly seems that you two have absolutely nothing in common whatsoever.\" The therapist's response is an example of which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Reactance", "B": "Restraining", "C": "Positioning", "D": "Prescription"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The therapist is using a paradoxical technique referred to as positioning. Positioning involves exaggerating the severity of the symptom.\nAnswer A: Reactance is not a paradoxical technique but refers to the tendency of people to do the opposite of what they are asked to do,\nespecially when they feel their personal freedom is being threatened.\nAnswer B: This technique (telling a client not to change) is referred to as restraining.\nAnswer D: Prescription involves instructing the client to deliberately engage in the target behavior, usually in an exaggerated form."}, {"question": "According to multicultural research, which of the following approaches would most likely be LEAST useful when a therapist is working with an Asian or Asian American client?", "options": {"A": "Identifying therapy goals during the initial session", "B": "Establishing an egalitarian relationship with the client early in therapy", "C": "Making use of \"relationship questions\" that elicit information about the client's interactions with others", "D": "Asking the client to try to identify exceptions to the presenting problem"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "It is dangerous to make generalizations about the best treatment approach for any group of clients, but the licensing exam may include a\nquestion that requires you to do so. An egalitarian relationship is usually contraindicated because of \"the Asian norms of deference to authority\nand modesty in the presence of superiors\" (H. Kitano and M. Maki, Continuity, change, and diversity, in P. B. Pedersen, et al., Counseling across\ncultures, Thousand Oaks, Sage Publ., 1995).\nAnswer A: A direct approach that focuses on speci\u0000c goals is generally considered useful for Asian American clients.\nAnswer C: Focusing on relationships is a good approach.\nAnswer D: Identifying strengths is also useful."}, {"question": "Research comparing the use of mental health services by heterosexual adults and gay/lesbian adults has generally found that:", "options": {"A": "gay men are more likely than heterosexual men to use mental health services, but lesbian women are less likely than heterosexual women to do so", "B": "lesbian women are more likely than heterosexual women to use mental health services, but gay men are less likely than heterosexual men to do so", "C": "gay men and lesbian women are more likely than their heterosexual counterparts to use mental health services", "D": "gay men and lesbian women are less likely than their heterosexual counterparts to use mental health services"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "A frequently cited investigation of mental health service use by gay and lesbian adults was conducted by Cochran, Sullivan, and Mays\n[Presence of mental disorders, psychological distress, and mental health service use among lesbian, gay, and bisexual adults in the United\nStates, Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology, 71(1), 53-64, 2003]. The results of this study (which have been subsequently replicated\nby several other researchers) indicated that gay and lesbian adults have higher levels of perceived need for mental health services and are\nmore likely to use mental health services.\nAnswers A and B: Neither of these statements is true. Both gay and lesbian adults are more likely than their heterosexual peers to seek mental\nhealth services according to the study noted above.\nAnswer D: The opposite is true."}, {"question": "A Jungian \"archetype\" is best described as:", "options": {"A": "an organized constellation of feelings, thoughts, and perceptions", "B": "a structural component of the collective unconscious", "C": "the \"mask\" adopted by a person in response to social demands", "D": "a signi\u0000cant event that occurred during childhood"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "According to Carl Jung, the psyche consists of a number of different, but interrelated, elements. For Jung, the major division in the psyche is\nbetween the personal unconscious and the collective (transpersonal) unconscious, with each containing unique elements. An archetype is a\nuniversal thought-form that is deposited in the mind when an experience has been constantly repeated for many generations. Numerous\narchetypes reside in the collective unconscious, including the archetypes of birth, death, the earth mother, and the wise old man.\nAnswer A: This better describes Jung's notion of \"complexes\" (e.g., mother complex), which exist in the personal unconscious.\nAnswer C: This best describes the \"persona,\" which is acquired in response to social demands as well as archetypal needs.\nAnswer D: This is not an accurate description of archetypes."}, {"question": "Among the groups listed below, admission rates for psychiatric hospitalization are highest for which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Never married men", "B": "Married men", "C": "Divorced men", "D": "Widowers"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Of the choices given, never married men have the highest rate of psychiatric hospitalization for both men and women. Other factors to consider\nfor admission rates include age (with the highest proportion between age 25 to 44) and race (with whites representing the largest number of\npsychiatric inpatients).\nAnswers B, C, and D: Research suggests that individuals who have never been married are more likely than those who are married, divorced,\nand widowed to be admitted to a psychiatric facility."}, {"question": "Which researcher developed a general stage theory of lesbian identity development that consists of four stages?", "options": {"A": "Richard Troiden", "B": "Joan Sophie", "C": "Janet Helms", "D": "David Sue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Joan Sophie developed a general stage theory of lesbian identity development that consists of four stages: \u0000rst awareness, testing and\nexploration, identity acceptance, and identity integration.\nAnswer A: Troiden is known for his Homosexual Identity Development Model which also consisted of four stages.\nAnswer C: Janet Helms is known for her White Racial Identity Development Model.\nAnswer D: David Sue is well known for his contributions to multicultural theory and the Racial/Cultural Identity Development Model."}, {"question": "At the end of the 16th session, your client expresses a desire to terminate citing a signi\u0000cant reduction in initial symptoms. Research conducted by Howard and colleagues (1996) would suggest that:", "options": {"A": "this is simply a transitory placebo effect", "B": "the client has completed the rehabilitation phase of treatment", "C": "this re\u0000ects a temporary boost in hopefulness expected during initial treatment", "D": "the client is likely experiencing true symptomatic relief"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This is a somewhat di\u0000cult question given the limited context provided. However, knowledge of Howard and colleague's three phases of\ntherapy would help you select the correct response. According to Howard and colleagues, clients often experience real symptom relief during\nthe second phase of therapy (remediation). Howard et al. (1996) hold that this symptom relief typically occurs around session 16.\nAnswer A: Although this is a possibility, as a placebo effect could occur at any time in therapy, response d is a better answer given that the\nquestion speci\u0000cally references Howard et al.'s research.\nAnswer B: The third stage of therapy (rehabilitation), does not begin until after the 16th therapy session. During this phase, the client unlearns\nmaladaptive patterns of being and establishes new healthy behaviors.\nAnswer C: This is not the best answer as the client has completed 16 sessions. According to Howard and his colleagues, during the \u0000rst few\nsessions of therapy, a client experiences an increase in hopefulness. This stage is referred to as remoralization."}, {"question": "Dr. Cruz is conducting a survey to obtain the information needed to compare therapists who have and have not had a sexual relationship with a client. The results of his survey will most likely indicate that therapists who say they have been sexually involved with a client __________________________.", "options": {"A": "are also more likely to have been sexually involved with their own supervisor", "B": "are also more likely to have had non-sexual dual relationships with their clients", "C": "are also more likely to make use of therapeutic touch in therapy", "D": "are not distinguishable from those who have not been sexually involved with a client in terms of sex with their own supervisor, non- sexual dual relationships, or the use of therapeutic touch"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The characteristics of therapists who have and have not had sexual relationships with their clients were assessed by a survey conducted by D.\nH. Lamb and S. J. Catanzaro (Sexual and nonsexual boundary violations involving psychologists, clients, supervisees, and students:\nImplications for professional practice, \"Professional Psychology: Research and Practice,\" 29 (5), 498-503, 1998). Psychologists reporting sexual\nrelationships with their clients also reported more nonsexual dual relationships.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Lamb and Catanzaro found that psychologists who had sex with their clients were no more likely to have\nhad sex with their supervisors than psychologists who did not have sex with their clients.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. The issue of therapeutic touch was not examined in the survey, and there is no evidence to support this\nanswer.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. The Lamb and Catanzaro study found an association between psychologists who had sex with their clients\nand psychologists who reported more nonsexual dual relationships."}, {"question": "You are a psychodynamic psychotherapist and are seeing a client who has several problems. One of these problems has been found to respond best to a behavioral technique that you are unfamiliar with. You should ______________________.", "options": {"A": "seek supervision while applying the technique", "B": "take a class on the technique", "C": "make a referral to a behavioral therapist", "D": "discuss the issue with the client and decide what she wants to do"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Given the limited information, this is the best answer. Keep in mind that you always want to be somewhat conservative when answering ethics\nquestions on the exam. Since you do not have the training or skills needed to provide the optimal treatment to the client, referral would be the\nbest course of action. Of course, you can continue treating the client for her other problems.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. This might be adequate in some situations. The question states, however, that you are a \"psychodynamic\npsychotherapist,\" so you may not be interested in adding a behavioral technique to your repertoire. This may also be inadequate if the problem\nis a very serious one.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. In most situations, simply completing a class would not be adequate preparation.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. This would not be a good course of action, as the client is not in a position to make an informed decision."}, {"question": "Dr. Street, a clinical psychologist, has been treating Bob B. for about \u0000ve months. Bob originally came to therapy to resolve the depression he had been experiencing since his divorce and, in the past few weeks, Dr. Street has noticed a marked improvement in Bob's symptoms. During one session, Bob says that, for some time, he has been having trouble going anywhere that requires him to use an elevator since being con\u0000ned in small spaces with other people makes him very anxious. He says he has had this problem for some time, but that it seems to have gotten worse recently. Dr. Street has not had much experience in treating phobic reactions and therefore decides to refer Bob to a behavioral therapist. However, even though Bob has started behavioral therapy with another therapist, Dr. Street continues to see Bob in order to continue working on Bob's depression. In terms of ethical practices, this arrangement is _________________________.", "options": {"A": "unethical since Dr. Street should have offered Bob a choice of therapists", "B": "unethical since Dr. Street should terminate his relationship with Bob while Bob is receiving treatment from another therapist", "C": "ethical as long as the therapists cooperate with one another during the course of Bob's treatment", "D": "ethical but therapeutically questionable since Bob's phobia is probably related to his divorce"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Dr. Street's actions are consistent with the provisions of the Ethics Code. He has recognized his professional limitations and has taken action\nto ensure that his client receives the best possible treatment. As noted above, Dr. Street has acted in an ethical and responsible manner by\nreferring Bob to another therapist and will continue to do so as long as he cooperates with the other therapist during the course of Bob's\ntreatment.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Referral to a speci\u0000c professional is not prohibited although it would be advisable, when appropriate, to\ngive the client a choice of alternatives. This, however, is not the best answer of those given.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Seeing a client who is seeing another professional is only a problem when the client is receiving similar\nservices from the other professional, which is not the case in this situation.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. There is insu\u0000cient information to support this conclusion."}, {"question": "Dr. Greene, a psychologist in private practice, sets all his clients' fees according to their ability to pay as determined by their yearly income. As a result, some of his clients are paying a very low fee or no fee, while others are paying a fee that is considerably above- average for the location of his practice. Dr. Greene's fee policy is _______________.", "options": {"A": "illegal in most states and clearly unethical", "B": "legal in most states but unethical", "C": "both legal and ethical", "D": "ethical but illegal in most states"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Sliding scale fees are legally and ethically acceptable as long as the fee arrangement is made clear from the onset of therapy and practices do\nnot violate the psychologist's contracts with insurance companies, including Medicaid and Medicare. In this case, Dr. Greene uses a sliding\nscale for all patients and thus is unlikely to be billing patient insurance.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Sliding scale fees are not unethical and illegal.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Sliding scale fees are not unethical.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. Sliding scale fees are not illegal."}, {"question": "Dr. Raj provided treatment to a client in State X where Dr. Raj resides and works. Treatment ended six months ago and the client has since moved to State Y. Dr. Raj receives a subpoena from the client's attorney who also resides and works in State Y. The subpoena requires Dr. Raj to provide testimony at a trial involving the former client. Dr. Raj _______________________.", "options": {"A": "is not required to appear since the subpoena is from another state", "B": "must appear and testify as requested as long as the client has signed a release of information", "C": "must appear as long as the subpoena was issued by the court", "D": "should contact an attorney to determine if he must appear as requested"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This is really a question of law and depends on applicable laws regarding jurisdiction. The best thing to do would be to consult a lawyer to\ndetermine how to respond to the subpoena. In his book on legal matters, R. Woody concludes that the appropriate course of action in this type\nof situation would be to consult an attorney to determine the legal authority of the subpoena (Legally safe mental health practice: Psycholegal\nquestions and answers, Madison, CT, Psychosocial Press, 1997).\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. It is unwise to ignore a subpoena. Seeking legal advice is the best approach.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect because the subpoena has been issued in another state. The best choice is to consult an attorney for\nguidance.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. One would not want to ignore a subpoena, but an attorney's advice can guide the psychologist's decision in\nthis case."}, {"question": "You receive an order from the court to conduct a presentencing evaluation of a defendant who has been convicted of murder. You should ______________________.", "options": {"A": "obtain a signed release of information from the defendant before conducting the evaluation", "B": "discuss the limits of con\u0000dentiality with the defendant even though a release of information is not legally required in this situation", "C": "say nothing about con\u0000dentiality since a release of information is not legally required and discussing con\u0000dentiality may decrease the defendant's willingness to be truthful", "D": "proceed with the evaluation since a release of con\u0000dentiality will have already been obtained by the court"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "In general, the results of an evaluation are not revealed without the individual's consent. However, this is modi\u0000ed when the evaluation is being\nconducted in response to a court order. This issue is addressed by G. B. Melton et al. in their discussion of court-ordered evaluations\n(Psychological evaluations for the court, New York, Guilford, 1987). They note that the therapist-patient privilege does not apply when the\ntherapist-patient relationship is the creation of the court\" (p. 46), which is the case in a court-ordered evaluation. However, from an ethical\nperspective, you should always warn an individual of the limits of con\u0000dentiality.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Given the therapist-client relationship is established by the court, a release is not necessary in this case.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. While a written release is not necessary, the psychologist should discuss the limits of con\u0000dentiality with\nthe client.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. The court will not have obtained a release of con\u0000dentiality."}, {"question": "During the third therapy session, a client con\u0000des to her psychologist that she was once a prostitute. The psychologist is offended by this type of behavior, feels incapable of dealing with the client objectively, and terminates therapy immediately. His action is ________________________.", "options": {"A": "unethical because it represents abandonment of the client", "B": "ethical because he could not deal with the client objectively", "C": "unethical because a therapist should not allow personal opinions to interfere with professional activities", "D": "ethical since he has seen the client in therapy for only a few sessions"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "This situation clearly represents abandonment of the client. Although a therapist might feel it necessary to terminate therapy due to personal\nbeliefs, termination must be handled in a professional, sensitive manner. A therapist must evaluate his/her feelings carefully, and arrange for a\nreferral if necessary. Abrupt termination is not appropriate. During the course of therapy, a therapist may occasionally feel incapable of\nproviding a client with the services the client needs. When such situations arise the therapist should handle them in an appropriate,\nprofessional manner. If the therapist feels that he/she must terminate therapy, he/she should do so with extreme care. The therapist should be\ncareful not to shame the client, and an appropriate referral should be made.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Although termination might have been appropriate in this situation, the therapist did not handle the\ntermination properly.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. Although it is important that therapists remain objective, a therapist cannot always prevent personal\nfeelings from affecting therapy. When these feelings interfere with the quality of the therapy, the therapist needs to take the action necessary to\nprotect the interests of the client.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. The situation described was not handled appropriately and clearly constitutes an ethical violation."}, {"question": "Dr. Watson is one of three organizational psychologists who have been hired by a large corporation to conduct research on the selection of top-level managers. The psychologists' research has provided some evidence that managers who score high on introversion are the least effective managers in the company. Prior to submitting the project report, which recommends that the company screen candidates for top management positions for introversion, Dr. Watson approaches the Project Director, who is also an industrial psychologist, and argues that, although there is some evidence that there is a relationship between introversion and management effectiveness, the evidence is insu\u0000cient to justify eliminating potential managers on the basis of this alone. Despite Dr. Watson's protest, the Project Director refuses to review the report and submits it to the company. Dr. Watson decides to go to the company's management to point out the possible limitations of the report's recommendations. Which of the following statements best describes the ethical issue in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Watson clearly acted unethically by directly contacting management and thereby compromising the Project Director's relationship with the company.", "B": "Watson acted unethically by not consulting with the Ethics Committee before taking further action against the Project Director after his refusal to review the report.", "C": "Watson acted unethically by directly contacting management and the Project Director acted unethically by not reviewing the report.", "D": "Watson acted ethically but the Project Director acted unethically by not reviewing the report."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This response is most consistent with the requirements of Standards 1.04 and 1.05 of the Ethics Code (which apply to resolving ethical\nviolations by colleagues) and Standard 5.01 (which requires psychologists to avoid making false, deceptive, or misleading statements). Watson\nacted ethically and responsibly by attempting to prevent distortion of psychological \u0000ndings, while the Project Director acted unethically by not\nreviewing the data contained in the report in light of Watson's protest and thereby possibly providing the company with misleading information.\nAnswer A: Although direct contact with company management may seem extreme, this is not the best answer of those given.\nAnswer B: Watson's responsibility did not stop after discussing the matter with the Project Director.\nAnswer C: Although the Project Director acted unethically, Watson's behavior was ethical."}, {"question": "A colleague of yours has just attended a seminar on Culture and Ethics where several experts argued that it is inappropriate for psychologists to attempt to obtain a signed written informed consent from members of certain cultural groups prior to their participation in therapy. As an ethical psychologist, you tell your colleague that it __________.", "options": {"A": "is always necessary to obtain a signed written consent from therapy clients regardless of their ethnic/cultural background", "B": "is always necessary to obtain either a signed written consent or a signed waiver of consent from all therapy clients regardless of their ethnic/cultural background", "C": "is acceptable to document an informed consent from therapy clients in an alternative way only when those clients are illiterate", "D": "may be acceptable to document an informed consent from therapy clients in an alternative way, especially when a signed consent would be inappropriate for the client"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Standard 3.10(c) of the APA's Ethics Code and Standard I.22 of the Canadian Code of Ethics apply to this situation: Standard 3.10(c) states that\n\"Psychologists appropriately document written or oral consent, permission, and assent\"; and Standard I.22 states that psychologists \"accept\nand document oral consent, in situations in which signed consent forms are not acceptable culturally or in which there are other good reasons\nfor not using them.\" (Note, however, that legal requirements or institutional regulations may differ, and psychologists should check applicable\nlaws and regulations before deciding to forego a written consent.) An ongoing discussion in the literature is related to the need to consider\nmodifying the informed consent process for members of ethnic/cultural groups who may \u0000nd written consents objectionable or may want to\ninclude family members in the consent process. See, e.g., L. O. Gostin, Informed consent, cultural sensitivity, and respect for persons, Journal\nof the American Medical Association, 274(10), 844-845, 1995.\nAnswer A: The APA's Ethical Code requires documentation of consent, rather than written consent.\nAnswer B: This answer does not align with the language used in the APA Ethics Code. It is important to remember that psychologists need to\ndocument consent or assent. A signed consent is not required.\nAnswer C: The language in this answer does not re\u0000ect the ethical requirement regarding consent."}, {"question": "The APA's Ethics Code requires that informed consents be _____________________.", "options": {"A": "in writing", "B": "in writing whenever the individual is not a minor", "C": "documented", "D": "signed by the individual or his/her legal guardian"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Paragraph 3.10(d) of the Code states that \"Psychologists appropriately document written or oral consent, permission, and assent.\" Although\nwritten, signed consents are advisable in both therapy and research, they are not required by the Ethics Code.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. While signed consents are a good idea, the language in the APA Ethics Code does not state they are\nrequired.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. In the case of a minor client, the psychologist should document assent, however, this answer fails to\naddress the speci\u0000c language of the APA Ethics Code.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. Again, this answer does not accurately re\u0000ect the language contained in the APA Ethics Code."}, {"question": "You have been seeing a couple in therapy for six months. The wife calls you and says she wants to start seeing you for individual sessions also. You should _________________________.", "options": {"A": "bring up this possibility in the next couples session", "B": "tell her you cannot see her individually until couples therapy is over", "C": "refer her to another therapist for individual therapy", "D": "make an appointment with her to determine if her problem warrants individual therapy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Standard 10.02 of the Ethics Code makes it clear that, in couples therapy, psychologists must avoid potentially con\u0000icting roles. One way to\nensure that con\u0000icts do not occur is to clarify one's policies and responsibilities. It may or may not be the psychologist's policy to provide\nindividual therapy while also providing conjoint therapy, and this should have been clari\u0000ed at the onset of therapy. While a referral may be\nappropriate, the issue should \u0000rst be discussed in conjoint therapy.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. The psychologist has accepted the couple as the client. If the therapist continues working with the woman\nafter couples therapy has terminated, the therapist's objectivity may be impacted should the couple seek treatment together again.\nAnswer C: While a referral may be appropriate, bringing up the issue in the next couples therapy session would be the best initial course of\naction.\nAnswer D: To avoid potentially con\u0000icting roles, the psychologist's best approach would be to deal with the issue in the next conjoint therapy\nsession."}, {"question": "Dr. A, a psychologist, provides o\u0000ce space and secretarial services to Dr. B, a newly licensed psychologist, in exchange for 25% of the fee that Dr. B collects from his clients. Dr. A begins referring clients to Dr. B and charges him 35% for the referred clients. This is ________________________.", "options": {"A": "unethical because the arrangement violates APA's prohibition against \"fee splitting\"", "B": "unethical because the percentage that Dr. A is charging Dr. B is exploitative", "C": "ethical only if the larger fee for referred clients re\u0000ects increased costs to Dr. A for making referrals to Dr. B", "D": "ethical because the arrangement does not violate the provisions of the Ethics Code regarding referral fees"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "It would be important to read all possible answers before selecting your response. Although referral fees are not entirely prohibited by the\nEthics Code, there are some restrictions. It is ethical to pay a fee for a referral when the fee re\u0000ects the person's expenses for making the\nreferral. However, charging more for referred patients than non-referred patients when there are no additional costs would be unacceptable.\nAnswer A: If the fee re\u0000ects the person's expenses for making the referral, it is ethical.\nAnswer B: From the information available, we do not know whether the fee is reasonable or not. Therefore Answer C is the better choice.\nAnswer D: Because we do not know the full \u0000nancial picture, we cannot judge whether or not the fee violates ethical requirements. Therefore\nAnswer C is the best choice."}, {"question": "Dr. Jefferson has been seeing Mr. Emory, a prisoner at the local jail, in therapy for the past \u0000ve months. The parole board contacts Dr. Jefferson to request that he evaluate Mr. Emory for an upcoming parole hearing. Dr. Jefferson should _______________.", "options": {"A": "agree to evaluate Mr. Emory since he is best quali\u0000ed to evaluate him fairly", "B": "agree to evaluate Mr. Emory only if Mr. Emory agrees to the arrangement", "C": "agree to evaluate Mr. Emory only if he feels that Mr. Emory is eligible for parole", "D": "refuse to evaluate Mr. Emory for the parole hearing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Although the APA guidelines state that multiple relationships should be avoided if there is a risk of exploitation or impaired objectivity, an\nexception is allowed for therapists in a forensic setting to also conduct parole evaluations. Thus, it would be okay for Dr. Jefferson to complete\nthe parole evaluation.\nAnswer B: The decision rests with Dr. Jefferson in this case, and he needs to abide by the guidelines established by the APA.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect, as Dr. Jefferson needs to avoid making a decision regarding parole, given his role as Mr. Emory's therapist.\nAnswer D: Because parole evaluations are allowable multiple relationships, A is the correct answer."}, {"question": "You receive a letter from the attorney of a former client requesting that you send test information to her from the client's \u0000le. The requested information will be used in a court case and includes the test questions, the client's responses to those questions, and the client's test scores. The attorney's letter is accompanied by a signed release from the client. To be consistent with ethical requirements, you should __________.", "options": {"A": "release all test information as requested", "B": "release only a summary of the client's test information", "C": "release the clients responses and test scores but not the test questions", "D": "refuse to release any information to anyone other than a mental health professional"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Standards 9.04 and 9.11 of the APA's Ethics Code distinguish between test data and test material and state that test data may be provided to\nan attorney or other person listed in a client's release but that test material should not be released because doing so may constitute a breach\nof test integrity and security. As de\u0000ned in Standard 9.11, test material includes \"manuals, instruments, protocols, and test questions or\nstimuli.\" Of the answers given, this one is most consistent with the provisions of the APA's Ethics Code and with Paragraph IV.11 of the\nCanadian Code of Ethics for Psychologists.\nAnswer A: This answer does not distinguish between test data and test material.\nAnswer B: This answer does not align with Standards 9.04 and 9.11 of the APA's Ethics Code.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. Test data may be released with client consent."}, {"question": "Dr. Peters is asked by a local radio station if she would be interested in doing a talk show on Saturday mornings. On the show, she will respond to people who call into the station. To be consistent with the provisions of the Ethics Code, Dr. Peters should_______________________.", "options": {"A": "refuse to appear on the show", "B": "avoid giving direct advice of a psychological nature to callers", "C": "be sure that callers understand that their conversations with her do not constitute a therapeutic relationship", "D": "be sure that she has adequate malpractice insurance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "As noted by M. B. Canter, et al. (1994), in their discussion of the Ethics Code, the ethical requirements with regard to media psychology are \"still\nevolving.\" However, since this question refers speci\u0000cally to the Code, the best answer is the one that best resembles the Code's language. Of\nthe responses, this one comes closest to the language of Standard 5.04 (Media Presentations). Canter, et al. note that on radio talk shows, the\npsychologist should be sure that he/she clearly distinguishes between \"media psychology\" and \"therapy\" (Ethics for psychologists: A\ncommentary on the APA Ethics Code, Washington, DC, APA, 1994).\nAnswer A: It is not necessary to refuse to do the show, but certain precautions should be taken.\nAnswer B: Discussions of media psychology in the literature have generally concluded that advice is actually better than therapy in this context.\nAnswer D: This is probably a good idea, but not the best response to this question."}, {"question": "As de\u0000ned in the APA's Ethics Code, uninvited in-person solicitation of business by a psychologist is ________.", "options": {"A": "acceptable as long as the solicitation is not coercive", "B": "acceptable when it involves providing disaster or community outreach services", "C": "acceptable as long as the person's \"best interests\" are of paramount concern", "D": "never acceptable"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The uninvited in-person solicitation of business is addressed in Standard 5.06 of the APA's Ethics Code. Standard 5.06 generally prohibits\npsychologists from engaging in uninvited in-person solicitation of business. However, it also notes that the prohibition does NOT preclude\npsychologists from \"providing disaster or community outreach services.\"\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect, as the language does not re\u0000ect the requirements of the Ethics Code.\nAnswer C: This wording is not included in the Ethics Code.\nAnswer D: Uninvited in-person solicitation of business is permissible under limited circumstances as described in Standard 5.06."}, {"question": "Dr. Vic Vasquez, the director of a private counseling center, prepares and distributes a brochure describing the center's services. The brochure is available to potential clients and other interested people, and its text includes a list of the center's staff members and their credentials. Speci\u0000cally, it indicates that \u0000ve of the six members have doctoral degrees from accredited universities and that the staff member who does not have a degree is a \"Ph.D. candidate.\" The designation of the non-degreed staff member is ________________________________.", "options": {"A": "acceptable because it accurately re\u0000ects the staff member's status", "B": "acceptable because it was not meant to be deceptive", "C": "acceptable because this issue is not addressed by ethical guidelines", "D": "unacceptable because it is potentially misleading"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Standard 5.01 of the APA's Ethics Code and Principle III.2 of the Canadian Code of Ethics address this situation. Ethical guidelines caution\nagainst presenting one's credentials in a way that could be misleading. Dr. Vasquez should not have listed the staff member as a \"Ph.D.\ncandidate\" because it is possible that many people will not understand what this means.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect as many people will not understand what \"Ph.D. candidate\" means.\nAnswer B: Dr. Vasquez's intention is less relevant than the fact that ethics codes caution against misrepresenting a clinician's credentials.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect as both the APA's Ethics Code and the Canadian Code of Ethics address this matter."}, {"question": "The attorney of a former client of yours requests that you provide her with test data and other information included in the client's \u0000le. You should ___________.", "options": {"A": "provide the information since the request comes from the client's attorney", "B": "provide the information as requested only if you feel it is in the best interests of the client", "C": "provide the information as requested only if the client has signed a release", "D": "refuse to provide the information unless the client has signed a release and you believe the attorney is quali\u0000ed to interpret the information"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Release of client information to a third party is addressed in Ethics Code Standard 9.04, which states that psychologists may withhold test data\n\"to protect a client/patient or others from substantial harm.\" However, that does not seem relevant to this situation. The psychologist should\nrelease the test data as requested once the client has signed a release.\nAnswer A: This answer fails to account for the need to secure the client's release.\nAnswer B: The best interests of the client are certainly of concern, but this is not the best answer, because it overlooks the more pressing issue\nof the client's permission to release the data.\nAnswer D: This response is incorrect. Standard 9.04 states \"Pursuant to a client/patient release, psychologist provide test data to the\nclient/patient or other persons identi\u0000ed in the release.\""}, {"question": "A Mexican-American client tells his psychologist that he wants to consult a curandero about the problems he is having. To be consistent with the requirements of the APA's Guidelines for Providers of Psychological Services to Ethnic, Linguistic, and Culturally Diverse Populations, the psychologist should __________.", "options": {"A": "explain any concerns she has about the incompatibility of her approach with the approach of a traditional folk healer", "B": "\u0000nd out as much about curanderos as possible and incorporate what she learns into her treatment of the client", "C": "refer the client to a therapist who is more familiar with traditional Mexican culture", "D": "support the client in his desire to work with a curandero and express her willingness to collaborate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This issue is addressed in Paragraph 5 of the Guidelines. Paragraph 5(b) states that \"effective psychological intervention may be aided by\nconsultation with and/or inclusion of religious/spiritual leaders/practitioners relevant to the client's cultural and belief systems.\" This response\nis most consistent with this provision.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect because it does not align with the guidance issued in the document cited.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. It is unrealistic to think the psychologist could adopt the role or employ the practices of a curandero.\nAnswer C: A referral might be appropriate under some circumstances, but there is nothing in the question to indicate this is the preferred\ndirection."}, {"question": "A pharmaceutical company asks a psychologist to help it design and conduct a research study on one of its new drugs. The company representative says the company will publish the results of the study only if they are favorable to the drug. The psychologist should __________________________.", "options": {"A": "agree to participate only if he is able to approve the \u0000nal report", "B": "agree to participate only if the company gives him appropriate authorship credit for his work", "C": "agree to participate since it is up to the company to decide what to publish and who to list as an author", "D": "not agree to participate in designing or conducting the study"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Standard 5.01 applies to this situation. It states that \"psychologists do not knowingly make public statements that are false, deceptive, or\nfraudulent concerning their research, practice, or other work activities or those of persons or organizations with which they are a\u0000liated.\"\nAgreeing with the company's decision to publish only supportive data would be supporting a form of deception and would not be in the best\ninterests of the potential users of the drug. Therefore, this response is most consistent with the \"spirit\" of the Ethics Code.\nAnswer A, B, and C: These answers are incorrect as it fails to address the central issue, which is potential deception of the public."}, {"question": "A client you have been seeing for two months suddenly informs you that this will be his last session. You believe this is not a good time for the client to stop therapy and are concerned about the potential negative consequences of doing so. You should _________________________________.", "options": {"A": "allow the client to quit", "B": "provide the client with appropriate referrals", "C": "discuss with him the reasons you believe he should continue and recommend that he re-think his decision", "D": "inform him that you are not responsible for any negative consequence of terminating prematurely"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "This is the best response of those given. In this situation, the therapist would want to discuss the possible risks of terminating therapy at this\ntime and encourage the client to re-think his decision.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Obviously, a client has a right to discontinue treatment. This creates an ethical dilemma, however, when the\ntherapist believes that termination is ill-advised.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Although the action described in Answer B would also be important, it should follow a discussion about the\npossible consequences of leaving.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. Discussing the risks of terminating therapy at this time and documenting that conversation is the correct\ncourse of action."}, {"question": "Dr. Lane, a psychologist, is conducting a research study on peer pressure, and the study's participants will be 120 \u0000fth- grade students. The study will involve a 30-minute interview with each student and observation of the students during their lunch hour. Before beginning the study, Dr. Lane should _____________________.", "options": {"A": "obtain an informed consent from each student", "B": "obtain an informed consent from each student's parent", "C": "obtain an informed consent from appropriate school personnel", "D": "obtain the assent of each student and an informed consent from each student's parent"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This answer is consistent with the APA's Ethics Code Standard 3.10(b), which states: \"For persons who are legally incapable of giving informed\nconsent, psychologists nevertheless (1) provide an appropriate explanation, (2) seek the individual's assent, (3) consider such persons'\npreferences and best interests, and (4) obtain appropriate permission from a legally authorized person if such substitute consent is permitted\nor required by law.\" This requirement applies to minors and to adults who have been declared legally incompetent.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. As minors, the students cannot legally provide consent.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect as it fails to address the need to secure the students' assent.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. School personnel cannot provide consent on behalf of students."}, {"question": "When attempting to expand your private practice, it is important to keep in mind that in-person solicitations _____________________________.", "options": {"A": "are always unethical", "B": "are unethical only when they target individuals receiving similar services from another professional", "C": "are unethical only when they include deceptive or misleading information", "D": "are unethical when they include deceptive information or when the person solicited is susceptible to undue in\u0000uence"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This answer is most consistent with Standard 5.06 of the Ethic Code: Solicitations are unethical when they include deceptive or false\ninformation or when their targets are individuals who are vulnerable to undue in\u0000uence. Although the APA's prohibitions against the solicitation\nof clients have been limited by the requirements of the Federal Trade Commission, APA still considers certain types of business solicitations\nunethical.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. In-person solicitations might be ethically acceptable in some situations, such as when they are \"invited\"\nsolicitations.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. This type of solicitation might also be acceptable in some situations.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect as it is not the most complete response of those given."}, {"question": "Which of the following would a managed care organization consider most useful for reducing its risk for legal liability?", "options": {"A": "Capitation", "B": "Behavioral health \"carve-outs\"", "C": "Credentialing of providers", "D": "Utilization review"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Of the procedures listed in the responses, only one would be likely to reduce a managed health care organization's risk for legal liability.\nCredentialing providers helps ensure that providers are competent to provide services and thereby reduces the risk for legal liability (i.e., it\ncontributes to risk management).\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Capitation refers to contracting for a set of services on the basis of a predetermined fee. It reduces the\norganization's costs but does not protect it from legal liability.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Behavioral health \"carve-outs\" involve contracting behavioral health care services that are separate from the\nservices provided for other health care. It is not associated with risk management.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. Utilization review is another cost containment technique and may involve denying payment for services\nthat are not deemed necessary by the managed health care organization."}, {"question": "Results of Pope's (1992) survey of APA members indicated that which of the following issues was cited most frequently as being ethically troubling or challenging?", "options": {"A": "Fees", "B": "Sexual matters", "C": "Con\u0000dentiality", "D": "The conduct of colleagues"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Con\u0000dentiality was listed by 18% of respondents as an ethically troubling issue. This is a straightforward question. Unfortunately, it requires\nyou to be familiar with the results of a speci\u0000c survey by S. Pope (Ethical dilemmas as encountered by members of the APA: A national survey,\n\"American Psychologist, 47(3), pp. 397-411, 1992), although you might have been able to rely on your own experience and those of your\ncolleagues.\"\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Fees were mentioned by 14% of respondents as being ethically troublesome.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Only 4% of respondents listed sexual issues as a problem.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. The conduct of colleagues was listed as a problem by 4% of respondents."}, {"question": "The APA's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct ____________________________.", "options": {"A": "clearly prohibits a psychologist from having sexual relationships with students and supervisees under any circumstances", "B": "clearly prohibits a psychologist from having sexual relationships with a student or supervisee when he/she is in a position of authority over that person", "C": "prohibits a psychologist from having sexual relationships with students and supervisees indirectly through the \"dual relationship\" clause", "D": "does not address the issue of sexual relationships with students and supervisees"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Standard 7.07 states that \"Psychologists do not engage in sexual relationships with students or supervisees who are in their department,\nagency, or training center or over whom psychologists have or are likely to have evaluative authority.\" For the exam, you want to be familiar with\nthe provisions of the Ethics Code regarding sexual relations with supervisees and students so that you can answer questions like this. For\nadditional information on this issue, see the Ethics and Professional Issues chapter of the written study materials.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. It is important to consider whether the psychologist is or is likely to be working in a capacity where the\npsychologist will have evaluative authority over the student.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. While sexual relationships are included under statements regarding dual or multiple relationships, the\nEthics Code offers speci\u0000c guidance regarding students and supervisees.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. The Code provides speci\u0000c guidelines regarding students and supervisees."}, {"question": "The only time a client is able to see you is during her lunch hour, which requires her session to be limited to 30 minutes because of the distance she must travel to get to your o\u0000ce. When you bill her insurance company, however, you bill for the entire hour. This is ________________________.", "options": {"A": "ethical since the shorter sessions are being provided at the client's request", "B": "ethical since your policy is to always bill clients for an entire hour", "C": "unethical", "D": "a legal, but not an ethical, issue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "This is the best answer of those given. Providing any inaccurate information to the insurance company would represent insurance fraud.\nAnswer A: Psychologists must accurately report their time spent with clients/patients.\nAnswer B: Even if this were the policy of the psychologist, they are still acting unethically and illegally.\nAnswer D: This answer is incomplete. The action described in this question represents insurance fraud which is both illegal and unethical."}, {"question": "Dr. Xi has been asked to administer a psychological test to an individual who requires special accommodations because of a physical disability. The test was not developed or normed for individuals with this disability. The APA's Guidelines for Test User Quali\u0000cations recommend that, in this situation, psychologists ____________.", "options": {"A": "refuse to administer the test", "B": "refuse to administer the test unless there are no alternative methods of assessment", "C": "exercise \"special care\" when administering and scoring the test and interpreting its results", "D": "obtain legal advice on relevant legal requirements regarding the administration and use of the test"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The APA's position on this issue is summarized by S. M. Turner, S. T. DeMers, H. R. Fox, and G. M. Reed in APA's Guidelines for Test User\nQuali\u0000cations: An executive summary (American Psychologist, 56, pp. 1099-1113, 2001). In their summary, Turner et al. note that there may be\nlegal requirements that apply to the administration and use of tests when an individual requires special accommodations. To ensure that these\nrequirements are met, psychologists should consider them and, when appropriate, \"obtain legal advice on legal and regulatory requirements\nregarding appropriate administration of tests and use of test data when assessing individuals with disabilities\" (p. 1103). Note that Standard\n10.2 of the Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing also addresses this issue and requires test users who do not have adequate\nknowledge or experience in modifying tests for individuals with disabilities to seek guidance from a consultant or supervisor who has\nexpertise.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Simply refusing to administer the test is insu\u0000cient. Dr. Xi should seek consultation.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. The correct course of action is to seek the advice of a consultant and to consider whether there are legal\nissues that should be taken into account.\nAnswer C: Taking \"special care\" is not a su\u0000cient response. The psychologist should seek consultation and consider legal implications."}, {"question": "An undergraduate student is asked by a professor to assist in a new research project. The student will help with the literature review and data analysis. According to the Ethics Code, when the research is published, the student should, at a minimum ____________________________________.", "options": {"A": "be listed as a co-author", "B": "be mentioned in an introductory statement or a footnote", "C": "be mentioned in the abstract", "D": "receive a letter of recommendation from the professor"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The Ethics Code mandates that contributors to research be given appropriate credit. At a minimum, the student should be acknowledged for\nhis/her assistance in a footnote or introductory statement.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. The researcher has the option to list the student as a co-author but this is not required.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect because the abstract is not an appropriate section to recognize research assistants. An abstract provides\nthe reader with a brief summary of the research and its results.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. Although it is possible that the student may request a letter of recommendation, the teacher is not ethically\nbound to comply with the students' wishes because of his/her assistance in the research project."}, {"question": "A transformational leader de\u0000nes a goal to employees in a way that imbues the goal with meaning and purpose. This is referred to as:", "options": {"A": "priming", "B": "escalating commitment", "C": "framing", "D": "positioning"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Transformational leaders differ from transactional leaders in several ways. One way is that they motivate employees by giving goals added\nmeaning by placing them in a larger context (e.g., the \"good of society\"). Giving goals added meaning is referred to as framing. It is one of the\ncharacteristics of transformational leaders.\nAnswer A: The term priming is used in several contexts. For example, in the \u0000eld of learning and memory, it refers to unconscious cues that\nactivate related associations (e.g., seeing the word \"test\" reminds you of the licensing exam even if you are not aware that you saw the word\n\"test\").\nAnswer B: Escalating commitment refers to the tendency to continue with a previously chosen course of action even when it is not working.\nAnswer D: Positioning is a paradoxical technique used in family therapy. It involves helping the family see a symptom from a new perspective\nby exaggerating its severity."}, {"question": "According to Fiedler's contingency model, leadership is most effective when the leader's style (task- or person-oriented) matches which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Level of employee \"maturity\"", "B": "Organizational goals", "C": "Degree of situational control", "D": "Role expectations"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Fiedler's contingency model distinguishes between two types of leaders - high-LPC and low-LPC. High LPC leaders are person-oriented and are\nconcerned primarily with maintaining good interpersonal relationships, while low LPC leaders are task-oriented and are most concerned about\nsuccessful task performance. Fiedler's contingency model predicts that task-oriented (low-LPC) leaders are most effective when the situation\nis extreme - that is, when the leader has either low or high situational control, while person-oriented (high-LPC leaders) are most effective when\nsituational control is moderate. As de\u0000ned by Fiedler, situational control is determined by three factors: leader-member relations, task structure,\nand leader position power.\nAnswer A: The level of employee \"maturity\" sounds more like Hersey and Blanchard's situational leadership model.\nAnswer B: Organizational goals is not associated with leadership according to Fiedler's contingency model.\nAnswer D: Role expectations are not associated with leadership according to Fiedler's contingency model."}, {"question": "Research on goal-setting theory suggests that monetary incentives:", "options": {"A": "are irrelevant to goal achievement as long as the goals have been accepted by employees", "B": "are irrelevant to goal achievement as long as the goals have been self-set by employees", "C": "have a positive effect only if employees participate in determining the di\u0000culty of the goals", "D": "have a positive effect whether or not employees have been involved in goal-setting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This is one of those questions you probably could have answered correctly without being familiar with the research. Monetary incentives seem\nto have a positive effect on goal achievement (especially when they are tied speci\u0000cally to achievement) regardless of the circumstances.\n(Although some research suggests that monetary incentives can actually have a detrimental effect when goals are self-set, this is not\naddressed by any of the answers, so this response is the best one. See M. Erez, et al., Effects of goal di\u0000culty, self-set goals, and monetary\nrewards on dual-task performance, Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processing, 47, 247-269, 1990.).\nAnswers A, B, and C: Research on goal-setting theory suggests that monetary incentives have a positive effect whether or not employees have\nbeen involved in goal-setting."}, {"question": "Applicants for a job at the Acme Corporation are very upset by some of the items included in the company's Biographical Information Bank. They feel that the items are not work-related and therefore violate their privacy. Apparently, these items:", "options": {"A": "lack content validity", "B": "lack face validity", "C": "are unfair", "D": "are unreliable"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "In the situation described in this question, some of the items on the Biographical Information Bank appear to be unrelated to the requirements\nof the job. Face validity refers to the extent to which a measure appears to measure what it is supposed to measure.\nAnswer A: A test has content validity to the extent that it adequately samples the content or behavior domain that it is designed to measure.\nAnswer C: Unfairness occurs when members of one group consistently obtain lower scores on the predictor than members of another\ngroup, but the difference in predictor scores is not related to differences in scores on the criterion. The situation described above\ndoes not represent unfairness.\nAnswer D: It is a measure of reliability that provides us with an estimate of the proportion of variability in examinees’ obtained scores that\nis due to true differences among examinees on the attribute(s) measured by the test."}, {"question": "Yerkes and Dodson's (1908) \"inverted-U\" hypotheses predicts that:", "options": {"A": "moderate levels of group cohesiveness are associated with the highest levels of performance", "B": "moderate levels of arousal are associated with the highest levels of performance", "C": "performance is a function of task complexity, level of arousal, and learned habits", "D": "performance is the result of an interaction between motivation, ability, and task di\u0000culty"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The inverted-U theory hypothesis is also known as the Yerkes-Dodson Law and has been applied to a number of activities including academic\nlearning and sports performance. The Yerkes-Dodson 1908 study is a classic in the \u0000eld and is likely to be asked about on the exam. The\ninverted-U hypothesis predicts that, for every activity, there is an optimum level of arousal and that arousal above or below that level has a\nnegative impact on performance. Of the answers given, this one best describes this hypothesis.\nAnswer A: It is the moderate levels of arousal, not group cohesiveness, that is associated with the highest levels of performance.\nAnswer C: This answer describes an assumption underlying Hull's (1943) drive theory.\nAnswer D: This answer does not accurately describe the inverted-U hypothesis."}, {"question": "The primary goal of a \"realistic job preview\" is to:", "options": {"A": "increase productivity", "B": "reduce turnover", "C": "increase achievement motivation", "D": "reduce sabotage"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "A realistic job preview provides job applicants with realistic information about the job. An assumption underlying the use of realistic job\npreviews is that disillusionment with the job is the primary cause of turnover. Realistic job previews attempt to reduce disillusionment and\nresulting turnover by providing accurate information about the job.\nAnswers A, C, and D: The primary goal of a \"realistic job preview\" is to reduce turnover."}, {"question": "From the perspective of the situational leadership model, when an employee is both competent and con\u0000dent, the best leadership style is which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Selling", "B": "Telling", "C": "Participating", "D": "Delegating"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Hersey and Blanchard's (1982) situational leadership model is based on the premise that a leader's style should match the \"maturity level\" of\nthe employee, which is related to both ability and motivation. A delegating leadership style is appropriate for highly mature employees. When\nadopting this style, the leader is low in both relationship and task behavior.\nAnswer A: A selling style is appropriate for employees who are moderate in maturity (are unable but willing or con\u0000dent).\nAnswer B: A telling leadership style is best for employees who are low in maturity (are both unable and unwilling or insecure).\nAnswer C: A participative leadership style is most effective for employees who are moderate to high in maturity (are able but unwilling or\ninsecure)."}, {"question": "According to John D. Krumboltz (1996), career development and career choice are primarily related to:", "options": {"A": "needs and drives", "B": "early childhood experiences", "C": "ego identity development", "D": "social learning"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Krumboltz proposes that career decisions are based on what the individual has learned. Krumboltz's emphasis is on social learning -- i.e.,\nmodeling and other aspects of the social environment -- and his theory is referred to as the Social Learning Theory of Career Decision Making\n(SLTCDM). Additional information about Krumboltz's theory is provided in the Industrial-Organizational Psychology chapter of the written study\nmaterials.\nAnswers A, B, and C: Career development and career choice are most closely associated with social learning."}, {"question": "Research investigating the relationship between leader intelligence and leader effectiveness has found a __________ relationship between these two variables.", "options": {"A": "strong positive", "B": "strong negative", "C": "weak positive", "D": "weak negative"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Perhaps surprisingly, the research has found that scores on cognitive ability tests are poor predictors of leader effectiveness. Fiedler and T.\nLink note that prior reviews of the research report median correlations of .20 to .30 between various measures of intellectual ability and\nleadership and managerial performance [Leader intelligence, interpersonal stress, and task performance, in R. J. Sternberg & R. J. Wagner\n(Eds.), Mind in context: Interactionist perspectives on human intelligence (pp. 152-170), New York, Cambridge University Press, 1994].\nExplanations for these low correlations focus on potential moderating variables. For example, one explanation is that it is the relative\nintelligence of the leader to the intelligence of his/her supervisees that are most important - i.e., that leaders are most effective when they are\nonly slightly more intelligent than their followers.\nAnswers A, B, and D: You needed to identify that the relationship between leader intelligence and leader effectiveness has found a weak\npositive relationship between the two variables."}, {"question": "E. H. Schein (1992) proposed that organizational culture exists on which of the following three levels?", "options": {"A": "Time orientation, space orientation, and activity orientation", "B": "Microsystem, mesosystem, and macrosystem", "C": "Observable artifacts, espoused values and beliefs, and underlying assumptions", "D": "Informal organizational elements, formal organizational elements, and individual characteristics of employees"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "According to Schein (1992), organizational culture provides stability and meaning. Although culture can reduce anxiety among organizational\nmembers that is caused by uncertainty about current and future events, it can also impede change. These are the three levels of organizational\nculture identi\u0000ed by Schein.\nAnswer A: These are three of the six value orientations that Kluckhohn and Strodbeck (1961) used to describe differences among cultural\ngroups. The other three dimensions are relation to nature, view of human nature, and view of relationships among people\nAnswer B: These are three of the \u0000ve systems or levels of Bronfenbrenner's (2004) ecological model of developmental in\u0000uences.\nAnswer D: According to Nadler (1988), these are three of the four factors that must be addressed to accomplish planned change in\norganizations. The fourth factor is characteristics of the employees and managerial tasks."}, {"question": "The results of research conducted at Ohio State University in the 1950s indicated that the behavior of leaders can be described in terms of which of the following dimensions?", "options": {"A": "Initiating structure and consideration", "B": "Theory X and Theory Y", "C": "Participative and autocratic", "D": "Transformational and transactional"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "All of the terms listed in the answers have been used to describe leader behaviors or leader styles. However, this question is asking about the\nresearch conducted in the 1950s at Ohio State University, so only one of the answers is correct. A number of approaches to leadership\ndistinguish between leaders who are task-oriented and leaders who are person-oriented. This distinction is traceable to studies conducted at\nOhio State University, which found that leader behavior can be described in terms of two dimensions: initiating structure (task-orientation) and\nconsideration (person-orientation).\nAnswer B: Theory X and Theory Y were concepts developed by the social psychologist, Douglas McGregor.\nAnswer C: Kurt Lewin identi\u0000ed three different leadership styles: autocratic, participative, and delegative.\nAnswer D: Transformational and transactional leadership styles were developed by James MacGregor Burns."}, {"question": "A problem with establishing \"comparable worth\" is that:", "options": {"A": "job evaluation techniques are not as useful for very complex jobs", "B": "males and females may use different strategies to reach the same decision or solution", "C": "the job evaluation techniques themselves may be gender-biased", "D": "it is di\u0000cult to compare achievement across different domains"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The doctrine of comparable worth extends \"equal content\" as a determinant of wages to \"comparable worth\". In other words, even if two jobs\nare different in terms of content, they should have the same salary as long as they involve similar processes and demands. Comparable worth\nbecame an issue because of the gender differences in wages. Di\u0000culty in establishing comparable worth, at least according to some experts,\nis that the job evaluation techniques used to establish it may themselves be gender-biased. This issue continues to be debated in the literature.\nAnswers A, B, and D: While still debatable in the literature, the di\u0000culty in establishing comparable worth is that the job evaluation techniques\nused to establish it may themselves be gender-biased."}, {"question": "Which of the following is not true of mediation?", "options": {"A": "A mediator can impose a settlement.", "B": "A mediator does not serve as an advocate for either party.", "C": "Participation in mediation is voluntary.", "D": "Arbitration provides a more \u0000nal resolution than mediation."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Mediation is the most used third-party option. A neutral third party (mediator) uses various tactics to facilitate voluntary agreement between\nthe disputants. Remember you are looking for a statement that is not true. Because the mediator has no formal powers, he or she cannot\nimpose a settlement.\nAnswer B: A mediator serves as a neutral facilitator, and thus does not advocate for either participant.\nAnswer C: Mediation is, indeed, voluntary.\nAnswer D: Arbitration is, indeed, the \u0000nal and most formal settlement technique."}, {"question": "The Position Analysis Questionnaire (PAQ) is useful for:", "options": {"A": "conducting a job analysis", "B": "evaluating an employee's job performance", "C": "reducing criterion contamination", "D": "reducing adverse impact"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The Position Analysis Questionnaire provides information on six dimensions of worker activity (e.g., mental processes, relationships). The PAQ\nis a quantitative, worker-oriented method of collecting data for the purpose of job analysis.\nAnswers B, C, and D: The PAQ is most useful for conducting a job analysis."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT an example of a centralized communication network?", "options": {"A": "Wheel", "B": "Chain", "C": "Circle", "D": "Y"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "In a centralized communication network, all communication passes through a central person. The wheel, chain, and Y are examples of a\ncentralized communication network, while the circle is an example of a decentralized network in which information can \u0000ow freely from one\nindividual to another without having to go through a central person.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"wheel\", is incorrect as it does have a center that the outer regions can pass through.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"chain\", is incorrect as it does have a center that the outer regions can pass through.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"Y\", is incorrect as it does have a center that the outer regions can pass through."}, {"question": "Job evaluation is to wages as needs assessment is to:", "options": {"A": "bene\u0000ts", "B": "selection", "C": "vocational choice", "D": "training"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to be able to distinguish between job analysis, job evaluation, and needs analysis. A needs analysis (or assessment) is\nconducted in organizations to identify training needs and determine the appropriate content for training programs.\nAnswers A, B, and C: Job evaluation is to wages as needs assessment is to training."}, {"question": "As de\u0000ned by Donald Super, career maturity:", "options": {"A": "is achieved by most people by the time they reach their late 20s or early 30s", "B": "is not fully achieved until mid-life or later", "C": "refers to the individual's mastery of tasks at each developmental stage", "D": "refers to the degree of match between the individual's self-concept and current occupational aspirations"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Super's life space/life span theory includes a stage theory that encompasses the entire lifespan. The \u0000ve stages are growth, exploration,\nestablishment, maintenance, and disengagement. Each stage involves a set of developmental tasks, and mastery of those tasks represents\ncareer maturity. Career maturity prepares the individual to ful\u0000ll the life roles characteristic of the stage the person is in as well as for the tasks\nof the subsequent stage.\nAnswers A, B, and D: As de\u0000ned by Donald Super, career maturity refers to the individual's mastery of tasks at each developmental stage."}, {"question": "Abraham Maslow derived his need hierarchy theory from:", "options": {"A": "his experiences in academia and research", "B": "his experience as an organizational consultant", "C": "a review of the literature on motivation", "D": "the principles of psychodynamic theory"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "During his work as a psychologist, Maslow noticed that people are motivated by different needs. Maslow derived his theory about the need\nhierarchy and self-actualization from his observations of patients, work with college students, and study of people he believed to be highly\nproductive (e.g., Abraham Lincoln, Thomas Jefferson, Eleanor Roosevelt).\nAnswers B, C, and D: Abraham Maslow derived his need hierarchy theory from his experiences in academia and research."}, {"question": "The bounded rationality model of decision-making in organizations most often fails because:", "options": {"A": "decisions are affected by \"social desirability\" and other decision biases", "B": "decisions are typically guided by economic concerns only", "C": "too many people are involved in the decision-making process", "D": "there is insu\u0000cient information, time, and money"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Decision-making strategies have been categorized in terms of several basic types. One strategy is referred to as bounded rationality decision-\nmaking; as its name implies, it involves examining alternatives in a rational way to identify the best one. Herbert Simon has proposed that this\nform of decision-making often fails because the decision-maker often \"satis\u0000ces\" (rather than \"maximizes\"); i.e., he/she chooses the \u0000rst\nsatisfactory alternative rather than continue to search for the optimal alternative due primarily to inadequate information about the problem\nand alternative solutions, to a lack of time and money, and to limits on the decision maker's own intelligence.\nAnswer A: Social desirability is a type of response style that affects how an individual responds to test items and is not relevant to decision-\nmaking strategies.\nAnswer B: Although the answer, \"decisions are typically guided by economic concerns only\", is true in some cases, it is not an accurate\ngeneralization.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"too many people are involved in the decision-making process\", has not been identi\u0000ed by the research on bounded\nrationality decision-making as a cause of bad decisions."}, {"question": "According to Fiedler's (1984) contingency theory of leadership, task-oriented leaders are most effective when the work situation is _______________ with regard to the leader's sense of certainty, predictability, and control over his/her employees.", "options": {"A": "moderate in favorableness", "B": "very high in favorableness", "C": "very low in favorableness", "D": "very high or very low in favorableness"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Fiedler's theory distinguishes between low-LPC (task-oriented) and high-LPC (person-oriented) leaders. Fiedler concluded that low-LPC leaders\nare most effective in extreme situations, i.e., in situations that are very high or very low in favorableness. In contrast, high-LPC leaders are most\neffective in moderately favorable situations.\nAnswers A, B, and C: According to the contingency theory of leadership, task-oriented leaders are most effective when the work situation is\nvery high or very low in favorableness with regard to the leader's sense of certainty, predictability, and control over his/her employees."}, {"question": "Criterion de\u0000ciency is most likely to result in:", "options": {"A": "high validity", "B": "low validity", "C": "no reliability", "D": "low reliability"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Criterion de\u0000ciency refers to the degree to which a criterion measure fails to measure all aspects of the ultimate (conceptual) criterion. A\ncriterion measure can have high reliability but low validity - i.e., it can give consistent results but measure only some aspects of the ultimate\ncriterion.\nAnswers A, C, and D: A criterion measure can have high reliability but low validity."}, {"question": "One area of interest to industrial psychologists is the effects of rest breaks on productivity. For example, Bhatia and Murrell (1969) evaluated daily output \u0000gures after the introduction of rest breaks that reduced the total work time from eight to seven hours per day. Generally speaking, studies like the one conducted by Bhatia and Murrell have indicated that the introduction of rest breaks results in:", "options": {"A": "a rise in daily output despite the fact that employees actually spend less time on the job", "B": "a decrease in daily output due to reduced time on the job", "C": "no signi\u0000cant change in productivity rates", "D": "a decrease in productivity but an increase in job satisfaction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Even if you are unfamiliar with the research in this area, it makes sense that the less the fatigue, the greater the productivity. Studies on the\neffects of rest breaks have indicated that such breaks actually increase productivity despite reduced time on the job, presumably due to\ndecreased fatigue.\nAnswer B: There is no evidence that rest breaks cause a decrease in output.\nAnswer C: Rest breaks do appear to cause an increase in productivity.\nAnswer D: Rest breaks do not decrease productivity."}, {"question": "The rotating shift is associated with a number of adverse consequences including fatigue, concentration, and performance errors. The negative effects can be reduced, however, when:", "options": {"A": "the rotation is clockwise and weekly", "B": "the rotation is clockwise and either rapid (every 3 days) or slow (every 3 weeks)", "C": "the rotation is counterclockwise and weekly", "D": "the rotation is counterclockwise and either rapid (every 3 days) or slow (every 3 weeks)"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Several factors contribute to the impact of the rotating shift on worker health and performance. There is evidence that the rotating shift has\nfewer negative consequences when the rotation is clockwise (morning, afternoon, night) and is either rapid or slow. Weekly rotations are\ngenerally most detrimental because they do not allow resynchronization of circadian rhythms (which slow rotations do) and are long enough to\nallow for the build-up of a sleep de\u0000cit (which fast rotations do not do). See, e.g., O. Van Reeth, Sleep and circadian disturbances in shift work:\nStrategies for their management, Hormone Research, 49, 158-162, 1998.\nAnswer A: Weekly rotations do not reduce the negative effects of fatigue, concentration di\u0000culties, and performance errors.\nAnswer C: Weekly counterclockwise rotations are the worst possible rotation for the adverse effects of fatigue, concentration di\u0000culties, and\nperformance errors.\nAnswer D: Counterclockwise rotations yield the adverse effects of fatigue, concentration di\u0000culties, and performance errors."}, {"question": "In training, providing identical elements is most useful for:", "options": {"A": "maximizing overlearning", "B": "reducing the probability of proactive and retroactive interference", "C": "ensuring transfer of training", "D": "providing opportunities for active practice"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The term identical elements refer to similarities in the learning and performance environments. Transfer of training is maximized when learning\nconditions are similar to performance conditions (i.e., when they are similar in terms of task, equipment, environment, etc.).\nAnswers A, B, and D: In training, providing identical elements is most useful for ensuring transfer of training."}, {"question": "Wolfgang Kohler's research on animal learning and animal cognition led to his conclusion that learning is:", "options": {"A": "biologically-based", "B": "insightful", "C": "the result of trial-and-error", "D": "the result of reinforcement and punishment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Kohler is a co-founder of Gestalt psychology, which focuses on perception. He is probably best known for his research on animals, especially\nSultan the chimpanzee. Kohler's research revealed that like humans, animals seem to experience an \"aha\" experience (\"insight\") while solving\nproblems.\nAnswer A: Albert Ellis believed that irrational beliefs are the results of certain biological tendencies.\nAnswer C: Trial-and-error learning occurs when an individual successively tries various responses in a situation, seemingly at random, until one\nis successful. Edward Thorndike posited that learning is due to the connections that develop between responses and stimuli as a result of trial-\nand-error.\nAnswer D: B. F. Skinner described the voluntary enactment of behaviors as a result of reinforcement and punishment."}, {"question": "A mental health facility establishes a new policy that prohibits the use of all forms of positive punishment to control the behavior of its inpatients. Which of the following techniques would be unacceptable under this policy?", "options": {"A": "Verbal reprimands (e.g., yelling \"stop\" after an undesirable behavior)", "B": "Response cost (e.g., removing TV privileges after an undesirable behavior)", "C": "Premack Principle (e.g., making TV privileges contingent on the performance of a desirable behavior)", "D": "Time-out (e.g., placing the individual in an empty room for ten minutes after an undesirable behavior)"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Positive punishment involves applying a stimulus following a behavior to decrease the frequency of that behavior. Verbal reprimands are a type\nof positive punishment because they involve applying a stimulus after a behavior in order to reduce or eliminate that behavior.\nAnswer B: Response cost is a type of negative punishment and involves removing a stimulus following a behavior in order to decrease the\nfrequency of that behavior.\nAnswer C: The Premack Principle is a type of positive reinforcement that involves using a high-frequency behavior as a reinforcer for low-\nfrequency behavior.\nAnswer D: Time-out is classi\u0000ed as a type of negative punishment because it involves removing all sources of reinforcement following a\nbehavior to decrease or eliminate that behavior."}, {"question": "Research (e.g., Davidson and Parker, 2001) evaluating the mechanisms that contribute to the effectiveness of EMDR (eye movement desensitization and reprocessing) has generally found that its bene\u0000cial effects are attributable to:", "options": {"A": "rapid eye movements", "B": "exposure to the feared stimulus", "C": "a placebo effect", "D": "counterconditioning"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "P. R. Davidson and K. C. H. Parker summarize the research in their article, Eye movement desensitization and reprocessing (EMDR): A meta-\nanalysis Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology, 69,305-316, 2001. Based on the results of their meta-analysis, these investigators\nconcluded that \"eye movements are unnecessary and that EMDR may be viewed as an imaginal exposure technique\" (p. 2).\nAnswer A: EMDR therapy utilizes bi-lateral stimulation, and lateral eye movements are one form of bi-lateral stimulation. The results of the\nmeta-analysis in this question indicate that the exposure component of EMDR, not the bi-lateral stimulation, accounts for its effectiveness.\nAnswer C: The results of the meta-analysis indicated that bene\u0000ts of EMDR are due to exposure to the feared stimulus and not due to a\nplacebo effect.\nAnswer D: Counterconditioning eliminates a maladaptive behavior by pairing a stimulus (CS) associated with that behavior with a stimulus (US)\nthat naturally elicits an incompatible behavior so that the maladaptive behavior is replaced by the incompatible behavior. An example of in vivo\n(\"real life\") counterconditioning is pairing alcohol consumption with an electric shock to reduce alcohol use."}, {"question": "The use of shaping to establish a complex behavior depends on which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Latent learning", "B": "Positive reinforcement", "C": "Higher-order conditioning", "D": "Successive discrimination"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Shaping involves reinforcing successive approximations to the target (desired) behavior. When using shaping to establish a new behavior,\nresponses that come closer and closer to the desired behavior are successively reinforced.\nAnswer A: Latent learning occurs without direct reinforcement.\nAnswer C: Higher-order conditioning is a type of classical (not operant) conditioning. Higher-order conditioning is a procedure where a\nconditioned stimulus (CS) of one experiment acts as the unconditioned stimulus (US) of another to condition a neutral stimulus.\nAnswer D: Successive discrimination teaches the learner to respond differently to two or more stimuli that are presented successively."}, {"question": "When using \u0000ooding (in vivo exposure) to treat a client's Speci\u0000c Phobia, which of the following instruments would be most useful for monitoring the client's progress in treatment and determining when to terminate treatment?", "options": {"A": "SUDS", "B": "SCID", "C": "MMSE", "D": "BDI"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Flooding involves exposing the client to high anxiety-arousing situations. When using this technique, the client's anxiety level is regularly\nmonitored to determine the progress of the exposure during a single session and over multiple sessions. Only one of the instruments listed in\nthe answers is useful for this purpose. When using SUDS (Subjective Units of Distress Scale), the client rates his/her level of anxiety on a scale\nranging from 1 to 100 (or, alternatively, from 1 to 10) at regular intervals during exposure. In general, a single exposure session should be\ncontinued until a 50% reduction in anxiety level occurs (Source: Rosqvist, J. (2005). Exposure treatments for anxiety disorders: a practitioner's\nguide to concepts, methods, and evidence-based practice. New York: Taylor & Francis Group.) Note that being familiar with SUDS would have\nhelped you identify this as the correct answer even if you are unfamiliar with its use in conjunction with \u0000ooding.\nAnswer B: The SCID (Structured Clinical Interview for DSM) is a semi-structured diagnostic interview that is useful for deriving a client's\ndiagnosis.\nAnswer C: The MMSE (Mini Mental State Exam) is a screening test for cognitive impairment in older adults.\nAnswer D: The BDI (Beck Depression Inventory) is useful for assessing and monitoring the severity of depressive symptoms."}, {"question": "In the context of operant conditioning, \"fading\" refers to:", "options": {"A": "a reduction in positive reinforcement", "B": "the gradual removal of prompts", "C": "extinguishing a previously reinforced response", "D": "eliminating stimulus generalization"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Nonverbal and verbal prompts are used to help initiate a response. Because the ultimate goal is for the response to occur independently,\nprompts are gradually removed once the response is well-established. This procedure is referred to as fading. Fading is often confused with\nthinning. For the exam, you want to be sure you know the difference between the two terms.\nAnswer A: A reduction in positive reinforcement (e.g., switching from a continuous to an intermittent schedule) is referred to as thinning.\nAnswer C: Operant extinction occurs when reinforcement is consistently withheld from a previously reinforced behavior to decrease or\neliminate that behavior. This will extinguish a previously reinforced response.\nAnswer D: Stimulus generalization may be eliminated when the occurrence of a behavior is affected by the presence of discriminative stimuli.\nThe behavior is then considered to be under stimulus control."}, {"question": "The primary goal of self-instructional training (SIT) for children exhibiting hyperactivity and excessive impulsivity is to:", "options": {"A": "eliminate obsessive ruminations", "B": "re-align intention and action", "C": "clarify response contingencies", "D": "insert thought between stimulus and response"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Self-instructional training (SIT) involves training the individual to guide his/her actions with internally-originated verbal instructions. Hyperactive\nand impulsive children often respond to events automatically. The goal of SIT is to reduce automatic behaviors by inserting thoughts between\nan event and the response to that event.\nAnswer A: SIT is typically used to treat impulsive and hyperactive children to improve their behavioral responses, not to eliminate obsessive\nruminations.\nAnswer B: Although SIT may be used to improve behavioral responses, the primary goal is the interpolation of adaptive and self-controlling\nthoughts between a stimulus situation and the response to that situation.\nAnswer C: Although self-instruction includes self-reinforcement, this is not the primary goal of SIT."}, {"question": "Pavlov found that requiring dogs to make di\u0000cult discriminations between stimuli during the course of conditioning trials led to which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Superstitious behavior", "B": "Experimental neurosis", "C": "Overshadowing", "D": "Blocking"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "In classical conditioning, stimulus discrimination training reduces stimulus generalization by teaching the subject to respond with a\nconditioned response (CR) only in the presence of the original conditioned stimulus (CS). When discriminations are di\u0000cult, the subject may\nexhibit experimental neurosis, such as restlessness, aggressiveness, or fear. In one series of studies, Pavlov found that dogs that were\nconditioned to respond with salivation to a circle but not to respond with salivation to an ellipse subsequently exhibited experimental neurosis\nwhen presented with a more circular ellipse. He attributed the experimental neurosis to a con\u0000ict between cortical excitation and inhibition.\nAnswer A: Accidental and noncontingent reinforcement can lead to superstitious behavior. When the behavior is reinforced, it increases the\nlikelihood that the behavior will occur again.\nAnswer C: Overshadowing occurs when one stimulus is more salient to the subject than a second stimulus, although both are presented at the\nsame time prior to an unconditioned stimulus (US). Subsequent presentations of the two stimuli together produce a conditioned response (CR,)\nbut when the two stimuli are presented separately, only the stimulus that is more salient to the subject will produce a CR.\nAnswer D: Blocking occurs once an association has been made between a CS and US which prevents future pairing between the US and a\nsecond neutral stimulus. Because there is already an association between a CS and a US, the presence of the CS blocks an association being\nmade between a new neutral stimulus and US when the CS and the new neutral stimulus are presented together prior to the US."}, {"question": "The effectiveness of the procedure known as time-out is maximized when:", "options": {"A": "the length of the time-out is directly related to the severity of the misbehavior", "B": "the alternative behavior is truly incompatible with the problem behavior", "C": "the time-out is alternated with the technique known as response cost", "D": "the time-out begins with a brief explanation of why it is being applied"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Time-out is used to reduce undesirable behavior and involves removing the individual from all sources of positive reinforcement following the\nbehavior for a speci\u0000c period of time. To maximize its effectiveness, the time-out should begin with either a signal or a brief explanation of why\nit is being applied (e.g., \"You hit your brother, so you'll have to sit in the time-out chair for \u0000ve minutes\").\nAnswer A: Relatively short time-outs (1-5 minutes) have been found to be as effective as longer time-outs, and there is no evidence linking the\neffectiveness of this technique to matching its length to the severity of the misbehavior.\nAnswer B: When using time-out, there is no incompatible behavior. (Of course, whenever one behavior is being eliminated, it's always a good\nidea to reinforce an alternative behavior at the same time, but this is not part of the time-out procedure itself.)\nAnswer C: Response cost is an application of negative punishment and involves removing a speci\u0000c reinforcer each time the target behavior is\nperformed (such as taking away a child's computer privileges for a speci\u0000ed period of time when they engage in a target behavior). There is no\ndata supporting the combination of response cost and time-out."}, {"question": "Identifying alternative behaviors to replace targeted undesirable behaviors is an integral part of which of the following behavioral techniques?", "options": {"A": "Response cost", "B": "Implosive therapy", "C": "Contingency management", "D": "Negative practice"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "While some behavioral techniques are designed speci\u0000cally to either decrease or increase a behavior, others attempt to accomplish both goals.\n\"Contingency\" refers to the consequence of a behavior, and contingency management involves identifying and applying punishments for\nundesirable behaviors and identifying and applying reinforcements for desirable ones.\nAnswer A: Response cost is used to decrease undesirable behavior. Response cost involves the removal of a speci\u0000c positive reinforcement\neach time a behavior is performed.\nAnswer B: Implosive therapy is used to extinguish a conditioned response and does not attempt to deliberately substitute an alternative\nresponse (although this may, of course, occur). Implosive therapy is conducted in imagination and combines exposure with psychodynamic\ninterpretations.\nAnswer D: When using negative practice, the person is required to deliberately repeat the undesirable behavior. It has been found to be an\neffective treatment for tics."}, {"question": "From the perspective of Rational-Emotive Behavior Therapy:", "options": {"A": "irrational beliefs are acquired primarily through social learning processes", "B": "irrational beliefs are acquired largely through the process of selective reinforcement", "C": "people are biologically prone to the acquisition of irrational beliefs", "D": "people adopt irrational beliefs as the result of early traumatic events that are still unresolved"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "This question is asking about one of the lesser-known facts about REBT. Ellis believed that the majority of behavior has a biological basis. With\nregard to irrational thoughts, he proposed that they are attributable to natural tendencies toward moodiness, negativism, and excitement-\nseeking.\nAnswer A: Ellis did not believe that irrational thoughts are primarily acquired through social learning processes.\nAnswer B: Ellis posited a biological basis for irrational beliefs, not learning them through selective reinforcement.\nAnswer D: Ellis did not predict a relationship between irrational beliefs and the experience of early trauma."}, {"question": "Research on the serial position effect has provided evidence for which of the following?", "options": {"A": "the presence of hemispheric specialization", "B": "the distinction between short- and long-term memory", "C": "the effects of proactive and retroactive interference", "D": "the effects of latent learning"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The serial position effect refers to the tendency of people to recall words in the beginning and end of a list better than words in the middle of\nthe list when asked to recall the words immediately after reading the list. The ability to recall words from the beginning of the list is referred to\nas the primacy effect and is attributed to the storage of those words in long-term memory. In contrast, the ability to recall words from the end\nof the list is referred to as the recency effect and is attributed to the presence of those words in short-term memory.\nAnswer A: Hemispheric specialization refers to the different functions performed by two hemispheres of the brain. For most people, written and\nspoken language, logic, and analytical thinking are left hemisphere functions and spatial relationships, creativity, and facial recognition are right\nhemisphere functions.\nAnswer C: Retroactive interference occurs when recently learned (new) material interferes with the recall of previously learned (old) material,\nwhereas proactive interference occurs when prior (old) learning interferes with the learning or recall of subsequent (new) material.\nAnswer D: Tolman's model of latent learning proposes that learning can occur without reinforcement and without being manifested in\nperformance improvement."}, {"question": "Which of the following is considered a cornerstone of Bandura's social-cognitive theory?", "options": {"A": "Cognitive load", "B": "Equilibration", "C": "Scaffolding", "D": "Vicarious learning"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Bandura proposed that human behavior is in\u0000uenced by vicarious learning - i.e., by watching another person engage in that behavior. Knowing\nthat Bandura's theory is also known as social learning theory and observational learning theory would have helped you identify the correct\nanswer to this question.\nAnswer A: Cognitive load is a key concept in cognitive theories that address the amount of information that can be held in short-term memory\nbefore information is lost.\nAnswer B: Equilibration is a central concept in Piaget's constructivism and refers to the drive to achieve an optimal state of balance between\none's cognitive structures and the demands of the environment.\nAnswer C: Scaffolding is associated with Vygotsky's sociocultural theory and refers to the assistance given to a learner by a parent, teacher, or\nanother person within the learner's zone of proximal development."}, {"question": "Research on Baddeley's (2000) multi-component model of working memory suggests that the ____________ plays an essential role in mental arithmetic and is responsible for accessing and executing computational algorithms and heuristics.", "options": {"A": "sensory register", "B": "central executive", "C": "phonological loop", "D": "visuo-spatial sketchpad"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "A. D. Baddeley's (2000) multi-component model of working memory describes working memory as being comprised of a central executive that\ncontrols and regulates three subsystems - the phonological loop, the visuo-spatial sketchpad, and the episodic buffer. The central executive\nserves a number of supervisory functions including focusing and switching attention, controlling encoding and retrieval strategies, and\nmentally manipulating information held in the subsystems. For example, while performing mental arithmetic, the central executive is\nresponsible for selecting and executing calculation heuristics.\nAnswer A: Sensory register is not one of the systems of working memory described by Baddeley.\nAnswer C: The phonological loop is responsible for processing and temporarily storing verbally encoded information. It also plays a role in\nmental arithmetic but, in contrast to the central executive, is responsible for the temporary storage of partial solutions and subvocal rehearsal\nof running totals.\nAnswer D: The visuo-spatial sketchpad is responsible for processing and temporarily storing visually encoded information."}, {"question": "A therapy client says, \"I feel useless and incompetent and, therefore, I must be a worthless person.\" From the perspective of cognitive therapy, this is an example of:", "options": {"A": "all-or-none thinking", "B": "overgeneralization", "C": "personalization", "D": "emotional reasoning"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "In this situation, the client is using her emotions as evidence for the way things really are. The logic underlying emotional reasoning is \"I feel,\ntherefore I am.\"\nAnswer A: All-or-nothing thinking is the tendency to evaluate performance or personal qualities in terms of extremist, black-and-white\ncategories.\nAnswer B: Overgeneralization refers to arbitrarily drawing the conclusion that a single negative event will happen over and over again.\nAnswer C: Personalization involves erroneously attributing external events to oneself."}, {"question": "In his research, a cognitive psychologist uses paired-associate and serial learning tasks. Apparently, this psychologist is investigating:", "options": {"A": "verbal learning", "B": "selective attention", "C": "procedural memory", "D": "iconic memory"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "A paired-associate task requires the individual to respond with one member of a pair when presented with the other member. A serial learning\ntask requires the individual to learn and recall a list of words in a particular order. These tasks are frequently used in the study of verbal\nlearning.\nAnswer B: Broadbent's (1958) \u0000lter theory of selective attention explains how information is transferred from sensory memory to short-term\nmemory.\nAnswer C: Procedural memory stores information about how to do things and is used to acquire, retain, and employ perceptual, cognitive, and\nmotor skills and habits.\nAnswer D: Iconic memory is a component of the visual memory system. It refers to sensory memory of visual images (i.e., icons)."}, {"question": "From the perspective of operant conditioning, which of the following techniques is most useful for combining component responses into a particular sequence?", "options": {"A": "Stimulus generalization", "B": "Response priming", "C": "Shaping", "D": "Chaining"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Translating this question into a less awkward statement might have made it easier to answer; e.g., \"Which operant technique is used to put\ntogether individual responses in a particular sequence?\" Chaining is used to combine a sequence of responses in order to end up with a\n\"behavior chain.\" Chaining is considered by advocates of operant conditioning as the mechanism underlying the acquisition of at least some\ncomplex behaviors.\nAnswer A: In classical conditioning, stimulus generalization occurs when an organism responds with the target response to stimuli similar to\nthe conditioned stimulus.\nAnswer B: Response priming refers to any procedure used to initiate the early steps in a sequence of responses. Although response priming\ncan be used to initiate a behavioral chain, this is not the best answer of those given.\nAnswer C: People often confuse shaping and chaining. Shaping is a procedure for teaching a new behavior in which closer and closer\napproximations to the desired behavior are reinforced. However, in shaping, the goal is the end behavior; in chaining, the whole string (chain) of\nbehaviors is important."}, {"question": "From the perspective of two-factor theory, the avoidance response in a phobic reaction is maintained by:", "options": {"A": "negative reinforcement", "B": "positive reinforcement", "C": "intermittent reinforcement", "D": "vicarious reinforcement"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Two-factor theory describes phobic reactions as involving both classical conditioning and negative reinforcement. Negative reinforcement is\noccurring when a response increases because an aversive stimulus is removed following the response. In phobic reactions, the avoidance\nresponse increases because, by avoiding the feared object or event, anxiety is reduced or eliminated.\nAnswer B: Positive reinforcement occurs when a behavior increases because of the stimulus that is applied (or added) following the behavior.\nThis is not a component of two-factor theory.\nAnswer C: Intermittent reinforcement refers to a schedule of reinforcement in which only some (not all) behaviors are reinforced.\nAnswer D: Vicarious reinforcement occurs when an observer sees a model being reinforced for engaging in the target behavior."}, {"question": "A research participant is given a list of unrelated words to remember. The list is taken away and the participant is engaged in a distracting task for 10 seconds before she is asked to recall the words in any order. The participant's recall will be best for:", "options": {"A": "words that were at the end of the list", "B": "words that were in the middle of the list", "C": "words that were in the beginning of the list", "D": "words that were in the beginning and middle of the list"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "This question is asking about the serial position effect. When there is a brief delay between exposure to the list and recall, words at the\nbeginning of the list are recalled best.\nAnswer A: When there is no delay between exposure to the list and recall, words at the beginning and end of a list are recalled the best and to\nabout the same degree. Since the question indicates that there is a delay between exposure to the list and the participant's recall of the list, the\nwords at the beginning of the list (not the end) will be remembered best.\nAnswer B: According to the serial position effect, words in the middle of the list will be most di\u0000cult to remember.\nAnswer D: When there is no delay between exposure to the list and recall, words at the beginning and end of a list are recalled the best and to\nabout the same degree. The serial position effect does not predict a situation where words at both the beginning and middle of the list will be\nbest recalled."}, {"question": "The \"FI scallop\" refers to:", "options": {"A": "the pause followed by accelerated responding that occurs within each \u0000xed interval", "B": "the increase in responding that occurs after reinforcement at the end of each \u0000xed interval", "C": "the gradual pause followed by an essential instantaneous high rate of responding that occurs between each \u0000xed interval", "D": "the pause in responding that signals satiation after exposure to an extended \u0000xed interval schedule"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The term \"scallop\" is ordinarily applied to the response pattern that is observed when an organism is being reinforced on a \u0000xed interval\nschedule. When using an FI schedule, the delivery of reinforcement is typically followed \u0000rst by a pause in responding and then an increasing\nrate of responding until the next reinforcement is delivered. On a cumulative record, this produces a \"scallop.\"\nAnswer B: The \"scallop\" effect describes a pause and increase in the rate of responding before (not after) the next reinforcement is delivered.\nAnswer C: Fixed interval schedules tend to produce low rates of responding, not high rates of responding. The \"FI scallop\" describes a pause\nfollowed by an acceleration in responding within each \u0000xed interval, but the acceleration is not instantaneous.\nAnswer D: Satiation typically occurs when using a continuous schedule of reinforcement; that is, when reinforcement is presented after each\nresponse. Satiation means the reinforcer has lost its reinforcing value."}, {"question": "A behaviorist would most likely argue that the use of spanking as a behavioral management technique is not effective because:", "options": {"A": "it leads to physical child abuse", "B": "it fosters a sense of learned helplessness in the child", "C": "changes in behavior aren't likely to persist over time", "D": "changes in behavior won't occur at all"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "From a behavioral perspective, punishments such as spanking are problematic in that they suppress but do not eliminate the behavior. Over\ntime, punishment alone is not likely to be effective, since it only results in a temporary suppression of behavior. In fact, as soon as the\npunishment is not present, the behavior is likely to increase.\nAnswer A: Though this may be true in some cases, it is an issue distinct from the effectiveness of spanking as a behavioral management\ntechnique (i.e., its effectiveness in reducing a particular behavior).\nAnswer B: While this may also be true in some cases, it is an issue distinct from the effectiveness of spanking as a behavioral management\ntechnique.\nAnswer D: Punishment usually does result in a decrease in the targeted behavior, though, as already noted, this increase is likely to be\ntemporary."}, {"question": "As de\u0000ned by Piaget, a __________ circular reaction occurs when a baby's action gets a pleasurable or interesting response from an object or other person, which then leads the baby to repeat the action.", "options": {"A": "re\u0000exive", "B": "primary", "C": "secondary", "D": "tertiary"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "According to Piaget, much of the cognitive development that occurs during the sensorimotor stage is the result of circular reactions, in which\nthe child learns to reproduce a pleasurable or interesting experience that originally occurred by chance. Piaget distinguished between three\ntypes of circular reactions - primary, secondary, and tertiary. Secondary circular reactions predominate from ages 4 to 8 months and are\nactions involving other people or objects. For example, at this stage, an infant may learn that grabbing a rattle with his/her hand and shaking it\nproduces an interesting noise.\nAnswer A: Piaget considered re\u0000exes to be the \"building blocks\" of cognitive development from birth to 1 month.\nAnswer B: Primary circular reactions predominate from ages 1 to 4 months. They involve simple motor habits that center around the baby's\nown body (e.g., thumb sucking).\nAnswer D: Tertiary circular reactions predominate from ages 12 to 18 months and involve varying an original action on an external object to see\nwhat happens."}, {"question": "Alex and Stuart are both men with depression. If Stuart has a strong identi\u0000cation with traditional male gender norms he is:", "options": {"A": "likely to prefer a male treatment provider", "B": "less likely to show an alexithymic response", "C": "equally likely to express symptoms as any other man", "D": "likely to be less effective in self-management of symptoms"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Seidler (2016) reviewed the research on male gender norms and depression and concluded that conformity to traditional male norms affects\nsymptom expression, attitudes toward help-seeking, and symptom management.[Seidler, Z. E., Dawes, A. J., Rice, S. M., Oliffe, J. L., & Dhillon, H.\nM. (2016). The role of masculinity in men's help-seeking for depression: A systematic review. Clinical Psychology Review, 49, 106118.].\nAnswer A: This has not been a focus of gender norm research.\nAnswer B: Depressed men with high conformity to traditional masculinity are more prone to be alexithymic, as they tend not to express their\nsymptoms.\nAnswer C: Research concluded that conformity to traditional male norms affects symptom expression, attitudes toward help-seeking, and\nsymptom management."}, {"question": "It is not until about _____ years of age that most children express more favorable attitudes toward members of their own ethnic or racial group than toward members of other groups.", "options": {"A": "4", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "10"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Early studies on in-group preferences found that children typically demonstrate preferences for members of their own ethnic/racial group and\ngender by about age 4 and 5, respectively. Clark and associates [Clark, A., Hocevar, D., and Dembo, M.H. (1980).The role of cognitive\ndevelopment in children's explanations and preferences for skin color. Developmental Psychology, 16(4), 332-339.] found that, by 4 years of\nage, children typically express more favorable attitudes toward members of their own ethnic group than members of other ethnic groups.\nAnswers B, C, and D: Early studies on in-group preferences found that children typically demonstrate preferences for members of their own\nethnic/racial group and gender by about age 4 and 5."}, {"question": "For children of divorced parents, increased frequency of contact with the noncustodial father:", "options": {"A": "is consistently associated with more behavior problems and lower academic achievement", "B": "is consistently associated with fewer behavior problems and better academic achievement", "C": "is associated with fewer behavior problems and better academic achievement only when the father is supportive and authoritative", "D": "is unrelated to severity of behavior problems or level of academic achievement regardless of the father's supportiveness and parenting style"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Hetherington found that frequency of contact with the noncustodial father itself was not predictive of child outcomes. However, a greater\nfrequency of contact was associated with better outcomes -- especially for boys -- when the father was supportive, authoritative in terms of\nparenting style, and did not frequently engage in open con\u0000ict with his ex-spouse in front of the child. [ E. M. Hetherington, E.M. (1993). An\noverview of the Virginia Longitudinal Study of divorce and remarriage with a focus on early adolescence. Journal of Family Psychology, 7(1),\n39-56].\nAnswers A, B, and D: For children of divorced parents, increased frequency of contact with the noncustodial father is associated with fewer\nbehavior problems and better academic achievement only when the father is supportive and authoritative."}, {"question": "Longitudinal research by Tizard and colleagues (e.g., Hodges and Tizard, 1989) indicates that infants initially raised in institutions are subsequently able to develop a close bond with their adoptive parents:", "options": {"A": "only if they are adopted by 12 months of age", "B": "only if they are adopted by 24 months of age", "C": "even if they are not adopted until 4 to 6 years of age", "D": "only if they had established a strong bond with a biological parent prior to being institutionalized"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Although these researchers found that children adopted between the ages of 4 and 6 (\"late adoptees) did develop strong bonds with their\nadoptive parents, their data also revealed that these children had higher-than-normal rates of emotional and social problems in childhood and\nadolescence, including an excessive need for adult attention and \"overfriendliness\" toward unfamiliar adults [Hodges, J. and Tizard, B. (1989).\nSocial and family relationships of ex-institutionalized adolescents, Journal of Child Psychology and Psychiatry, 30, 77-97].\nAnswers A, B, and D: Longitudinal research by Tizard and colleagues indicates that infants initially raised in institutions are subsequently able\nto develop a close bond with their adoptive parents even if they are not adopted until 4 to 6 years of age."}, {"question": "Taddio and colleagues (1997, 2005) examined pain perception in newborns and found that, when newborn boys were not given anesthesia during circumcision:", "options": {"A": "they showed no adverse reactions, suggesting that they did not experience pain", "B": "they showed an adverse reaction but exhibited no subsequent effects of this experience", "C": "they showed an adverse reaction followed by increased sensitivity to pain in subsequent months", "D": "they showed an adverse reaction followed by decreased sensitivity to pain in subsequent months"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Contrary to what was previously believed, research has con\u0000rmed that newborns are sensitive to pain. Taddio et al. found that newborns who\nwere not given anesthesia during circumcision displayed a more adverse reaction to a vaccination several months later than did those who\nwere given anesthesia. Other research has con\u0000rmed that exposure to painful medical procedures during early infancy can affect later pain\nperception, although the exact effects may depend on whether the infant was full-term or premature.\nAnswers A, B, and D: Research indicates when newborn boys were not given anesthesia during circumcision they showed an adverse reaction\nfollowed by increased sensitivity to pain in the subsequent months."}, {"question": "Research on attachment has shown that young victims of child abuse are most likely to exhibit which of the following attachment patterns?", "options": {"A": "Disengaged", "B": "Avoidant", "C": "Ambivalent", "D": "Disorganized"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Ainsworth distinguished between three attachment patterns (secure, insecure/ambivalent, and insecure/avoidant), and Main subsequently\nidenti\u0000ed the disorganized/disoriented pattern, \u0000nding that about 80% of maltreated children exhibit a disorganized/disoriented pattern of\nattachment. A baby with this type of attachment exhibits fear of his caregiver, dazed or confused facial expressions, and other\ndisorganized attachment behaviors (e.g., greeting his mother when she returns but then turning away from her).\nAnswers A, B, and C: Research on attachment has shown that young victims of child abuse most likely exhibit a disorganized attachment\npattern."}, {"question": "Studies investigating self-recognition in young children have found that by _______ months of age, over 60% of toddlers recognize their own image in a mirror.", "options": {"A": "21", "B": "18", "C": "15", "D": "12"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Researchers have used a number of techniques to investigate self-recognition in babies including mirrors, photos, and use of the words \"I\" and\n\"me.\" In a frequently cited study investigating the emergence of self-recognition, J. Brooks-Gunn and M. Lewis (1979) had mothers wipe the\nnoses of their babies, ages 9 to 24 months, with a red dye and then place the babies in front of a mirror. Results indicated that about 30% of the\n18-month-old toddlers recognized themselves in the mirror (i.e., they rubbed the red spot on their own noses when they saw their re\u0000ection),\nwhile 60% of the 21-month-old toddlers did so (Social cognition and the acquisition of self. New York: Plenum).\nAnswers B, C, and D: Studies investigating self-recognition in young children have found that by 21 months of age, over 60% of toddlers\nrecognize their own image in a mirror."}, {"question": "Kochanska and Knaack (2003) conclude that effortful (inhibitory) control is an important contributor to the early development of conscience. Their research found that effortful control becomes a stable, coherent trait by ________ months of age.", "options": {"A": "8 to 12", "B": "12 to 18", "C": "18 to 30", "D": "33 to 45"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Research on the development of conscience indicates that its precursors are apparent during the second and third years of life. G. Kochanska\nand A. Knaack found that effortful control is modestly coherent across tasks at age 22 months but does not become highly coherent (trait-like)\nuntil 33 months of age. They also found that children with higher effortful control at 22 to 45 months had stronger consciences at 56 months\nof age [Kochanska, G. and Knaack, A. (2003). Effortful control as a personality characteristic of young children: Antecedents, correlates, and\nconsequences. Journal of Personality, 71(6), 1087-1112].\nAnswers A, B, and C: Kochanska and Knaack's research found that effortful control becomes a stable, coherent trait by 33 to 45 months of age."}, {"question": "Egan and Perry's (2001) multidimensional model of gender identity consists of \u0000ve components: membership knowledge, gender typicality, gender contentedness, felt pressure, and:", "options": {"A": "intergroup bias", "B": "gender stability", "C": "integrative awareness", "D": "gender constancy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "This may have been one of those \"distant galaxy questions\" for you. If so, a good approach would have been to see if you could eliminate any\nanswers because you know they refer to a different theory. Doing so would increase your chance of choosing the correct answer. This answer\nlists the \u0000fth component identi\u0000ed by S. K. Egan and D. G. Perry [Egan, S.K. and D. G. Perry, D.G. (2001). Gender identity: A multidimensional\nanalysis with implications for psychosocial adjustment. Developmental Psychology, 37,451-463]. As de\u0000ned by these authors, membership\nknowledge refers to the individual's knowledge about his/her own gender; gender typicality is the degree to which the individual perceives\nhis/her characteristics to be similar to those of individuals of the same gender; gender contentedness is the extent to which the individual is\nsatis\u0000ed with his/her own gender; felt pressure is the degree to which the individual feels pressure to conform to gender group norms; and\nintergroup bias refers to the belief that one's same-sex group is superior to the other sex group.\nAnswer B: Gender stability is the second stage in Kohlberg's model of gender identity development.\nAnswer C: Integrative awareness is the \u0000nal stage of Atkinson, Morten, and Sue's (1993) Racial/Cultural Identity Development Model.\nAnswer D: Gender constancy is the third stage in Kohlberg's model of gender identity development."}, {"question": "Freud proposed that, during the ________ stage of psychosexual development, an individual experiences guilt as a result of the con\u0000ict between his or her unacceptable desires and a fear of being punished for having those desires.", "options": {"A": "anal", "B": "phallic", "C": "latency", "D": "genital"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "For the licensing exam, be familiar with the basic characteristics of each of Freud's stages of psychosexual development - oral, anal, phallic,\nlatency, and genital. During the phallic stage, libido is centered in the genitals and the primary task is resolution of the Oedipal or Electra\ncon\u0000ict, which is marked by a desire for the opposite-sex parent and a view of the same-sex parent as a rival. Feelings of guilt arise during this\nstage as the superego emerges and conscience develops.\nAnswer A: During the anal stage, libido (sexual energy) is centered in the anus and the primary task is control of bodily wastes.\nAnswer C: During the latency stage, libidinal energy is diffuse rather than on achieving sexual grati\u0000cation.\nAnswer D: During the genital stage, libido is again centered in the genitals but the successful outcome at this stage occurs when sexual desire\nis blended with affection to produce mature sexual relationships."}, {"question": "The effects of teratogens on the developing organism vary with the type of teratogen and the organ system. However, in general, these agents are most likely to cause severe structural damage:", "options": {"A": "during the \u0000rst eight weeks of development", "B": "during the 9th through 18th weeks of development", "C": "during the second trimester", "D": "during the third trimester"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The effects of teratogens on fetal development depend on several factors including the type and amount of the substance, the duration of\nexposure, the time of exposure, and the mother's physiological condition. Teratogens can have a negative effect on fetal development during\nthe entire pregnancy, but their consequences are much more severe during critical periods of development when the organ system is growing\nmost rapidly. The critical period varies from organ to organ but generally occurs within the \u0000rst eight weeks of development. (An exception is\nthe central nervous system, which has a critical period that extends into the 16th week of development.)\nAnswers B, C, and D: In general, teratogens are most likely to cause severe structural damage during the \u0000rst eight weeks of development."}, {"question": "The symptoms of fetal alcohol syndrome (FAS):", "options": {"A": "gradually decline during early childhood with the appropriate environmental stimulation", "B": "are largely reversible with the proper diet during the \u0000rst several months of life", "C": "are limited primarily to physical defects (facial abnormalities and retarded growth)", "D": "are largely irreversible and include physical, behavioral, and cognitive impairments"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Fetal alcohol syndrome is the most severe form of fetal alcohol spectrum disorder (FASD) and is characterized by a pattern of physical,\nbehavioral, and cognitive impairments that are associated with alcohol consumption by pregnant women. The severity and nature of the\nsymptoms are related to the amount of alcohol consumed by the mother during pregnancy. For most children, however, the symptoms are\nserious and are, to a large degree, irreversible.\nAnswer A: Although some of the facial deformities become less obvious with increasing age, most de\u0000cits are irreversible.\nAnswer B: Proper diet has not been found to have a signi\u0000cant effect on impairments due to fetal alcohol syndrome.\nAnswer C: Fetal alcohol syndrome also involves substantial cognitive and behavioral problems."}, {"question": "Klinefelter's syndrome is a genetic disorder that:", "options": {"A": "affects males and is caused by the presence of an extra X chromosome", "B": "affects males and is caused by the presence of an extra Y chromosome", "C": "affects females and is caused by the presence of an extra X chromosome", "D": "affects females and is caused by the presence of an extra Y chromosome"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Klinefelter's syndrome is one of the most common genetic abnormalities. A male with Klinefelter's syndrome has two or more X (female)\nchromosomes in addition to a single Y chromosome. Symptoms include small testicles, infertility, enlarged breast tissue, scant facial/body\nhair, and a decreased libido.\nAnswers B, C, and D: Klinefelter's syndrome is a genetic disorder that affects males and is caused by the presence of an extra X chromosome."}, {"question": "Kohlberg's cognitive-developmental theory distinguishes between three stages in gender identity development. The \u0000rst stage is ________, which involves recognizing one's own gender and is ordinarily \u0000rst apparent between 2 and 3 years of age.", "options": {"A": "gender constancy", "B": "gender conservation", "C": "gender identity", "D": "gender stability"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "According to Kohlberg's cognitive-developmental theory, the development of gender identity involves a sequence of three stages: gender\nidentity, gender stability, and gender constancy. Children begin to identify themselves (and others) as either a boy or girl at 2 to 3 years of age.\nThis is referred to as gender identity.\nAnswer A: Gender constancy does not develop until 6 to 7 years of age.\nAnswer B: Gender conservation is an alternative term for gender constancy.\nAnswer D: Gender stability is usually evident by 4 to 6 years of age."}, {"question": "According to Erikson's theory of psychosocial development, middle-aged adults are in which stage of development?", "options": {"A": "Ego integrity versus despair", "B": "Initiative versus guilt", "C": "Generativity versus stagnation", "D": "Industry versus inferiority"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Erikson's theory of psychosocial development includes eight stages. Generativity versus stagnation is the psychosocial con\u0000ict for middle-\naged adults, which is the second to last stage.\nAnswer A: Ego integrity versus despair is the \u0000nal psychosocial con\u0000ict.\nAnswer B: Initiative versus guilt is the con\u0000ict for children aged three through six.\nAnswer D: Industry versus inferiority is the con\u0000ict for those aged six to twelve."}, {"question": "Which of the developmental changes is most likely to occur in males between the ages of 40-45?", "options": {"A": "A decrease in work satisfaction", "B": "Signi\u0000cant personality change and emotional disturbance due to the \"mid-life\" crisis", "C": "A shift in perspective from \"time-since-birth\" to \"time-left-to-live\"", "D": "The Dream"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "According to Levinson, the mid-life transition, or the transition between early and middle adulthood, occurs between the ages of 40-45. As a\nresult of an increasing awareness of mortality, the mid-life transition is marked by a shift in awareness from \"time-since-birth\" to \"time-left-to-\nlive.\"\nAnswer A: Research suggests that middle-aged men (and women) are more satis\u0000ed with their work than younger adults.\nAnswer B: Contrary to popular belief, research suggests that mid-life is not a time of crisis for most people.\nAnswer D: The early adult transition leads to the formation of The Dream, which is an image of an ideal life that guides one’s decisions and\nchoices."}, {"question": "Solitary (non-social) play:", "options": {"A": "is the predominant form of play for children between one and three years of age", "B": "is useful as an indicator of cognitive development during the preschool years", "C": "may be indicative of developmental delays if it constitutes a signi\u0000cant portion of the child's play after age three", "D": "is not necessarily less mature than group play and, in some circumstances, may be associated with a high level of competence"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Researchers on children's play suggest that it is the quality, not the quantity, of solitary play that determines its signi\u0000cance. Nonsocial play is\nnot necessarily more immature than group play. In fact, because it often consists of goal-directed or educational activities or activities\ninvolving large-motor movements, it is often associated with high levels of competence.\nAnswers A, B, and C: Non-social play is not necessarily less mature than group play and, in some circumstances, may be associated with a high\nlevel of competence."}, {"question": "Research using elicited imitation tasks (e.g., imitating a sequence of events such as removing a mitten from a puppet, shaking the mitten, and then replacing the mitten on the puppet) indicate that, for most infants, the onset of the ability to recall the past occurs:", "options": {"A": "between 3 and 6 months of age", "B": "between 6 and 12 months of age", "C": "between 12 and 18 months of age", "D": "between 18 and 24 months of age"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "There is physiological and behavioral evidence that the neural mechanisms required for long-term memory recall undergo signi\u0000cant\ndevelopment during the second half of the \u0000rst year of life. In one study using the procedure described in this question, 75% of infants 6, 9, or\n11 months of age imitated at least one action (e.g., removing the mitten) after a delay of 24 hours [Barr, R. et al. (1996). Developmental\nchanges in deferred imitation by 6- to 24-month old infants. Infant Behavior and Development, 19, 159-170.]\nAnswers A, C, and D: Research using elicited imitation tasks indicate that, for most infants, the onset of the ability to recall the past\noccurs between 6 and 12 months of age."}, {"question": "Studies examining the consequences of divorce and maternal remarriage on children has revealed that boys and girls have di\u0000culty adjusting to a stepfather during the \u0000rst two years after remarriage and that:", "options": {"A": "both adapt thereafter and, in many cases, show better adjustment than peers whose mothers have not remarried", "B": "boys (but not girls) adapt thereafter and, in many cases, show better adjustment than male peers whose mothers have not remarried", "C": "girls (but not boys) adapt thereafter and, in many cases, show better adjustment than female peers whose mothers have not remarried", "D": "subsequently continue to be less well-adjusted than peers whose mothers have not remarried"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Boys adapt to stepfathers better than girls and often eventually show better adjustment than male peers whose mothers did not remarry [see,\ne.g., Hetherington, E.M. (1989). Coping with family transitions: Winners, losers, and survivors, Child Development, 60, 60-114].\nAnswers A, C, and D: Studies examining the consequences of divorce and maternal remarriage on children have revealed that boys (but not\ngirls) adapt thereafter and, in many cases, show better adjustment than male peers whose mothers have not remarried."}, {"question": "Gerald Patterson and his colleagues have developed a model to explain the origins of adolescent delinquency. Their approach is most consistent with which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Object relations theory", "B": "Social learning theory", "C": "Cognitive developmental theory", "D": "Social comparison theory"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "According to Patterson and his colleagues, adolescent delinquency is traceable to unskilled and ine\u0000cient childrearing by the child's parents,\nhigh levels of punishment, and a tendency to react to a child's negative actions in kind. Patterson's model emphasizes the impact of\nmodeling/imitation and reinforcement and, therefore, is consistent with a social learning approach.\nAnswers A, C, and D: Patterson and colleagues' approach to adolescent delinquency is most associated with social learning theory."}, {"question": "A woman is having trouble with her six-month old son who often \"fusses\" at bedtime and wakes up at least once or twice during the night. She does an Internet search on the problem and \u0000nds several articles about Dr. Richard Ferber. She learns that his method for getting babies to sleep (\"Ferberizing\") is supported by many experts and involves:", "options": {"A": "a \"progressive-waiting\" approach", "B": "therapeutic touch", "C": "a \"cold turkey\" approach", "D": "co-sleeping"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "When using Ferber's method, the parent puts the child to bed. If he cries, the parent returns and reassures the child and pats him on the back\nbut does not pick him up. If the child cries again later, the parent waits a little longer before returning. On subsequent nights, the wait period is\ngradually increased, which is why the method is known as the progressive waiting method. [Ferber, R. (2006). Solve Your Child's Sleep\nProblems. New York: Fireside]\nAnswers B, C, and D: The method Ferber utilizes to aid in getting babies to sleep is known as the \"progressive-waiting\" approach."}, {"question": "The \"storm and stress theory\" of adolescent mental health proposes that most adolescents experience extensive physical, social, and psychological turmoil. Research conducted in the past three or four decades on this topic:", "options": {"A": "con\u0000rms that most adolescents do experience substantial \"storm and stress\"", "B": "demonstrates that \"storm and stress\" is characteristic of adolescents in westernized cultures only", "C": "indicates that, for the large majority of individuals, adolescence is not a time of signi\u0000cant \"storm and stress\"", "D": "shows that \"storm and stress\" is largely due to increases in family con\u0000icts during the adolescent period"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "While adolescents may exhibit greater emotional lability, only about 10 to 20% of adolescents exhibit some type of severe emotional turmoil,\nwhich is approximately the same percentage as found in the adult population.\nAnswer A: Research conducted in the past few decades has largely refuted Hall's (1904) proposition that adolescence is a time of substantial\n\"storm and stress.\"\nAnswer B: Incorrect. This is not shown to be true of adolescents in westernized cultures, or any other for that matter.\nAnswer D: In fact, contrary to the popular view of adolescence, the research suggests that family con\u0000icts do not actually increase\nsubstantially during this period."}, {"question": "The age of onset of separation anxiety varies from child to child but is usually between six and eight months of age. It then peaks in intensity at about ______ months and thereafter declines.", "options": {"A": "10 to 14", "B": "14 to 18", "C": "18 to 24", "D": "24 to 28"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The age at which developmental milestones are reached vary considerably from child to child, so it is di\u0000cult to make generalizations. Most\nauthorities list it as beginning at about 6 to 8 months of age. The intensity of separation anxiety continues to increase during the \u0000rst half of\nthe second year (14 to 18 months) and subsequently declines.\nAnswers A, C, and D: The age at which separation anxiety peaks in intensity is at about 14 to 18 months and thereafter declines."}, {"question": "Freud's psychosexual theory of development is based on the premise that in each stage:", "options": {"A": "efforts to obtain pleasure center on a different part of the body", "B": "a different psychological need is \"prepotent\"", "C": "the source of anxiety is related to a different psychosocial con\u0000ict", "D": "the source of anxiety is related to a different psychic need"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Freud's psychosexual theory emphasizes the impact of the sex drive (libido) on development. In each stage of development, a different part of\nthe body is most sensitive to erotic stimulation.\nAnswers B, C, and D: Freud's psychosexual theory of development is based on the premise that in each stage efforts to obtain pleasure center\non a different part of the body."}, {"question": "Based on her study of preschoolers and their parents, Diana Baumrind (1967, 1971) concluded that children of __________ parents tend to be aggressive, mistrusting, dependent, and \"con\u0000icted-irritable.\"", "options": {"A": "instrumental", "B": "permissive", "C": "authoritative", "D": "authoritarian"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Baumrind originally categorized parents as authoritarian, authoritative, permissive, or rejecting-neglecting. Baumrind's research linked\nauthoritarian parenting with children who are \"con\u0000icted-irritable\" - i.e., fearful, moody, easily annoyed, and aimless.\nAnswer A: This is not one of the categories identi\u0000ed by Baumrind.\nAnswer B: Baumrind found that children of permissive parents tend to be \"impulsive-aggressive.\"\nAnswer C: Baumrind found that children of authoritative parents are most often \"energetic-friendly.\""}, {"question": "From the perspective of Vygotsky's sociocultural theory, \"private speech\" is:", "options": {"A": "a manifestation of a young child's egocentrism", "B": "self-directed speech that guides the child's behavior", "C": "evidence that language acquisition is a creative (rather than imitative) process", "D": "unrelated to cognitive development"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Vygotsky believed that private (self-directed) speech is essential for cognitive development and is a source of self-guidance for young children.\nThis refers to a preschool child's talking aloud to him/herself while engaging in daily activities.\nAnswer A: This is Piaget's interpretation of private speech.\nAnswer C: This describes \u0000gurative speech (the deliberate use of words in a \u0000gurative, rather than literal, way).\nAnswer D: Vygotsky believed that self-directed speech is essential for cognitive development."}, {"question": "Research on infantile amnesia has shown that:", "options": {"A": "children do not develop memories for autobiographical events until age 3 or 4", "B": "the loss of early memories is universal and is unrelated to gender, culture, or type of event", "C": "memories for autobiographical events that occurred prior to age 3 or 4 remain stable over the lifespan", "D": "the ability to recall memories for autobiographical events that occurred prior to age 3 or 4 varies over the lifespan"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Infantile amnesia refers to a person's inability to recall events that occurred prior to about age 3 or 4. While early research suggested that the\nloss of early memories is absolute, subsequent studies indicate that many older children, adolescents, and adults are able to recall some\nevents that took place prior to age 3 or 4. In addition, the recall of early memories varies across the lifespan. Rybash and Hrubi, for example,\nfound that older adults typically have later-occurring \u0000rst memories than younger adults do [ Rybash, J.M. and Hrubi, K.L. (1997). Psychometric\nand psychodynamic correlates of \u0000rst memories in younger and older adults, Gerontologist, 37, 581-587].\nAnswer A: Children under the age of 3 do develop memories for autobiographical events, but many of these memories are \"lost\" in subsequent\nyears.\nAnswer B: There is evidence that the loss of memory is, in fact, related to gender, cultural background, type of event, and other factors.\nAnswer C: Research on infantile amnesia has actually shown that the ability to recall memories for autobiographical events that occurred prior\nto age 3 or 4 varies over the lifespan."}, {"question": "In general, peer pressure reaches its peak in intensity during:", "options": {"A": "preadolescence (ages 10 to 13)", "B": "early adolescence (ages 13 to 15)", "C": "middle adolescence (ages 15 to 17)", "D": "late adolescence (ages 17 to 19)"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Peer pressure increases during preadolescence, peaks during early adolescence (ages 13 to 15), and then gradually declines.\nAnswers A, C, and D: Peer pressure reaches its peak in intensity during ages 13 to 15."}, {"question": "A middle-aged man sustained a traumatic brain injury from a motor vehicle accident. He presents with mild impairments in judgment, insight, and planning and reduced sexual interest. Damage to which of the following areas of the brain is most likely responsible for these de\u0000cits?", "options": {"A": "dorsolateral prefrontal region", "B": "orbitofrontal region", "C": "medial temporal region", "D": "posterior parietal region"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Although traumatic brain injury can produce diffuse brain injury, the frontal and temporal lobes are the areas of the cortex that are usually most\nadversely affected. The dorsolateral prefrontal region mediates executive cognitive functions. Damage to this area is associated with de\u0000cits in\nattention, planning, problem-solving, and other higher-order cognitive abilities as well as reduced sexual interest and apathy.\nAnswer B: The orbitofrontal region is involved in inhibition. Damage to this area may produce disinhibition, impaired social insight, and\ninappropriate affect.\nAnswer C: The temporal lobes are involved in learning and memory, and damage to the medial temporal lobes along with certain adjacent\nstructures can produce amnesia.\nAnswer D: The posterior parietal region is a major sensorimotor area of the brain, and damage can produce apraxia or contralateral neglect."}, {"question": "_______ is an entirely novel word, sometimes produced by someone with aphasia.", "options": {"A": "Neologism", "B": "Paraphasia", "C": "Syntax", "D": "Alexia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "A neologism is an entirely novel word that may be generated by the substitution of a phoneme.\nAnswer B: Paraphasia is a feature of aphasia in which an individual loses the ability to speak correctly, substitutes one word for another, and\ninappropriately changes words and sentences.\nAnswer C: Syntax is the arrangement of words and phrases to create well-formed sentences in a language.\nAnswer D: Alexia is the inability to read."}, {"question": "Damage to the right hemisphere may include all of the following except _______.", "options": {"A": "aphasia", "B": "left-side hemi neglect", "C": "impulsivity and disinhibition", "D": "visual-perceptual disturbances"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Aphasia typically results in damage to the left hemisphere which is responsible for language and motor activities.\nAnswer B: This is consistent with damage to the right hemisphere due to contralateralization.\nAnswer C: Affective abnormalities are common with right hemisphere damage. Individuals may experience indifference, euphoria, mania,\ndisinhibition, and abnormal sexual behavior.\nAnswer D: The right hemisphere is associated with perceptual and visuospatial activities, maintenance of body image, and processing visual,\nfacial, and verbal emotions."}, {"question": "Which of the following describes the perception of the body's own movements?", "options": {"A": "organic sense", "B": "cutaneous sense", "C": "kinesthesia", "D": "functionalism"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Kinesthesia is the ability to sense the motion of a joint or limb. This sense is primarily in\u0000uenced by muscle spindles and is secondarily\nin\u0000uenced by skin receptors and joint receptors.\nAnswer A: Organic sense is a sense modality that arises from receptors located within the inner organs of the body.\nAnswer B: Cutaneous sense is one of the somatosenses that includes sensitivity to stimuli that involve the skin.\nAnswer D: Functionalism is the principle that the best way to understand a biological phenomenon is to understand its useful functions for the\norganism."}, {"question": "The developmental stage in which cells acquire distinctive characteristics, such as those of neurons, as the result of expressing particular genes is called _______.", "options": {"A": "DNA sequencing", "B": "cell differentiation", "C": "translation", "D": "transcription"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Cell differentiation is the process by which dividing cells change their functional or phenotypical type.\nAnswer A: DNA sequencing is the process by which the order of nucleotides in a gene, or amino acids in a protein, is determined.\nAnswer C: Translation is the process by which amino acids are linked together (directed by an mRNA molecule) to form protein molecules.\nAnswer D: Transcription is the process during which mRNA forms bases complementary to a strand of DNA. The resulting message (called a\ntranscript) is then used to translate the DNA code into protein molecules."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a common symptom of early stages multiple sclerosis?", "options": {"A": "akinesia", "B": "akathisia", "C": "ataxia", "D": "athetosis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Ataxia involves a loss of balance and coordination and is a symptom of a number of disorders including multiple sclerosis, cerebral palsy,\nspinal injury, and stroke. It can be caused by damage to the cerebellum, dorsal spinal cord, or vestibular system. Although its symptoms\ndepend on the area affected, they often include unsteady gait, di\u0000culty with \u0000ne motor tasks, abnormal eye movements, slurred speech, and\ndi\u0000culty swallowing.\nAnswer A: Akinesia is a complete or almost complete loss of movement and is associated with advanced, or \u0000nal stage multiple sclerosis.\nAnswer B: Akathisia is an uncomfortable sense of restlessness or agitation.\nAnswer D: Athetosis involves slow writhing involuntary movements."}, {"question": "Zachary is a 15-year-old male with Fragile-X syndrome due to a mutation in the FMR-1 gene causing a CGG repeat. Zachary's face is long and narrow, with a prominent forehead and large ears. He has a below average IQ, experiences social di\u0000culties, repetitive behaviors, and tactile defensiveness. Which of the following is not considered a phenotype of Fragile-X syndrome?", "options": {"A": "long and narrow facial features with large ears", "B": "enlarged testicles, \u0000at feet, and unusually \u0000exible \u0000ngers", "C": "mutation in the FMR-1 gene", "D": "hand \u0000apping and hyperarousal to sensory stimuli"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "To answer this question, you need to know the difference between a genotype and a phenotype. It is less important to know the speci\u0000c\nsymptoms or features of Fragile-X syndrome. A phenotype is the physical or observable expression of an individual's genotype. All the answers\nlisted are considered physical and behavioral phenotypes of Fragile-X syndrome except one. A mutation in the FMR-1 gene or CGG repeat is a\ngenotype as it is the genetic material of Fragile-X syndrome.\nAnswer A: This is considered a phenotype as they are all physical features of Fragile-X syndrome.\nAnswer B: These are also physical features of Fragile-X syndrome and are considered part of the phenotype.\nAnswer D: This is considered a behavioral phenotype. Individuals with Fragile-X syndrome may display symptoms of Autism Spectrum\nDisorder."}, {"question": "Agranulocytosis is a potential side effect of clozapine, carbamazepine, and a number of other psychiatric drugs. Which of the following are early symptoms of agranulocytosis?", "options": {"A": "fever, sore throat, mouth ulcers, and lethargy", "B": "nausea, diarrhea, vomiting, and a metallic taste in the mouth", "C": "sweating, palpitations, headache, tremulousness, and cardiac arrhythmia", "D": "constricted pupils, decreased visual acuity, sweating, constipation, and nausea"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Agranulocytosis is caused by a failure of the bone marrow to produce a su\u0000cient number of certain white blood cells, which increases the\nbody's susceptibility to infection. Fever, sore throat, chills, mouth ulcers, lethargy, and weakness are early signs of agranulocytosis.\nAnswer B: These are potential gastrointestinal side effects of lithium.\nAnswer C: These symptoms may occur when a beta-blocker is abruptly discontinued.\nAnswer D: These are signs of narcotic-analgesic use."}, {"question": "Which syndrome is characterized by the lack of developed ovaries but otherwise normal female sex organs and genitalia?", "options": {"A": "androgen insensitivity syndrome", "B": "Turner's syndrome", "C": "persistent Mllerian duct syndrome", "D": "congenital adrenal hyperplasia"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Individuals with Turner's syndrome have only one sex chromosome (an X chromosome). Those with Turner's syndrome have no gonads at all,\nthey develop into females with normal female internal sex organs and external genitalia.\nAnswer A: Androgen insensitivity syndrome is a condition caused by a congenital lack of functioning androgen receptors in a person with XY\nsex chromosomes, which causes the development of a female with testes but no internal sex organs.\nAnswer C: Persistent Mullerian duct syndrome is a condition caused by a congenital lack of anti-Mullerian hormone or receptors for this\nhormone. In a male, this causes the development of both male and female internal sex organs.\nAnswer D: Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is a condition characterized by hypersecretion of androgens by the adrenal cortex. In females,\nthis causes masculinization of the external genitalia."}, {"question": "A 9-year-old female with Attention De\u0000cit Hyperactivity Disorder, Combined type and Autism Spectrum Disorder is prescribed Strattera. Which of the following is least likely to occur?", "options": {"A": "a paradoxical effect", "B": "increased risk of suicidal thoughts", "C": "therapeutic response within 4 weeks", "D": "increase of norepinephrine and dopamine"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Of the answer options listed, a paradoxical effect is least likely to occur. A paradoxical effect occurs when a medication causes side effects of\noutcomes opposite of the intended outcome. Although, paradoxical effects are observed in people with ADHD, it is more commonly associated\nwith amphetamines or stimulant medications and benzodiazepines. Strattera is a non-stimulant medication for ADHD.\nAnswer B: Strattera does come with a warning that children and adolescents are at increased rate of experiencing suicidal thoughts when\ntaking this medication.\nAnswer C: Unlike stimulant medications, Strattera can take 2-8 weeks to achieve maximum therapeutic effects.\nAnswer D: Strattera's mechanism of action occurs through blocking reuptake of norepinephrine and dopamine, resulting in an increase in levels\nof dopamine and norepinephrine."}, {"question": "_______ occurs when two or more action potentials arrive in rapid succession along a single presynaptic neuron.", "options": {"A": "Depolarization", "B": "Spatial summation", "C": "Polarization", "D": "Temporal summation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Temporal summation occurs when one presynaptic neuron releases neurotransmitters many times over some time.\nAnswer A: Depolarization is a change in the difference between the electric charge on the inside and the outside of the cell membrane.\nAnswer B: Spatial summation is the effect of triggering an action potential in a neuron from one or more presynaptic neurons.\nAnswer C: When a neuron is not stimulated, its membrane is polarized. Being polarized means that the electrical charge on the outside of the\nmembrane is positive while the electrical charge on the inside of the membrane is negative."}, {"question": "_______ have a dual function. They produce ova or sperms, and they secrete hormones.", "options": {"A": "Gonads", "B": "Gametes", "C": "Testes", "D": "Genes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Gonads are ovaries or testes. They have a dual function. Gonads are organs responsible for producing the sperm and ova, but they also secrete\nhormones and are considered to be endocrine glands.\nAnswer B: Gametes are mature reproductive cells. The production of gametes entails a special form of cell division.\nAnswer C: The testes are a pair of sperm-producing organs that maintain the health of the male reproductive system.\nAnswer D: A gene is the basic physical and functional unit of heredity and is made up of DNA."}, {"question": "Global aphasia involves _______.", "options": {"A": "intact comprehension, non-\u0000uent speech, dysarthria, anomia and de\u0000cits in repetition", "B": "impaired comprehension, non-\u0000uent speech, and de\u0000cits in naming and repetition", "C": "impaired comprehension, \u0000uent speech, anosognosia, and paraphasia", "D": "intact comprehension, \u0000uent speech, paraphasia, and de\u0000cits in repetition"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "As its name suggests, global aphasia is caused by widespread brain injury and is characterized by extensive disruption in the ability to produce\nand understand language. Impaired comprehension, impaired speech, anomia (inability to name a common or familiar object, attribute, or\naction), and paraphasia (substitution of a word with a sound or incorrect word) are symptoms of this type of aphasia.\nAnswer A: These symptoms are characteristic of Broca's aphasia.\nAnswer C: These symptoms are characteristic of Wernicke's aphasia.\nAnswer D: These symptoms are characteristic of conduction aphasia."}, {"question": "The cell bodies of the sensory neurons are in clusters of neurons outside the spinal cord called the ________.", "options": {"A": "dorsal root ganglia", "B": "spinal cord", "C": "medulla", "D": "brainstem"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The dorsal root ganglia contain the cell bodies of sensory neurons that bring information from the periphery to the spinal cord.\nAnswer B: The spinal cord is the pathway for messages sent by the brain to the body and from the body to the brain.\nAnswer C: The medulla is a section of the brain which is responsible for automatic functions like breathing, blood pressure, digestion, and\ncirculation.\nAnswer D: The brainstem has many basic functions including, regulation of heart rate, breathing, sleeping, and eating. All information from the\nbody to the cerebrum and cerebellum and vice versa must go through the brainstem."}, {"question": "Which of the following structures is involved in receiving sensory input including information about the presence of odors and pheromones?", "options": {"A": "amygdala", "B": "hypoglossal nerve", "C": "lateral sulcus", "D": "central nucleus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The amygdala includes several subnuclei involved in receiving sensory input, including information about the presence of odors and\npheromones, and relays it to the medial basal forebrain and hypothalamus. The amygdala plays a role in odor-driven behavioral responses and\nis often activated by unpleasant or pleasant odors.\nAnswer B: The hypoglossal nerve or cranial nerve XII originates in the medulla and damage to this nerve can cause problems with articulation\nsimilar to symptoms of Broca's aphasia.\nAnswer C: The lateral sulcus is the \u0000ssure separating the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes.\nAnswer D: The central nucleus, a region of the amygdala, receives information from the basal, lateral, and accessory basal nuclei and sends\nprojections to a wide variety of regions in the brain. It is involved in emotional responses."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a drug that blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors?", "options": {"A": "atropine", "B": "neostigmine", "C": "albumin", "D": "hemicholinium"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Atropine is a natural alkaloid anticholinergic agent that has potent antimuscarinic effects and is used to treat symptomatic bradycardia.\nAnswer B: Neostigmine is a drug that inhibits that activity of acetylcholinesterase.\nAnswer C: Albumin is a protein found in the blood that transports free fatty acids and can bind with some lipid-soluble drugs.\nAnswer D: Hemicholinium is a drug that inhibits the uptake of choline."}, {"question": "The aspect of implicit memory known as procedural memory is mediated primarily by which brain structure?", "options": {"A": "hippocampus", "B": "thalamus", "C": "amygdala", "D": "striatum"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Implicit memories are retrieved or recalled without conscious awareness. Several types of implicit memories are distinguished including\nprocedural memories (i.e., memories for skills and procedures) and associative memories (i.e., memories that result from classical\nconditioning). Implicit memory has been linked to several areas of the brain including the striatum, amygdala, and cerebellum. The dorsolateral\nstriatum is part of the basal ganglia and plays an important role in habit acquisition and procedural memory.\nAnswer A: The hippocampus is involved in the processing and consolidation of explicit memories.\nAnswer B: The thalamus also plays a role in explicit memory.\nAnswer C: The amygdala is linked to implicit memories and integrates sensory input to determine the level of threat of a stimulus. More\nspeci\u0000cally, it attaches emotional signi\u0000cances to memories and plays a major role in fear responses."}, {"question": "A woman in menopause is undergoing hormone replacement therapy with estrogen. Which of the following is she most likely to experience as a result of her treatment?", "options": {"A": "decrease in mood lability and increased risk for bone loss", "B": "decreased risk of stroke and blood clots", "C": "decrease in hot \u0000ashes, night sweats, and mood lability", "D": "reduction in vaginal dryness and improvement in sex drive"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) is used to offset or reduce the negative effects associated with menopause. Some treatments involve\naltering estrogen levels only, while others involve altering both estrogen and progesterone levels. HRT tends to be effective for eliminating hot\n\u0000ashes, mood swings, and vaginal dryness.\nAnswer A: Although HRT has demonstrated effectiveness for decreasing mood lability, it does not increase risk for bone loss. It actually\nreduces risk for bone loss.\nAnswer B: Research has shown that HRT can increase risk of stroke, blood clots, and breast cancer.\nAnswer D: Although HRT does reduce vaginal dryness, there is no clear evidence that estrogen improves sex drive."}, {"question": "________ is reduced visual acuity that is not caused by optical or retinal impairments.", "options": {"A": "Glaucoma", "B": "Myopia", "C": "Amblyopia", "D": "Retinopathy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Amblyopia occurs when acuity is poor in one eye, even though the eye and retina are normal.\nAnswer A: Glaucoma is a group of eye conditions that damage the optic nerve, the health of which is vital for good vision. This damage is often\ncaused by abnormally high pressure in your eye. It is one of the leading causes of blindness for people over the age of 60.\nAnswer B: Myopia is also called nearsightedness. It is the inability to focus the retinal image of objects that are far away.\nAnswer D: Retinopathy is any damage to the retina of the eyes, which may cause vision impairment. This refers to retinal vascular disease or\ndamage to the retina caused by abnormal blood \u0000ow."}, {"question": "A patient who has recently been prescribed \u0000uoxetine as a treatment for depression _______.", "options": {"A": "must avoid tyramine-rich foods", "B": "should be advised that they will temporarily experience dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention, and blurred vision", "C": "should be warned that sleep and anxiety problems may temporarily increase", "D": "should be warned that they may temporarily experience some mild cognitive impairment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Overall, Prozac (\u0000uoxetine) and the other SSRIs are associated with fewer side effects than the tricyclics. One disadvantage of the SSRI's is that\nthey may temporarily exacerbate sleep and anxiety problems.\nAnswer A: This is true about the MAOIs, not the SSRIs.\nAnswer B: These are side effects of the tricyclics.\nAnswer D: Cognitive impairment is more likely with a tricyclic."}, {"question": "Amitriptyline is often used as a treatment for neuropathic pain. Through which of the following mechanisms of action does it exert its effects?", "options": {"A": "increases norepinephrine and serotonin levels", "B": "increases norepinephrine levels and decreases serotonin levels", "C": "decreases serotonin and dopamine levels", "D": "increases serotonin levels and decreases dopamine levels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Amitriptyline is a tricyclic antidepressant that increases the levels of both norepinephrine and serotonin by inhibiting their reuptake.\nAnswer B: While it does increase norepinephrine through inhibiting its reuptake, Amitriptyline also increases serotonin levels through the same\nmechanism of action.\nAnswer C: Amitriptyline increases serotonin levels in the brain and some evidence suggests it is also a weak dopamine antagonist resulting in\na dopamine increase.\nAnswer D: While Amitriptyline increases serotonin levels through inhibiting its reuptake, it may also increase dopamine through a similar\nmechanism of action."}, {"question": "Which of the following functional abilities and/or de\u0000cits will someone with transcortical aphasia most likely experience?", "options": {"A": "communicates verbally but will have de\u0000cits in comprehension", "B": "intact language comprehension but exhibits a number of grammatical errors and labored speech", "C": "signi\u0000cant di\u0000culties in both producing and comprehending speech", "D": "intact language comprehension and can carry out commands, but unable to repeat verbal phrases"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Transcortical aphasia results when the major speech areas in the left (dominant) hemisphere become isolated from other cortical areas due to\na loss of blood supply. A person with transcortical aphasia is unable to verbally describe what they see or desire and also has de\u0000cits in\nlanguage comprehension. However, the person may be able to generate automatic-like responses (e.g., singing) and be able to repeat\nstatements made by others. When the damage is limited to certain areas of the brain, the individual may exhibit a more limited type of\ntranscortical aphasia - i.e., transcortical motor aphasia (intact comprehension with di\u0000culties in producing speech) or transcortical sensory\naphasia (di\u0000culties in comprehension only).\nAnswer A: This is characteristic of someone with Wernicke's aphasia.\nAnswer B: This is consistent with someone experiencing Broca's aphasia.\nAnswer D: These are characteristics of conduction aphasia."}, {"question": "_______ is associated with the emergence or re-emergence of symptoms that were absent or controlled while taking medication but appear when the same medication is discontinued or reduced in dosage.", "options": {"A": "Therapeutic effect", "B": "Side effect", "C": "Therapeutic index", "D": "Rebound effect"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "A rebound effect occurs if the condition the drug was used to treat re-emerges stronger when the drug is discontinued or losses its\neffectiveness.\nAnswer A: Therapeutic effects refer to the response after a treatment of any kind, the results of which are judged to be useful or favorable.\nAnswer B: Side effects, also known as adverse events, are unwanted or unexpected events or reactions to a drug. Side effects can vary from\nminor problems like a runny nose to life-threatening events, such as a heart attack.\nAnswer C: The therapeutic index is used to compare the therapeutically effective dose to the toxic dose of a pharmaceutical agent."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a class of light-sensitive receptor cells in the retina that are most active at low levels of light?", "options": {"A": "lens", "B": "retina", "C": "cone", "D": "rod"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Rods are a type of photoreceptor cell in the retina. They are sensitive to light levels and help with vision in low light. They are concentrated in\nthe outer areas of the retina.\nAnswer A: The lens is a structure in the eye that helps focus an image on the retina.\nAnswer B: The retina is the receptive surface inside the eye that contains photoreceptors and other neurons.\nAnswer C: Cones are a class of photoreceptor cells in the retina that are responsible for color vision."}, {"question": "Which of the following is least likely to occur in someone with retrograde amnesia?", "options": {"A": "They can recall semantic information better than episodic information.", "B": "They have di\u0000culty forming new memories.", "C": "Their recent memories tend to be more affected than their remote memories.", "D": "Their events from childhood and adolescence are typically intact."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "All of the answers are consistent with someone experiencing retrograde amnesia except di\u0000culty with forming new memories, which is\ncharacteristic of anterograde amnesia.\nAnswer A: Individuals with retrograde amnesia tend to recall semantic memories or factual knowledge better than autobiographical or episodic\ninformation.\nAnswer C: Even though individuals with retrograde amnesia experience a loss of pre-existing memories, it is usually temporally graded (i.e.,\nrecent memories tend to be more impacted than remote memories).\nAnswer D: Individuals experiencing retrograde amnesia tend to recall events from childhood and adolescence as the nature of memory loss is\ntemporally graded (i.e., recent memories are more affected than remote memories)."}, {"question": "According to the _______________, \"successful intelligence\" is the ability to adapt to, modify, and choose environments that accomplish one's goals and the goals of society and proposes that it is composed of three abilities - analytical, creative, and practical.", "options": {"A": "triarchic theory", "B": "theory of multiple intelligences", "C": "Carroll's Three-Stratum theory", "D": "Spearman's Two-Factor theory"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Sternberg's triarchic theory de\u0000nes successful intelligence as the ability to adapt to, modify, and choose environments that accomplish one's\ngoals and the goals of society. According to Sternberg, traditional intelligence tests focus on analytical ability but neglect creative and practical\nabilities.\nAnswer B: Gardner's theory of multiple intelligences distinguishes between eight types of cognitive ability, which include linguistic, musical,\nlogical-mathematical, spatial, bodily-kinesthetic, interpersonal, intrapersonal, and naturalistic.\nAnswer C: Carroll's Three-Stratum theory distinguishes between three levels (strata) of intelligence. Stratum III is general intelligence; stratum II\nconsists of eight broad abilities including, \u0000uid intelligence, crystallized intelligence, and general memory; and stratum I consist of speci\u0000c\nabilities that are each linked to one of the second stratum abilities.\nAnswer D: Spearman two-factor theory proposes a general intellectual factor (g) and argued that performance on any cognitive task depends\non g plus one or more speci\u0000c factors (s) unique to the task."}, {"question": "The impact of heredity on intelligence can be expressed in terms of the _________, which indicates the proportion of variability in intelligence that is due to inherited factors.", "options": {"A": "Flynn effect", "B": "heritability estimate", "C": "con\u0000uence model", "D": "cross-sectional design"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The impact of heredity on intelligence can be expressed in terms of a heritability estimate, which indicates the proportion of variability in\nintelligence that is due to inherited factors versus the environment. These estimates range from 0 to 1, with 0 indicating that genetics is not a\ncontributing factor.\nAnswer A: The Flynn effect refers to the observed rise in intelligence over time in standardized intelligence test scores. This is not explained by\ngenetics since it involved changes over generations and is, therefore, believed to be the result of environmental factors.\nAnswer C: The con\u0000uence model explains the \u0000rstborn child's advantage in terms of the changing intellectual environment in the family.\nAnswer D: A research design where individuals, typically of different ages or developmental levels, are compared at a single point in time is\ntermed a cross-sectional design."}, {"question": "On the WAIS-IV, a Full Scale IQ score of 85 is ________ below the mean.", "options": {"A": "one standard deviation", "B": "one and one-half standard deviations", "C": "two standard deviations", "D": "three standard deviations"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "This is an easy question as long as you know that the WAIS-IV Full Scale IQ has a mean of 100 and standard deviation of 15. On the WAIS-IV, a\nscore of 85 is one standard deviation below the mean (100 - 15 = 85).\nAnswer B: A Full Scale IQ score of 85 is one standard deviation below the mean. One and one-half standard deviations below the mean would\nbe a score of 77.5.\nAnswer C: A Full Scale IQ score of 85 is one standard deviation below the mean. Two standard deviations below the mean would be a score of\n70.\nAnswer D: A Full Scale IQ score of 85 is one standard deviation below the mean. Three standard deviations below the mean would be a score\nof 55."}, {"question": "Which of the following is an assessment that was developed as a measure of cognitive recovery during the \u0000rst weeks to months following a head injury?", "options": {"A": "Glasgow Coma Scale", "B": "Tower of London", "C": "Rancho Scale of Cognitive Functioning-Revised", "D": "Stroop Color-Word Association Test"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The Rancho Scale of Cognitive Functioning-Revised is a measure used during the \u0000rst weeks to months following a head injury. The patient is\nrated in terms of ten levels.\nAnswer A: The Glasgow Coma Scale is used to assess levels of consciousness following a brain injury and involves rating the patient in terms\nof three responses: visual response, best motor response, and best verbal response.\nAnswer B: The Tower of London is a variation of the Tower of Hanoi puzzle and measures attention, memory, and executive functioning. Poor\nperformance has been linked to frontal lobe damage, ADHD, autism, and depression.\nAnswer D: The Stroop Color-Word Association Test assesses the degree to which an examinee can suppress a prepotent (habitual) response in\nfavor of an unusual one and measures cognitive \u0000exibility, selective attention, and response inhibition. The Stroop is sensitive to frontal lobe\ndamage, and poor performance has been associated with ADHD, mania, depression, and schizophrenia."}, {"question": "In the Seattle Longitudinal Study, K. W. Schaie (1996) used which of the following research designs to investigate the relationship between age and intelligence?", "options": {"A": "counterbalanced", "B": "crossover", "C": "cross-sectional", "D": "cross-sequential"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to be familiar with the methodology and major results of the Seattle Longitudinal Study. To reduce problems associated\nwith longitudinal and cross-sectional research (e.g., dropouts, cohort effects), Schaie used a cross-sequential design that combined the two\nstrategies. The results of his research identi\u0000ed predictable changes (increases or decreases) in speci\u0000c cognitive abilities during adulthood\nand indicated that the range of change for each ability is fairly narrow until age 60 or later.\nAnswer A: A cross-sequential approach was used in the Seattle Longitudinal Study.\nAnswer B: A cross-sequential approach was used in the Seattle Longitudinal Study.\nAnswer C: In the Seattle Longitudinal Study, a cross-sequential approach was used, combining a cross-sectional approach with a longitudinal\napproach, to minimize the confounding effects that typically result from both types of the latter approaches independently."}, {"question": "The _________ is used to evaluate personal and social skills of children and adults with intellectual disability, autism spectrum disorder, ADHD, brain injury, or dementia and to assist in the development of educational and treatment plans.", "options": {"A": "AAMR Adaptive Behavior Scales", "B": "Vanderbilt Assessment Scales", "C": "Scales of Independent Behavior-Revised (SIB-R)", "D": "Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales-2nd Edition"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The VABS-II is used to evaluate the personal and social skills of individuals from birth to age 90. This test assesses adaptive behavior in four\ndomains: communication, daily living skills, socialization, and motor skills.\nAnswer A: The Adaptive Behavior Scales assess adaptive functioning in \u0000ve areas including personal self-su\u0000ciency, community self-\nsu\u0000ciency, personal-social responsibility, social adjustment, and personal adjustment.\nAnswer B: The Vanderbilt Assessment Scales are used to help diagnose ADHD in children between the ages of 6 and 12 years old. This scale\nadditionally helps assess other conditions such as conduct disorder, oppositional-de\u0000ant disorder, anxiety, and depression.\nAnswer C: The Scales of Independent Behavior-Revised is a comprehensive, norm-referenced assessment of adaptive and maladaptive\nbehaviors used to determine an individual's level of functioning in key behavior areas."}, {"question": "The Strong Interest Inventory would probably be LEAST useful:", "options": {"A": "as a job selection test to predict future performance", "B": "as a guide for choosing a college major", "C": "as a guide for choosing a career", "D": "as a tool for predicting job turnover"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Research has found interest tests to be good predictors of academic and job choice as well as job persistence and satisfaction. However, they\nare not accurate predictors of job performance.\nAnswer B: As the Strong Interest Inventory is used to indicate potential \u0000t between an individual and various career \u0000elds, the test could be\nused to guide an individual toward a college major one may need to pursue a career in a \u0000eld of best \u0000t.\nAnswer C: The Strong is a widely known as a good predictor of occupational choice, satisfaction, and persistence.\nAnswer D: As the Strong is a good predictor of occupational choice and satisfaction, it can be used as a broad predictor for job turnover,\nconsidering it is less likely that an individual will leave their job if they are satis\u0000ed in their career."}, {"question": "A patient with Alzheimer's dementia would be expected to perform BEST on which of the following WAIS-IV Indexes?", "options": {"A": "Perceptual Reasoning", "B": "Processing Speed", "C": "Working Memory", "D": "Verbal Comprehension"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The WAIS-IV provides a Full Scale IQ (FSIQ), scores on four Indexes, and scores on 10 core and \u0000ve supplemental subtests. The Indexes\ninclude the Verbal Comprehension Index (VCI), the Perceptual Reasoning Index (PRI), the Working Memory Index (WMI), and the Processing\nSpeed Index (PSI). The Verbal Comprehension Index provides information on verbal conceptualization, knowledge, and experience. Knowing\nthat patients with Alzheimer's dementia do better on verbal subtests than on performance subtests would have helped you identify this as the\ncorrect response.\nAnswer A: Perceptual Reasoning is a measure of nonverbal reasoning.\nAnswer B: Processing Speed is a measure of response speed. Patients with Alzheimer's dementia do most poorly on this Index.\nAnswer C: Working Memory provides information on number ability and sequence processing."}, {"question": "Which of the following instruments would be used to assess the general learning abilities of children who are hearing impaired?", "options": {"A": "Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery (HRNB)", "B": "Kaufman Assessment Battery for Children-II (KABC-II)", "C": "Fagan Test of Infant Intelligence (FTII)", "D": "Hiskey-Nebraska Test of Learning Aptitude (H-NTLA)"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The H-NTLA is a non-linguistic intelligence test for deaf children from age 3 to 17 years old. It assesses learning abilities and is used as a\npredictor of academic achievement for the hearing impaired.\nAnswer A: The HRNB is a neuropsychological test battery used to evaluate a wide range of nervous system and brain functions in individuals\naged 15 years and older. This battery has been effective in identifying impairment associated head trauma, tumors, cerebrovascular accidents,\ninfections, degenerative diseases, learning disabilities, and speci\u0000c neurological disorders.\nAnswer B: The KABC-II is an intelligence test for children and adolescents aged 3 through 18. It is similar to the WISC-III and the WPPSI-R,\noffering information regarding a child's cognitive functioning.\nAnswer C: The FTII is a pediatric test designed to assess visual recognition memory, based on the time that an infant spends looking at a novel\nstimulus. It is a clinically useful test for diagnosing infants at high risk for future mental retardation and intellectual de\u0000cit, which allows early\nintervention in high-risk infants."}, {"question": "According to research on fear arousal and attitude change, it has been found that greater attitude change occurs under the following conditions:", "options": {"A": "lower levels of fear arousal are associated with the greatest amount of opinion change", "B": "the greater the amount of fear aroused, the greater the opinion change", "C": "high levels of fear arousal are associated with the greatest amount of opinion change as long as certain conditions are met", "D": "fear arousal is not a good method for inducing opinion change"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Although some early research suggested that high fear arousal results in less attitude change than low fear arousal, subsequent studies have\ndemonstrated that high levels of fear arousal are the most effective for inducing attitude change as long as the fear-arousing message is\naccompanied by information about what actions to take to reduce or avert dangerous consequences.\nAnswers A, B, and D: Higher fear arousal is associated with the most attitude change as long as certain conditions are met--namely, that the\nfear-arousing message is accompanied by information on actions to take to reduce or avert danger."}, {"question": "In a study examining a\u0000liation, experimental subjects (introductory psychology students) are told that they will be receiving a series of electric shocks. Subjects are then given the opportunity to either wait alone, wait with other students who will be participating in the same experiment, or wait with other students who are merely waiting to see their faculty advisors. Results of the study will most likely con\u0000rm the hypothesis that:", "options": {"A": "misery loves company", "B": "misery loves miserable company", "C": "misery loves unmiserable company", "D": "misery loves no company"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Research on a\u0000liation has found that highly anxious subjects tend to prefer to wait for an experiment to begin with other highly anxious\nsubjects. Schachter (1959) found that high anxiety subjects not only prefer to a\u0000liate, but prefer to a\u0000liate with others in similar\ncircumstances. In other words, \"misery loves miserable company.\"\nAnswer A: Highly anxious subjects not only preferred company, but company who were anxious.\nAnswer C: \"Miserable\" subjects like being in the company of others, especially others who are also miserable.\nAnswer D: Highly anxious subjects would rather be with others than wait alone."}, {"question": "A person is exhibiting the \"actor-observer effect\" when he/she tends to:", "options": {"A": "view the behavior of others as due to dispositional factors and his/her own behaviors as due to situational factors", "B": "view the behavior of others as due to situational factors and his/her own behaviors as due to dispositional factors", "C": "overestimates his/her own ability to succeed and underestimates the ability of others to succeed", "D": "underestimates his/her own ability to succeed and overestimates the ability of others to succeed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Research on causal attribution has found that we tend to attribute the behaviors of others to dispositional factors (which is referred to as the\n\"fundamental attribution bias.\") However, when making attributions about our own behaviors, we tend to attribute them to situational factors.\nThe difference when making attributions about our own behaviors and the behaviors of others is referred to as the \"actor-observer effect.\"\nAnswers B, C, and D: According to the actor-observer effect, a person tends to attribute his/her own behavior to situational factors, but others'\nbehavior to personal (dispositional factors)."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT true about the effects of crowding:", "options": {"A": "men are more negatively affected than women by crowded conditions", "B": "increasing age is associated with an increasing negative impact of crowding", "C": "people are more willing to discuss intimate details in crowded (versus uncrowded) conditions", "D": "crowded (versus uncrowded) conditions are associated with a higher risk for health-related problems"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The outcomes of crowding have been linked to a number of individual and environmental characteristics. People are less willing to discuss\nintimate topics and to seek social support in crowded situations. (G. W. Evans et al., Residential density and psychological health: The\nmediation effects of social support, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 57, 994-999, 1989.)\nAnswer A: Men do tend to be more adversely affected by crowding.\nAnswer B: Children are less affected by crowding than adults.\nAnswer D: Living and working in crowded conditions is associated with more health problems."}, {"question": "According to Berscheid and Walster's (1974) \"two-factor theory\" of love, love is the result of:", "options": {"A": "attraction and arousal", "B": "passion and intimacy", "C": "mutual attraction and reciprocal reinforcement", "D": "physiological arousal and a label for it"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Berscheid and Walster conceptualize love as being similar to other emotions. According to their \"two-factor theory,' it is physiological arousal\nand a label/name for it. Support for the two-factor theory is provided, for example, by a study showing that couples who participate in an\nexciting, arousing game together subsequently report stronger positive feelings toward one another than couples who participate in a more\nmundane activity.\nAnswers A, B, and C: Two-factor theory is comprised of physiological arousal and a label for it."}, {"question": "Social comparison theory's predictions about behavior are particularly applicable to:", "options": {"A": "situations involving uncertainty", "B": "inequitable situations", "C": "people who are working on a di\u0000cult task", "D": "people who belong to a cohesive group"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "According to social comparison theory, people evaluate their own attitudes, abilities, and emotions by comparing themselves with similar\nothers. Social comparison theory applies to a variety of situations but seems to be particularly applicable to situations that lack objective\nstandards (i.e., to uncertain situations).\nAnswers B, C, and D: Social comparison theory's predictions about behavior are particularly applicable to situations involving uncertainty."}, {"question": "When males view erotic \u0000lms that depict aggressive acts toward females:", "options": {"A": "they become more accepting of violence toward women", "B": "they become more willing to support women's rights", "C": "they become sexually aroused and less aggressive, even when provoked", "D": "they are less likely to act aggressively than males who view mild non-aggressive erotic \u0000lms"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The research has found that, at least under certain conditions, viewing violence tends to increase violence. A number of studies have found\nthat exposure to aggressive erotic \u0000lms increases aggression toward females as well as acceptance of violence toward women.\nAnswer B: This is the opposite of what has been found.\nAnswer C: Studies have found that exposure to an aggressive erotic \u0000lm can increase aggressiveness and that this effect is enhanced when\nthe individual is provoked following viewing of the \u0000lm.\nAnswer D: Exposure to mild non-aggressive erotica has been found to reduce aggression."}, {"question": "Fritz Heider's (1958) \"balance theory\" proposes that the relationship between three entities can be either balanced or unbalanced. These entities are:", "options": {"A": "a person, another person, and an attitude object", "B": "the intrapersonal self, the interpersonal self, and the agent self", "C": "attitudes, cognitions, and behaviors", "D": "the person's past, present, and future goals"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Balance theory is a cognitive consistency theory that predicts that a person is motivated to change an attitude when he/she experiences\ninconsistencies in attitudes. Balance theory is also known as P-O-X theory, where P is the person, O is another person, and X is the attitude\nobject.\nAnswers B, C, and D: The three components of balance theory are: a person, another person, and an attitude object."}, {"question": "A psychology intern considers her supervisor to be likeable, admirable, and accepting. As a consequence, the intern is open and responsive to her supervisor's comments and recommendations. In this situation, the intern is responding to her supervisor's:", "options": {"A": "referent power", "B": "reward power", "C": "expert power", "D": "legitimate power"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "French and Raven's (1959) research identi\u0000ed six bases of social power. In the situation described in the question, the supervisor has become\na \"signi\u0000cant reference person\" for the intern -- i.e., the supervisor's in\u0000uence stems from the intern's desire to identify with (or \"be like\") this\nperson.\nAnswer B: A person has reward power when he/she controls desirable rewards.\nAnswer C: A person has expert power when he/she has superior knowledge or expertise.\nAnswer D: A person has legitimate power when he/she has authority, status, or social position."}, {"question": "The Parent-Teacher Association (PTA) votes to keep a certain book out of the high school library because of its controversial nature. When students \u0000nd out about the PTA's decision, many of them immediately buy the book at local bookstores. This is an illustration of:", "options": {"A": "deindividuation", "B": "door-in-the-face", "C": "psychological reactance", "D": "cognitive dissonance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The tendency to act in ways opposite of what is required or requested (especially when one feels that one's freedom is being threatened) is\nreferred to as \"psychological reactance.\" In this situation, the students are reacting to a restriction placed on their access to a particular book.\nAnswers A, B, and D: These terms do not describe the scenario in the question."}, {"question": "To assess the relationship between the linear combinations of two or more X variables and two or more Y variables, you would use which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Multivariate analysis of variance", "B": "Analysis of covariance", "C": "Discriminant function analysis", "D": "Canonical correlation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "To identify the answer to this question, you need to determine (1) whether you are interested in the relationship (correlation) between variables\nor the effects of one or more variables on another variable or variables and (2) how many variables there are.\nAnswer A: MANOVA is used to assess the effects of one or more IVs on two or more DVs.\nAnswer B: ANCOVA is used to assess the effects of an IV on a DV when the effects of an extraneous variable have been statistically removed.\nAnswer C: Discriminant function analysis is used to assess the relationship between variables when there are multiple X variables and one Y\nvariable that is discrete (categorical)."}, {"question": "Obtaining research participants for a study on the sexual practices of single young adults from computer dating services, \"singles' clubs,\" and activities for singles sponsored by a variety of organizations would help increase _____.", "options": {"A": "statistical power", "B": "statistical signi\u0000cance", "C": "internal validity", "D": "external validity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "In this situation, the researcher is obtaining participants from a variety of \"natural\" settings. External validity refers to the generalizability of\nresearch results. When participants are selected from natural settings (i.e., from settings where singles are likely to go to meet other singles),\nthis will increase the generalizability of the results.\nAnswer A: To increase statistical power the researcher will employ one or more of these strategies: increase alpha, conduct a one-tailed test,\nincrease the effect size, decrease random error, increase the sample size.\nAnswer B: For an increase in statistical power, a researcher will typically increase the sample size or increase alpha.\nAnswer C: Internal validity can be improved by properly controlling for extraneous variables, demand characteristics, and investigator effects.\nStandardizing instructions and counterbalancing can also help."}, {"question": "Which of the following techniques would be appropriate for using scores on measures of self-esteem, hopelessness, and perceived social support to classify adolescents as either non-depressed, vulnerable to depression, or depressed?", "options": {"A": "Linear regression analysis", "B": "Multiple regression analysis", "C": "Multiple discriminant function analysis", "D": "Principal components analysis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Multiple discriminant function analysis is an extension of discriminant function analysis when multiple predictors will be used to classify\nindividuals into one of three or more criterion groups.\nAnswer A: Linear regression is appropriate when a single predictor is used to predict or estimate a score on a single criterion.\nAnswer B: Multiple regression is appropriate when multiple predictors will be used to predict a score on a single criterion.\nAnswer D: Principle components analysis is similar to factor analysis and is used to identify the set of variables (components) that accounts\nfor the total variance in a set of test scores."}, {"question": "During an experiment, the investigator asks participants about their beliefs regarding the study's purpose and how they were expected to perform. When analyzing the data, she \u0000nds that participants' actual performance is consistent with their beliefs and expectations. This suggests that the study's results may be confounded by _____.", "options": {"A": "carryover effects", "B": "the halo bias", "C": "the Hawthorne effect", "D": "demand characteristics"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "In this situation, the participants may have acted in ways consistent with their expectations rather than simply in response to the experimental\nmanipulation. Demand characteristics are unintentional cues in the experimental environment or manipulation that affect participants' beliefs\nor expectations and may account for the results of the study.\nAnswer A: Carryover effects occur in repeated measures designs when the effects of one treatment have an impact on the effects of\nsubsequent treatments.\nAnswer B: The halo bias is a type of rating error and occurs when a rater's rating of a person on one dimension of performance affects how the\nrater rates that person on unrelated dimensions of performance.\nAnswer C: The Hawthorne effect occurs when research participants act differently because of the novelty of the situation and the special\nattention they receive as research participants."}, {"question": "A researcher is examining the relationship between television viewing habits and academic achievement test scores. The results showed that children who watched more television generally had lower test scores. The Pearson-product moment correlation coe\u0000cient was -.81. What is the proportion of variance of achievement test scores that is accounted for by television viewing?", "options": {"A": "0.9", "B": "0.66", "C": "-0.81", "D": "-0.9"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The value of the Pearson-product moment correlation coe\u0000cient ranges from -1.0 to +1.0, and the corresponding proportion of variance,\nreferred to as the coe\u0000cient of determination, is computed by squaring the correlation coe\u0000cient. The correlation coe\u0000cient for television\nviewing and achievement scores is -.81. The proportion of variance is calculated by squaring -.81, which is approximately .66.\nAnswer A: To calculate the proportion of variance (coe\u0000cient of determination) you have to square the correlation coe\u0000cient. -.81 squared is\n.66.\nAnswer C: The proportion of variance (coe\u0000cient of determination) is the result of squaring the correlation coe\u0000cient. When you square -.81,\nthe resulting coe\u0000cient of determination is .66.\nAnswer D: The proportion of variance is calculated by squaring -.81, which is approximately .66."}, {"question": "A psychologist working at an alcohol rehabilitation clinic is conducting a study to determine if treatment length is related to relapse. She adds four weeks to the standard 12-week program and randomly assigns one-half of the current clinic clients to the extended 16-week treatment and the other half to the standard 12-week treatment. Relapse is measured as the number of times an individual drinks during the 3 months following the end of their respective treatment program. The appropriate correlation coe\u0000cient for the data collected in this study is _____.", "options": {"A": "Pearson r", "B": "tetrachoric", "C": "point biserial", "D": "Spearman rho"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "In this situation, there is one continuous variable (relapse rate) and one dichotomous variable (treatment length -- 12 weeks or 16 weeks). The\npoint biserial coe\u0000cient is used when one variable is continuous and the other is a true dichotomy. In this case, treatment length is a true\ndichotomy because participants receive either a 12-week or a 16-week program.\nAnswer A: This would be the appropriate correlation coe\u0000cient if treatment length was also a continuous variable (e.g., the possible values\nwere 1 week, 2 weeks, 3 weeks, and so on).\nAnswer B: The tetrachoric coe\u0000cient is used when both variables are continuous but have been arti\u0000cially dichotomized.\nAnswer D: The Spearman rho (rank-ordered) correlation coe\u0000cient is used when data on both variables are ranks."}, {"question": "You conduct a research study to assess the effects of T.V. violence on aggressive behavior. You plan to observe sixteen children during recess for three days and calculate their average number of aggressive acts. You will then have all children observe aggressive T.V. programs for three hours and again observe the children during recess for three days and calculate their average number of aggressive acts. Which of the following statistical tests will you use to analyze the data collected?", "options": {"A": "T-test for a single sample", "B": "T-test for correlated samples", "C": "One-way ANOVA", "D": "Chi-square for a single sample"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "This study has one dependent variable measured on a ratio scale (number of aggressive acts) and one independent variable that has two levels\n(before \u0000lm and after \u0000lm). The analysis will involve comparing two means obtained from the same group of participants. Therefore, the means\nare related and the t-test for related (correlated) samples is the appropriate test.\nAnswer A: The t-test for a single sample is used to compare an obtained sample mean to a known population mean.\nAnswer C: The one-way ANOVA is used to compare two or more independent means.\nAnswer D: The chi-square is used to compare nominal (frequency) data (i.e., the number of observations in each category)."}, {"question": "Dr. Muzak wants to determine if background music (music versus no music) affects performance on three different cognitive tasks. Subjects will receive all combinations of all levels of music and task, but Dr. Muzak will administer them in a different order to different subgroups of subjects. This is an example of which research design?", "options": {"A": "Split-plot", "B": "Randomized block", "C": "Solomon", "D": "Counterbalanced"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This question describes a type of within-subjects design because each subject receives all combinations and levels of the IV. The\ncounterbalanced design is a type of within-subjects design in which the order of the different levels of the independent variable(s) are applied\nto different subjects or subgroups of subjects in a different order.\nAnswer A: When using a split-plot (mixed) design, the study has at least one between-groups variable and one within-subjects variable.\nAnswer B: In a randomized block design, at least one of the IVs is a blocking (extraneous) variable, and subjects within each block are\nrandomly assigned to levels of another IV.\nAnswer C: The Solomon four-group design is used to measure the effects of pretesting on a study's internal and external validity."}, {"question": "To evaluate an intervention designed to increase the time a hyperactive child stays on-task when doing schoolwork, the best technique would be _____.", "options": {"A": "situational sampling", "B": "event recording", "C": "interval recording", "D": "sequential analysis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "In this situation, the behavior may have no clear beginning or end, and the purpose of the evaluation is to determine how long the child exhibits\nthe behavior. Interval recording involves dividing a period of time into equal intervals and recording whether or not the target behavior occurred\nduring each interval. This technique could be easily used to assess time-on-task.\nAnswer A: Situational sampling is used when an experimenter is interested in observing a behavior in different contexts.\nAnswer B: Event recording is used primarily to investigate rare events (behaviors) and usually involves counting the number of times the\nbehavior occurs during an event, which wouldn't be appropriate in this case.\nAnswer D: Sequential analysis is used to study complex behavioral sequences (e.g., social interactions)."}, {"question": "Which of the following is used to determine the amount of variability in one variable that is predictable from another variable?", "options": {"A": "Coe\u0000cient of determination", "B": "Cronbach's alpha", "C": "Coe\u0000cient of concordance", "D": "Eta"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "To calculate the coe\u0000cient of determination, square the correlation coe\u0000cient. This provides a measure of shared variability, or the amount of\nvariability in one variable that can be explained by variability in another variable (i.e., the amount of variability in one variable that is predictable\nfrom another variable).\nAnswer B: Cronbach's alpha provides an estimate of a measure's internal consistency reliability.\nAnswer C: The coe\u0000cient of concordance provides an estimate of inter-rater reliability for ranked data and three or more raters.\nAnswer D: Eta is a correlation coe\u0000cient that is used to estimate the variability of two continuous variables that have a nonlinear relationship."}, {"question": "Latent class analysis (LCA) is most similar to which of the following techniques?", "options": {"A": "Multitrait-multimethod matrix", "B": "Cluster sampling", "C": "Latent trait analysis", "D": "ANCOVA"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "LCA is used to identify the underlying latent structure of a set of observed data. Latent trait analysis (LTA) is also used to identify the underlying\nlatent structure of a set of observed data. A primary difference between the two techniques is that, in LCA, the latent variable that determines\nthe structure is nominal; in LTA, the latent variable is continuous.\nAnswer A: The multitrait-multimethod matrix is used to assess construct (convergent and divergent) validity.\nAnswer B: LCA is similar to cluster analysis, not cluster sampling, which is a sampling technique that entails selecting clusters (groups) of\nparticipants rather than individual participants.\nAnswer D: The ANCOVA (analysis of covariance) is a type of analysis of variance that is used to statistically remove the effects of an\nextraneous variable (the covariate) on the dependent variable so it is easier to detect the effects of the independent variable on the dependent\nvariable."}, {"question": "You conduct a research study to compare the effects of four weight-loss programs. To compare the average weight lost by participants in the four groups, you could conduct either a series of t-tests or a one-way ANOVA. You decide to use the one-way ANOVA because its use _____.", "options": {"A": "will decrease the likelihood that you will make a Type I error", "B": "will decrease the likelihood that you will make a Type II error", "C": "will allow you to control the effects of an extraneous variable", "D": "will allow you to assess both main and interaction effects"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "If you conduct separate t-tests in this study, you will have to make many comparisons: Treatment 1 vs. Treatment 2, Treatment 1 vs. Treatment\n3, Treatment 1 versus Treatment 4, Treatment 2 vs. Treatment 3, etc. As the number of comparisons in a study increases, so does the\nprobability of making a Type I error. An advantage of the one-way ANOVA is that it helps control the \"experiment-wise error rate\" (i.e., the\nprobability of making a Type I error). If alpha is set at .05 for this study, for instance, the probability of making a Type I error would remain at\n5%. In contrast, if the individual t-tests were conducted, each at the .05 level, the probability of making a Type I error would be much higher.\nAnswer B: The opposite is actually true because of the inverse relationship between Type I and Type II errors.\nAnswer C: The analysis of covariance is useful for this purpose.\nAnswer D: A study must include at least two independent variables to assess interaction effects. This study seems to have only one\nindependent variable."}, {"question": "When discussing a new screening test that you've developed with several psychologists, one of the psychologists says, \"Let's say that I know a particular patient does not have the disorder that is assessed by the screening test. What is the chance that the test results will show that my patient does not have the disorder?\" This psychologist is interested in your test's:", "options": {"A": "sensitivity", "B": "speci\u0000city", "C": "positive predictive value", "D": "negative predictive value"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "A test's speci\u0000city refers to the proportion of individuals without the disorder who are correctly identi\u0000ed by the test as not having the disorder.\nThe terms listed in the answers to this question are used to describe the accuracy or usefulness of diagnostic tests.\nAnswer A: A test's sensitivity refers to the proportion of individuals with the disorder who are correctly identi\u0000ed by the test as having the\ndisorder.\nAnswer C: A test's positive predictive value refers to the proportion of individuals who test positive for the disorder and actually have the\ndisorder.\nAnswer D: A test's negative predictive value refers to the proportion of individuals who test negative for the disorder and actually do not have\nthe disorder."}, {"question": "A psychologist constructs a new test of cynicism. She correlates the scores on her new test with scores on another test of cynicism and obtains a correlation of .22. Then, she correlates scores on her test with scores on a test of repressed anger and obtains a correlation of .60. The psychologist has obtained evidence that her test has:", "options": {"A": "good construct validity", "B": "inadequate construct validity", "C": "good criterion-related validity", "D": "inadequate criterion-related validity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The psychologist's test has inadequate construct validity. The psychologist has obtained a low correlation (.22) between her test and another\ntest that measures the same theoretical construct (cynicism). In contrast, she obtained a relatively high correlation (.60) between her test and a\ntest that measures a different theoretical construct (repressed anger). This test has low convergent validity since the correlation between the\ntest and another test of the same construct is low. It also has low divergent validity, as evidenced by the high correlation between the test and a\ntest of a different trait. Convergent and divergent validity provide evidence of construct validity, or the usefulness of a test for measuring a\ntheoretical trait or construct.\nAnswer A: A test with good construct validity would have a high correlation with another test of the same trait (high convergent validity) and a\nlow correlation with another test of a different trait (high divergent validity).\nAnswer C: A test has high criterion-related validity when scores on the test correlate highly with scores on a criterion measure.\nAnswer D: A test has low criterion-related validity when scores on the test do not correlate highly with scores on a criterion."}, {"question": "An ipsative score provides information about:", "options": {"A": "an examinee's score on a test relative to scores obtained by examinees in the same age group", "B": "an examinee's score on a test relative to scores obtained by examinees in the same academic grade", "C": "an examinee's score on one scale of a test relative to his or her scores on the other scales", "D": "an examinee's score on a test relative to a prede\u0000ned standard of performance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "An ipsative score is different from most other types of scores which either provide information on the examinee's standing in a norm group or\nstanding in terms of an absolute standard of performance. An ipsative score indicates the relative strength of an examinee's status or\nperformance on the various scales of the test.\nAnswer A: This describes a type of norm-referenced score.\nAnswer B: This describes a type of norm-referenced score.\nAnswer D: This describes a criterion-referenced score."}, {"question": "When conducting a factor analysis, a researcher would choose an orthogonal (rather than oblique) rotation when:", "options": {"A": "the variables included in the analysis are correlated", "B": "the variables included in the analysis are uncorrelated", "C": "the effects of one or more variables have been removed from X only", "D": "the effects of one or more variables have been removed from X and Y"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "When conducting a factor analysis, the rotation of factors can be either orthogonal or oblique. An orthogonal rotation is used when the\nvariables included in the analysis are believed to be uncorrelated.\nAnswer A: An oblique rotation is used when the variables included in the analysis are believed to be correlated.\nAnswer C: This answer describes semi-partial correlation.\nAnswer D: This answer describes partial correlation."}, {"question": "The optimal item di\u0000culty (p) for a true-false test is:", "options": {"A": ".25", "B": ".50", "C": ".75", "D": ".95"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The optimal item di\u0000culty depends on several factors, including the likelihood that an examinee can choose the correct answer by guessing.\nThe optimal item di\u0000culty is halfway between 100% of examinees answering the item correctly and the likelihood of answering the item\ncorrectly by chance alone. For true-false items, the probability of choosing the correct answer by chance is .50, so the optimal di\u0000culty level is\n.75.\nAnswers A, B, and D: The optimal item di\u0000culty (p) for a true-false test is .75."}, {"question": "A test has a test-retest reliability coe\u0000cient of .70. This means that:", "options": {"A": "70% of variability in test scores is true score variability", "B": "49% of variability in test scores is true score variability", "C": "70% of variability in test scores is attributable to measurement error", "D": "49% of variability in test scores is attributable to measurement error"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "A reliability coe\u0000cient is always interpreted directly. A reliability coe\u0000cient of .70 indicates that 70% of the variability in test scores is due to\ntrue score variability, while the remaining 30% is due to measurement error.\nAnswers B, C, and D: A test has a test-retest reliability coe\u0000cient of .70, which means that 70% of the variability in test scores is true score\nvariability."}, {"question": "When conducting a principal components analysis, the components are extracted so that:", "options": {"A": "each component accounts for the same amount of variability", "B": "the \u0000rst component accounts for the largest amount of variability", "C": "the last component accounts for the largest amount of variability", "D": "the middle component accounts for the largest amount of variability"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Principal components analysis is similar to factor analysis and is used to identify a set of variables that accounts for all (or nearly all) of the\ntotal variance in a set of test scores. Principal components are extracted so that the \u0000rst component accounts for the largest amount of\nvariability in test scores; the second component accounts for the second-largest amount of variability, etc.\nAnswers A, C, and D: When conducting a principal components analysis, the components are extracted so that the \u0000rst component accounts\nfor the largest amount of variability."}, {"question": "Bobby obtains a test score of 110. The test has a mean of 120 and a standard deviation of 10 and test scores have a range of 100 and are normally distributed. If Bobby's teacher converts all of the students' test scores to T-scores or z-scores, Bobby's score will be which of the following?", "options": {"A": "T = 60; z = +1.0", "B": "T = 90; z = -1.0", "C": "T = 40; z = -2.0", "D": "T = 40; z = -1.0"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "T-scores and z-scores are standard scores, which means they report an examinee's score in terms of standard deviations from the mean.\nKnowing that the T-score distribution has a mean of 50 and standard deviation of 10 and that the z-score distribution has a mean of 0 and\nstandard deviation of 1 would have helped you identify the correct answer to this question. In the T-score distribution, a score of 40 is one\nstandard deviation below the mean and, in the z-score distribution, a z-score of -1.0 is one standard deviation below the mean. Since Bobby's\nraw score is one standard deviation below the mean, his T-score would be 40 and his z-score would be -1.0.\nAnswers A, B, and C: Based on the data above, if Bobby's teacher converts all of the students' test scores to T-scores or z-scores, Bobby's score\nwill T = 40; z = -1.0."}, {"question": "The internal consistency of test items can be assessed with which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Kappa statistic", "B": "Split-half reliability", "C": "Alternate forms reliability", "D": "Coe\u0000cient of stability"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Internal consistency (homogeneity of test items) is used as an index of reliability. Split-half reliability is a type of internal consistency reliability\nand provides information on the consistency of items in two halves of the test.\nAnswer A: The kappa statistic is used to measure inter-rater reliability.\nAnswer C: Alternative forms reliability provides information on the equivalence of items contained in two different forms of the test.\nAnswer D: The coe\u0000cient of stability (test-retest reliability coe\u0000cient) provides information of the consistency of a test over time."}, {"question": "In the context of item response theory, item di\u0000culty is de\u0000ned:", "options": {"A": "by the number of examinees in the tryout sample who answered the item correctly", "B": "by the ability of the item to discriminate between examinees who fail or succeed on an external criterion", "C": "by the probability that an examinee with a given level of the ability measured by the test will answer the item correctly", "D": "by the probability that an examinee will pass the item given his/her predicted score on the test"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Item di\u0000culty is one of the characteristics indicated by the item characteristic curve (ICC). An item is di\u0000cult when only examinees with very\nhigh levels of the attribute measured by the test can be expected to answer the item correctly at a high probability.\nAnswer A: This is how item di\u0000culty in the context of classical test theory is determined.\nAnswer B: This is not how item di\u0000culty is determined.\nAnswer D: This is not how item di\u0000culty is determined."}]};
